        <p>_ _AL ELL__ _losS, NOVEMBER , IsT., 1912 TO THE p.,tqkotts, OF WHITECROSS. 'WARD. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN, have been invited and have consented to become a Candidate for the above Ward at the forthcoming Municipal Election. As a substantial ratepayer I view with grave concern the alarming increase in rater, notwithstanding a corresponding increased'assessment on nriiperty, and I 'should do my utmost to prevent any unwise or . unnecessary public expenditure, particularly with a view to restricting further advances, and thus encourage -Manufactures to extend their works within the borough. Although always an advoiate for the beet possible education for she masses, I ata strongly in favour of the children being permitted in necessitous cases, to leave school before attaining the age of 14 years. School accommodation in the Ward is strictly limited, and I would therefore urge a speedy com- mencement of the contemplated New School in Lovely I would make it my inunediate businees to urge for- ward some scheme of connecting Sankey and Long- ford routes via Lovely Lane (either by Motor bus or Tramway) all the great development of this portion of the Ward demands some such service, and I am firmly convineed it would be patronised to. such an extent as would obviate it ever becoming a burden on the rates. In connection with any Such scheme the question of half-penny fares for intermediatei stages would—as on previous occasions when in the Council—receive my utmost support. the question of the Public Health is always one of i vital importance and any suggestion for the mprove, ment thereof, together with the opening out of Recreation Grounds both for children and adulta would! receive my ardent support. As a prominent Friendly Society Worker the pro visions of the Insurance Act as it affects the Town Council would naturally claim my particular attention. Provided I am honoured with your confidence at, the Poll, it will always be my endeavonr to represent the Interests of the whole Ward irrespective of Party. I am, Ladies and Gentlemen, Your obedient servant, 64 West at., Warrington. PETER PEACOCK. TO THE ELECTORS OF TOWN HALL WARD WITHDRAWAL OF LIBERAL CANDIDATE. LA DIES AND GENTLEMEN, The Liberal Association having invited me to offer my services to you, under the impression that only one Other candidate was to be in the field, now find that '4tnother so-called Liberal has come out—making a three- cornered contest. Under these circumstances, the , Liberal Association, believing that the Conservative candidate will assuredly have a "walk over " think it la not advisable for them arid myself to waste our strength on a futile contest, and they have advised me to withdraw. The Liberal Association tell me that they will send you a circular explaining their position. I have accepted their advice and withdrawn. Sincerely thanking all those persons who promised me their votes, and everyone who has engaged in assist- ing me. I remain, Yours sincerely 72, Bewsey Street, WII:LIADI ISAAC. Warrington.</p>
        <p>oonstanttostett</p>
        <p>TO THE ELECTORS OF ST. AUSTIN'S WARD. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN, I have accepted an invitation to become a candidate for the above Ward at the forthcoming Elections. If I am honoured by being elected, I would work for Efficiency and Economy, and would always endeavour to promote your best interests quite irrespective of party politics. I am strongly In favour of better houses and better lighted streets and passages. f would take a keen interest in supporting the Housing, Town Planning, Bcc. Act, and in helping for- ward the many advantages it offers for better housing conditions and a healthier town. I would devote special attention to the Public Health Act because I believe that the well-being of the com- munity depends so much upon its wise administration. The most hopeful solution of the Smoke Abatement Problem lies largely in the increased use of gas fuel. The present system of making big profits out of gas consumers does not commend itself to me, and I feel sure that many more would be prepared to take advantage of gas for beating and cooking purposes if the price were reduced. lam in favour of cheaper gas I would always advocate the Fair Wage Clause in al Gornoration contracts, and the giving of a living wage to Corporation workmen. Healthy Recreation both for ol4ildren an 4 &amp;Wits, would have my hearty support: , I should be glad to give my attehtle4clyour auglies- lions and to assist in remedying any just complaints- Your ohedient servant, EDMUND OSBORNE.- 23, Bold at., Warrington.</p>
        <p>MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS, NOVEMBER IsT 1912. TO THE ELECTORS OF LATCHFORD WARD.</p>
        <p>I.LA DIES AND GENTLEMIV, At the request of a huge number of ratepayers anik residents of the Latchford Weird. I have 'Consented jo become a Candidate at the forthcoming ,hlupiAtil, Election. ' • I have resided for many years le the Ward and am constant:4ofm4z with the Electots. I can Wiry claini, that I know the needs 'of the Ward and 'of ' the rate- payers generally. I am anxious to see the Ward the healthiest in the town.. If elected,-I shall give' my warto-hearted atiPpert to all measures tending to protect child-lifa and•to prevent disease. • I hold it to be the duty of the Municipality to See that the children ate given the fullest opportunity fof physical and mental development. I cousider it most desirable that all shim dwellings should be swept away and healthy housing accommodation provided ; streets kept clean and well lighted. Hashes recreation elan should be provided for children and adults, and to WI, end I consider that much more might be done, with, Victoria Park. • firmly believe that cheap labour is dear laboura Am in favour not only of "a ink day's wage for }fain dafa,work,7.)but a' living wage.' There Would then ha' no doubt abbot the fair day's work. ' I would press the question of halfpenny fares for, intermediate stages on our Tramways. , The alarming increase in rates must be a matter of grave concetn to the ratepayers. 'I would resist all unnecessary expenditure and any extravagance in deal- ing with the ratepayers' money. ' I believe that every encouragement should be given to the extension of our Industries, and to accomplish this we must have some Readjustment of our present Isystem of Local Rating. If elected I should count Wan' honour to represent the Ward, and would strive to further the interests of all, irrespective of Creed or Party, I am, Ladies sad Gentlemen, YOUTS E. WYNNE JONES. 540, Koutsford-road, Latchford, October 17th, 1912. TO THE ' BURGESSE4...OF, ORFORD WARD, LADIES AND GENTLEMEN, , At the request of a large number of ratepayers and residents, I have again consented to be nominated fot. the above Ward. During the last three years I have given my best attention to the furtherance of your Interests in the Council, except when illness has prevented me. I am a firm believer in the child of to-day being better cared for by local authorities than has been in the past, and I believe also that while the patents should perform their share in the upbringing of the children, local authorities should assist by providing open spaces, healthy housing accommodation, clean and well lighted, streets, and wherever possible, open-air swimming baths. The health of the ward shall have my constant atten- tion and I shall keep a strict eye upon all unnecessary expenditure of the ratepayers money. Yours faithfully. 32,LongIontat4 ELIJAH CUNNINGTO.N. warringtow. -</p>
