        <p>THREE LUCKY ENGLISHMEN. THE Statement that British Subjects never win any thing with Foreign Bonds has received another startling contradiction, for at the drawing of Hungarian yonder 1906 Bonds held at Buda Pest on August 26th,the FIRST PRIZE OF £18,500 was won by a gentleman residing at Taunton; the Second Prize was secured by a Retired Sea Captain in Cornwall ; and the third and fourth prizes both went to a gentleman residing at Busby Heath. All these Bonds were sold by CUNLIFFE, RUSSELL &amp; CO., of 10 &amp; 12, PLACE DE LA BOURSE, PARIS. The first-mentioned Bond had only been fully paid for in May last, and on the second Bond several instal- ments still remained to be paid, whereby the assertion that their Customers frequently win Prizes during the time they are making their remittances receives new, confirmation. Hungarian Fonder 1906 Bonds take part in sir drawings each year at which drawings enormous Bum of money are distributed—indeed in Deoember 1910 another Client of Messrs. CIINLIFFE, _RUSSELL &amp; CO. (also a British Subject) drew Forty-one thousand Two Hundred and Fifty Pounds sterling, which amount will again be drawn for next December.</p>
