      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>WARRINGTON BAND OF HOPE UNION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BAND OF HOPE UNION.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Council was held in Atkinson's Cafe, Bridge-street, Mrs James Dodds pre- siding over a good attendance of representatives from the various societies. The lion Secretary (Mr F Gandy) presented the report of the Executive Com- mittee which showed that a large amount of useful and educational work had been done during the past twelve months. Mr R Pierce Williams, bon tieasurer, in submitting the financial statement, referred to an increased income due to the success of the May Festival. The following officers were elected : President. Mrs James Dodds; vice-presidents Revs J Yonge, W Cooper, J S Chedburn, E Morgan, H Middlewood, H Ogden, G Curtis, W T Jaye, W Williamson, W A Beckett, Dr Mellor, Parry Jones, Dendey Cock, Mrs Simpson, Miss B Dutton, Mrs E V Walker, Mrs W.l Walker, Mrs Thomas Owen, Messrs A Mourfield, W Clatworthy; J .1 Firth,Ben Few, Henry Thornton, J Griffiths, hon treasurer, Mr R Pierce Williams ; bon secrettuy, Mr F Gandy ; hon. assistant secretaries. Mr J Pardo° and Mr Ralph Fellows ; press secretary, Mr Ci Whitfield ; auditors, Mr J Thornily and Mr J Whitmore ; stock-keepers, Mrs Shaw and Mrs Nurse ; registrar, Mr W Watkins; representatives on the Lancashire and Cheshire Band of:Hope Union, Mr W Watkins, Mr C Powney and Mrs E Andrews ; organist Mr A W Clatworthy ; conductor, Mr A Spruce. In thanking the meeting for her re-election Mrs Dodds said she considered it a very great honour to be the president of the Union and rejoiced in the knowledge that the movement had spread to such a large extent, the result being that the men and women of the future will be a sober people. Mr Thomas D Leslie was elected as a member of the executive committee. The question of acquiring premises for the Union head- quarters was referred to the executive committee to -consider. Mr J Thornily gave a report of the Lanca- shire and Cheshire Band of Hope Union Conference</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>WARRINGTON CHAMBER OF COMMERCE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CHAMBER OF COMMERCE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Council held at Market Gate Chambers on Thursday last, the l'resident (Mr Frost) in the chair, the Secretary said that he was happy to be able to inform the Council that, in accordance with the suggestion of the Cbamber, the London and North-Western Railway Company were at last improv- ing the platforms at Bank Quay station, and that the Warrington Fire Engine Committee had decided to purchase a motor engine. Mr FROST reported that he and Mr R T Fair- dough had attended the autumnal meeting of the Associated Chambers at Newcastle, and gave a brief account of the proceedings, which were highly interesting and important. On behalf of the Warrington Chamber he had moved the following resolution, which was carried by a large majority : "That this meeting is of opinion that, in order to per- manently secure the safety of the Empire audits world- wide trade, it is highly desirable to obtain the co-opera- tion of all:the Colonies in the creation of an Imperial Navy,and that all British citizens of suitable age should compulsorily undergo such a course of training in dicipline and arms as would fit them to take their part in a properly organized scheme of Imperial Defence." It was decided to endeavour to arrange a meeting at the Town Hall to consider the question of co-partnership as a possible solution of some of our present industrial difficulties and to Invite Sir William H. Lever or some other prominent authority to be present and to give an address upon the subject.</p>
      </div>
