      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>COMMON-SENSE RULE</head>
        <p>A COMMON-SENSE RULE</p>
        <p>WARINGTON TOWN</p>
        <p>COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>THE MAYOR ON PARTY POLITICS IN MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS. CHILDREN AND THE PICTURE PALACES.</p>
        <p>The final meeting of the Warrington To vu Clime i for the municipal year was held at the Town Hall, on Tuesday evening, the Mayor (Councillor Dr Joseph) presiding. There were also present :—The Deputy Mayor (Alaerman Forshaw) ; Aldermen A Bennett, John Evans, .1 Smethurst and D Tinnion ; Councillors Archer, W H Bellian,J Birtles,J Broadhurst, T Chantler, E Cunnington, J,Hawthom, It Henshall, C Jackson Holmes, W H J Jenkins, F T Lash ford, F Lindley, Dr McKee, .1 Pemberton. H Roberts, J Ssnkey, 'l' b Steel, 0 Took, .1 Wilkinson and W .1 Wilson ; Messrs J L Whittle (toen clerk), A T Hallway (deputy town clerk), T H Spinks (assistant town clerk), J Fairhurst (borough treasurer), A M Ker (borough surveyor), W S Haddock (gas eng.neer), ' F V L Mathias (electrical engineer), .1 M Murray (director of education), Dr G W N Joseph (assistant medical officer), and J Gray (water engineer). THE LATE MR. F. MONKS. The MAYOR said he had received a letter from Mr. F W Monks, thanking the Mayor and the members of the Council for the kind vntec,f condolence passed with the family in respect to their recent bereavement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>LATE MR. T. LONGDIN.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR. T. LONGDIN.</p>
        <p>The MAvott read the following letter Clenthurst, Versailles-road. Anerley,S.E , Cctober 18th, 1912. Dear Mr Whittle,—My mother has received your letter, enclosing a copy of a resolution of the Warrington Borough Council conveying its sympathy to her, myself and my brothers and sisters in our great bereavement, and also a copy of a similar resolution passed by each of the various committees with which my late dear father was associated in his work as borough surveyor. It is some consolation to my mother to know how highly my dear father was esteemed by his fellow- townsmen with whom his official duties brought him in daily contact, and more particularly to learn that the Council's minutes contain a record of the appreciation of its members of toy father's ability, integrity and faithful discharge of his duties for so many years. My mother has asked rue to write and request you to convey to the Council and to the members of the various com- mittees at their next meeting her grateful thanks, and those of the rest of the family, for the sympathy they have extended to us in our great and sudden bereave- ment. We have also very sincerely to thank you person- ally and the rest of the officials of the Council for the expression of your own and their sympathy conveyed to us in your letter.-1 remain, dear Mr Whittle, yours very sincerely HERBERT W.M. LONGDIN." THE LATE MR. D. S. DUNCAN.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR said it was his sad duty to announce the death of an ex-member of the Council, Mr D S Duncan, and to move a resolution of condolence with the family. His Worship outlined Mr Duncan's municipal wort( and said that at the time of his retirement he was the sealer Councillor.</p>
        <p>Alderman TINNION, in seconding, said the latelfr Duncan gave his heart and soul to the work of the Cor- poration. He bad had considerable experience as an engineer and his excert knowledge, which was ungrudg. ingly given, was of great value to the committees on which he served.</p>
        <p>Tbe resolution was carried in silence, all standing.</p>
        <p>THE LATE MR. T. J. HULSE.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR moved a similar resolution in the case of the late Mr T.l Hulse, also an ea-member of the Council, who, he said, was a very worthy man. Alderman 13EsNErr seconded. He said the late Mr Hulse was a very old friend of his, and on many grounds he deeply regretted his death. The resolution was carried. ALTERATIONS AT THE POLICE STATION.</p>
        <p>The Borough Surveyor was authorized by the Watch Committee to prepare plans and estimates for altera- tions required at the police premises. Councillor BIRTLEh inquired what was the nature of the alterations and repairs proposed to be carried out ? The MAYOR replied that dry rot had appeared in some of the walls and the accommodation of n room in the inspector's house was inadequate. The Surveyor was requested to prepare a report as to whether the room could be reasonably enlarged and at what cost. Councillor PR:\ (BERTON said that if the resolution meant tbat no work would be done unless approved by the Council, he was satisfied. Be did not believe in shutting their eyes opening their mouths and tak:ng whatever people liked to put in them. The MAYOR gave an assurance that no work wonld be undertaken without the sanction or knowledge of the Council.</p>
        <p>The minute was confirmed. CHILDREN AT PICTURE PALACES. SHOULD THEY ATTEND NIGHT PERFORMANCES?</p>
        <p>The question of the attendance of young children at places of amusement at night time has been considered by the Watch Committee. The matter was introduced to their notice by the Health Committee, who forwarded the following resolution : "That the attention of the Watch Committee be drawn to the number of children under eight years of age who visit the different places of entertainment in the borough after nine p.m. and that the Watch Committee be requested to take Buell steps as may be deemed advis- able to have the practice discontinued." Full consideration of the subject was deferred. Councillor ARCHER said he should like to know what the intentions of the Watch Committee were regarding this important question. While he hoped the members would disabuse their minds of the tirade in the public Press against places of amusement up and down the country, they ought to consider the facts impartially and bring the issue more to their own doors. It was obvious to anyone who traversed the streets late at night that children of tender years were attending places of amusement—especially the picture palaces—under no parental control or supervision. In the interests of the children themselves such conditions ought not to pre- vail. There was one aspect of the question which appealed to him most forcibly. A vast amount of money was spent on education and be said advisedly that the teachers were severely handicapped by the attendance of children at theatres at unduly late hours. If the parents had no control over the children or if they tolerated such a thing it was the duty of the Town Council to pot the matter in order. There ought also to be supervision of the pictures displayed. Certain items in the programmes at picture palaces were not desirable for children to see. There were occasions when the pictures and items appealed to morbid tastes and the young mind was carried away by them. He hoped that the question having been brought to the notice of the authorities some good would accrue, and trusted that the Watch Committee would seriously view the situation and assist the education of the children along the right and proper lines. (Applause.) The MAYOR: This matter has been been deferred for further consideration. Mr A OCHER : Yes, I have seen that Nir Mayor. The MAYOR : No doubt what you have said will be considered along with other matters relating to the subject. Toe minutes of the committee were agreed to. TOWN PLANNING.</p>
        <p>Alderman BFSNECTT moved the paosing of the minutes of the Street Improvement Committee end referred to the recent Local Government Board inquiry at the Town Hell in reference to town planning. He said be thought that all who were present and all who read Ihe report of the proceedings in the newspapers would agree that the inquiry was satisfactory in every way. They hoped soon to have the sanction of the Board to the preparation of a scheme which would enable them to supervise the future development of the town.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the committee were approved.</p>
        <p>PAVING OF STREETS AND PASSAGES. At a meeting of the Paving and Sewerage Committee on the 11th instant, the tender of Mr William Owen, amounting to £2,762 Os 4d for the paving of 26 streets streets and passages, was accepted. The minute was approved. THE FIRE BRIGADE. INCREASE OF SALARIES. The Fire Engineer Committee resolved that the following increase of wages be granted as from Ist July last :—Superintendent F Turner £l5 to £52 per annum; Deputy Superintendent F Turner from £l3 tn £2O per annum ; Enginser 3 Wakefield from 35s to 37s Ed per week. The Council agreed. THE MAYOR THANKED.</p>
        <p>THE CALLS UPON HIS WORSHIP'S TIME AND PUR SE. Alderman TniNioN moved : " That the best thanks of this Council be presented to Dr George William Joseph for the satisfactory manner in which he has dis- charged the responsible duties devolving upon him as Mayor and chief magistrate of the borough during this. his second year of &amp;lice, and for the unvarying courtesy and impartiality displayed by him when presiding over the deliberations of the Council, and in his intercourse with its members." (Applause.) The Alderman said he regretted that owing to a family bereavement their dear old friend Alderman Smethurst did not feel equal to the task of proposing the resolution, fie was sure Alderman Smethurst and the family had the deepest sympathy of the Council. (Hear, hear). With reference , to the Mayors of Warrington, he sometimes wondered how they had found time to attend to the many duties of the office, especially in recent years, and he should thought the time had now arrived when theg not be at the beck and call of every society or body which liked to call upon them. He would admire the man who would dissociate himself from some of the services which the occupant of the civic chair had been expected to render. He also rather protested against the onslaught on the purse of the lqayor.</p>
        <p>Some people seemed to think that the contributions which his Worship made to various objects came out of the rates, but it was well that they should understand that all those donations and subscriptions were from the Mayor's private purse. The point he wished to make was that the necessary work pertaining to the position of Mayor was quite sufficient without so many varied duties being thrown upon him ; but in respect to the present occupant of the chair, in the generosity of his heart and love for the duties of the office, he had worked exceptionally hard, and ha deserved the heartiest thanks of the people of Warrington. (Hear, hear and applause) His conduct of the deliberations of the Council bad given universal satisfaction. He had been most courteous to all, but at times he had been a little touchy. But he would get over that, and would find that it was not desirable to worry about little matters, but that he must forgive, if he could, the many faults of the memhers of the Council, and try to live above them. (Laughter.) With that excep- tion he had not much fault to find with the Doctor, and he hoped that at the end of his next year of office he would have worked off his blerni,hes—(laughter)-- small as they might be, and if be (Alderman Tinnion) proposed or seconded a vote of thanks to him he hoped he would be able to say that he had profited by the advice which had been given him. (Laughter.) Dr. Joseph had been one of the most excellent mayors who had over sat in the chair. (Hear, hear.) The Alderman concluded with a tribute to the Mayoress for the grace- ful manner in whids she had discharged her duties dur- ing the past 12 months.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT seconded the motion, and said he heartily agreed with practically all that Alderman Tinnion had said. Tee little failing to which reference bad been made was a failing on the virtuous side, and showed what a tender heart and acute brain the Mayor bad that he should be so susceptible to those little annoyances flt times. The members of the Council were very trying at .times, end the wonder was that the Mayor had been able to exercise such unvarying courteousy cud impartiality in dealing with a body sometimes so unruly. (Hear, hear, and laughter.) in public life there was only one way to get through work quite happily, and that was to cultivate a thick akin, steer straight ahead, not to worry, but to do one's duty and to leave the rest with the public. (Hear, hear.) That was his advice. No one could have attended to his duties better than the Mayor had done, or have siv,m more general satisfactior, and his personal association with the members had been courteous to a degree. In reapect to outside work he had not spared himself, and every section and creed must be indebted to him. He had, in fact, done more than was expected of him, and he would be able to feel that he had earned the gratitude of the whole of the town. He had heard people try to belittle the office of Mayor, but they spoke without knowledge or thought, as he considered such an office was the greatest honour a citizen could achieve, and</p>
        <p>candidly why he had agreed he could not say why, because there were many members of the Council who had perhaps greater abilities to fill the position. Perhaps force of habit had influenced him to some extent. Having got into the habit of doing the work, he felt that be should like to go on for a little longer, if he were permitted to do so, so as to further one or two schemes which ho had still at heart. He had been advised to take no notice of little disagreements and breezes, but he was afraid he was too old to grow the thick skin which Alderman Bennett had advised him to cultivate. He appreciated very highly the remarks of Councillor Toole, and was glad the offiicals had been recognized. What he had said about the Coulson's high moral standing was perfectly true. Its position in that respect was quite unassailable, and he considered that he could speak with some authority on the subject. (Hear, hear.) The social calls upon a Mayor were very heavy indeed ; in fact, the social and religious calls were enormously heavy, all but political, and out of those he had religiously kept. (Hear, hear.) In addition there were outside calls. He had attended functions in other towns at his own expense, because he felt that as the civic head of an import- ant borough friendly and diplomatic relations should be maintained with other municipalities. Reviewing the chief events of the year, his Worship said the reconstruction of Warrington Bridge had not proceeded as rapidly as they would wish, but of late the progress had been more satisfactory. On the question of the public health, be thought considerable good might be done by periodical visits to the slums by members of the Council. He was proud of the fact that Wazringten was one of the first municipalities to be recognized under the Insurance Act, as having sanatorium accommodation for consumptives. Each year brought adrift ional legislation which not only imposed more work on the Council but left a residue of taxation to be met, thus increasing the local rates.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>TRAFALGAR DAY.</head>
        <p>TRAFALGAR DAY.</p>
        <p>Monday was the 107th anniversary of the battle of Trafalgar, and not only in Great Britain but throughout the Empire was honour paid to the memory of Nelson.' our picture shows the base of the Nelson Column in Trafalgar-square, which, in accordance</p>
        <p>with the custom of many years' standing, was gaily decorated with laurels. Beautiful floral tributes were sent by many institutions. From .the earliest hours there was an un- ceasing procession of people round the monu- ment.</p>
