.:;::::...ii3E.W: .......'.....`"W.i3 ..3i,/,'1M.:::3:3333'..V3&amp;. cy ' •."• :: - ~.,:. , 4,4:14:4, . --,,,,,A.:„.:,...4,04,44,414wfte.:...10.,,..,4,,,,,,,,m,:,,,,,,,,,,„:,.:„„,,.„,...w ~, .4 i,,,,,., .. -..,,..:::,,,.....,..:, •:• ••..,,,,.....z4u,s,zfoitlokitiklix,lma.,..,..,:-.:.,:;,44ept..,...,:e;,:1ittel ......: ...,......i„,,,:.w,,,,,,,,....a...4..,0,:,,,%4„,..,::, ~:„......,p;:. .: .....,::,,,:...,-.4..,:w,,,,,•,:,,,•:::.:..,,:,•:•.:,, •,,,,,,,,, ..,4,..7...,?,.::.....,...,,.1,,;,....;.....40..vgadig,•.:•8q,!ganNpv:•,2,44,:?.40,,.,4.t,i..,:m*,.,g20-..,R:4:L:,•,•:,,,.,,,,,•,,,,,,,,, -- r•:e,oksfti-AvT.7i,.7k:.:.:•..•..i.,..mp,'r:-..ifa•gp::A:Nu•smnu,•,:•,::.•'••,••:' -.. ,I..41:1•1•!: .......,:5..,4,4;•,.,.,44,.,:,:i,,.e.::,,,,,,,:•,g,-„,41,g0vA.,,,,,„,:..„:::&gt;,..- • -,,,,,,..i.,4•J,:•%&lt;*e.:titiT1AT.a:A.:.,,,„,,,k,..„,.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <p>when the duties were so faithfully carried out, as they had been in the case of Dr Joseph, and had given satis- faction to the citizens generally, one's sense of satis- faction quite outweighed all the incidental worries which arose from time to time. With Alderman Tinnion he cordially joined in thanking the Mavoress for the graceful manner in which she had discharged her duties. (Applause.) Councillor TOOLE said that as that was probably the last occasion on which he would address the Council, he wished to endorse all that had been said about the Mayor and at the same time to thank the chairmen of the various committees' the members of the Council generally, and the officials for the kindness and 'assist- ance accorded to him during his public) work. As an old Warringtontonian leaving Warrington, he hoped that the high moral standard which characterized the conduct of the Corporation business would always be maintained. lie was willing to declare on any platform or in any company that whilst he had been a member of the Council be had seen nothing in connection with its affairs that any municipality might not well be proud of.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROI3ERTS, in supporting the resolution, disagreed with Alderman Tinnion in his discrimination between the duties of the Mayor as chief magistrate and chief citizen. In that dual capacity there were many demands made upon his Worship which he did not see how he could very well resist. He was not referring to the pecuniary demands, butte the claims for recogni- tion on the part of public bodies, which the chief citizen of the borough could help forward by his personal recognition and presence. With regard to those matters the Mayor had exceptionally well fulfilled the duties appertaining to his important office. He agreed that any man might very laudably aspire to the position of chief magistrate of Warrington. It was a matter of considerable honour and pride to the occupant, and especially when the on mpant felt that he had conferred upon him an honour which it was his duty to maintain in the highest and best degree. In that respect again Dr. Joseph had put them under an obligation. As a citizen he (Councillor Roberts) said without hesitation that for his indefatigable energy and attention to its various requirements in so many ways the town owed Dr Joseph a very guest meed of praise and thanks. He was particularly glad the Mayoress had been associated with the resolution. Councillor PEMBERTON most heartily gave the motion his support. Alderman Tinnion should speak only for himself when he referred to " unruly members." The present Mayor had allowed an unprecedented latitude in discussion, which had been for the good of the tc..wn. The Mayor had on every possible occasion extended full privilege and every courtesy to anyone who had anything to say in regard to public affairs, and very properly too. He bad appreciated the privilege himself and so had other members of the Council. There had been an inclination on the part of past Mayors to cot down discussion." and never mind the consequences, or how imp,artant it was—it was simply ' out of order.'" The public did not always sufficiently realize how enormous were the calls upon the Mayor's private purse. He thought the gentleman who accepted the office should be to some eXtent protected from such unreasonable demands. When one con- sidered how many charitable bodies there were calling for support, and how many incidentals arose in two years, an enormous amount could be spent during that period of office. Then it had been a matter of astonishment to him how a busy professional man like Dr Joseph had managed to devote so much time to his public duties. The work must have made great inroads upon his leisure time, entailing great self-sacrifice and leaving scarcely a moment for private rest and recrea- tion. The Mayoress had performed her duties to the entire satisfaction of the public, and had done her work with tact and grace. (Hear, bear). Councillor HENSEL ALL also supported the resolution. He said the Mayor had been " to our virtues ever kind and to our faults a little blind " The resolution was put by the DEPUTY MAYOR and carried with acclamation. The MAYOR, in returning thanks. said he should experience extreme difficulty in adequately replying to the many kind things said concerning the Mayoress and himself. He first desired to associate himself with all that had been said regarding the bereavement which Alderman Smethurst and his family had sustained. He heard a still further trouble threatened the alderman in the illness of his sister-in-law. Turning to the vote of thanks, he said that a good deal of what had been said regarding the duties of his office were true, but perhaps the compliments paid to him were a little too eulogistic It was true that be had tried to do his work to the best of his ability. Allusion had been made to the fact that shheowwan that s tal it ss so much in Council tttioeu'c'htionuechy,"bmuut hedicdnnocil a not think perhaps k be bad on committees. Frankly speaking. and without wishing to compliment himself, he had shown his best side in the Council Chamber. The duties of his office had seriously encroached upon his leisure time, leaving but few hours to himself. At the same time he had felt he was doing something for the good of the community, and that thought had encouraged him. Considering the self-sacrifice demanded, it might well be wondered .thy he had con- sented to act for another year. If he were asked</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <p>The elective auditors had now finished their work, and the Government audit system had started. He only hoped the change would be for the good of the town, but he could testify that in the past the work had been well and conscientiously done. He alluded to the retirement from the Council of Councillors Bellian, Jackson Holmes, Sankey and Toole, who, he said, had been valuable and useful members of the Corporation. He trusted that their continued activities in other spheres would benefit the community. THE MAYOR AND PARTY POLITICS IN MUNICIPAL ELECTiONS. Continuing, the MAYOR said; There are no party politics in this Council, and I cannot help regretting that some of our most useful members—in fact we are all useful—are sometimes removed through the instru- mentality of either one party or another. Of course lii my official capacity, lam not a party man. I can see the panorama of party politics passing before me, and I can see the per- fections and the imperfections better than if I happened to be inside, and Ido think it is a pity in an assembly such as ours, where party politics have no influence at all, and cannot have any possible influence upon the work—because there is no such thing as Con- servative paving or Liberal paving—that the same influence should remove good ar.d useful members from our midst. (Hear, hear.) You may say, " Well, if we take one good member away we put in another good member,' but that new member has not got the three, six, or nine years' experience of the man you have removed. lam speaking now as one who has intense interest in the work of the Council, and who desires to see efficiency and usefulness, and for the good of the town I hope and intend some time v.hen I am in a posi- tion of greater freedom to raise this important question It is a very important one. In Imperial Parliament, politics are absolutely necessary,but in a Town Council they are not only unnecessary, but ought not to influ , ence it.</p>
        <p>In conclusion the Mayor thanked the Council for its kind expressions. He said the work had been a real pleasure to him,although it had been bard coupled with his private work. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>THE LATE MR. COLERIDGE TAYLOR'S ESTATE Mr Samuel Coleridge Taylor, of Aldwick, Croydon, composer of the "Hiawatha" triology and other works, including the incidental music for" Herod,"" Ulysses," "Nero," and "Faust," Professor of Composition at Trinity College, and conductor of the Handel Society, who died on the Ist ult., left estate of the gross value of £874, of which £756 is net personalty. Letters of administration of his property have been granted to his widow.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>"Bella Donna."</p>
        <p>No more interesting play or finished performance has been presented at the Royal Court Theatre for a long time than one finds this week in the " Bella Donna" of Robert lichens and J B Fagan, author and adapter respectively. In the main the play is a' study of the modern woman of Society at her worst, with all her selfishness, pettiness and cruelty laid bare and naked to the eye. Or, perhaps, it would be more accurate to say that in " Bella Donna" is presented merely a phase of the life of the society woman, show- ing her as the prey to temptations which inspire all her evil passions and reveal none of her virtues. Mrs. Chepstow, the Bella Donna of a " fast " circle and a " heroine " of the Divorce Court, is yet a beautiful woman. She captivates Nigel Armine,a decent, straight, but ordinary young Englishman, who is heir-presumP- five to a title and a fortune. With the blindness of love he can see nothing but good in Mrs. Chepstow—she has been misunderstood, slandered, and so on. But Dr. Isaacson, Armine's greatest frieud, sees below the surface and distrusts the outcome of an alliance between so ill-assorted a pair. Quite early in the story the lady affords the doctor a glimpse into typically modern feminine soul, during a consultation at which mutual antipathy seems to be bred.</p>
        <p>Dr. Isaacson : " Yes. It is possible to have pre- served the health of the body and yet to have lost the health of the soul."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Chepstow : " The soul! Really,Dr.lsaacson. How amusing to meet a doctor who believes in the- soul. I thought the soul was one of the things that are no longer mentioned in polite society. I don't happen to believe in the soul and Ido believe verl much in the body. I'm afraid I believe when we die we go out (she snaps her fingers)—just like that. And so I mean to have a good time as long as I aro here."</p>
        <p>Here one sees the callous materialism of Afrs• Chepstow and her class. Possibly she might have been content and had her "good time" if events had turned out as she planned. But the scene shifts t? the Nile. Mrs Chepstow accompanies Armine as his wile to Egypt, and here they learn that the birth of an heir to his elder brother removes the inheritance (coveted solely by Mrs. Armine) entirely' out of reach. The inherent selfishness of the won?e° is manifested in a bitterness of spirit which the doting husband is far from realising. Mrs Armine seeks other means of attaining her desires, and in doing e° comes comp!etely under the spell of Mahmoud Baroudi, an influential and wealthy Egyptian whose compelling force of character, Oriental calm and fatalism, subjucate her entire personality. She wants to get rid of her husband, and Baroudi shows the weF —a slow poisoning by sugar of lead introduced into Armine's coffee in minute doses over a period of weeks. The tragedy develops on board the Egyptian's luxurious dayahbeeah, which convele husband and wife to Luxor on the Nile. Here Isaacsoe re-appears, only to meet the lively hostility of Armine. By a combination of force and strategy ite secures control of the patient and the whole CO blooded plot is revealed—and checkmated. Airs' Armine meets her deserts. Armine learns all and the chapter of married life is summarily closed. Bella Donna, siren, now a slave to her own passion, turns t° Baroudi, and throws herself into his arms. But the Egyptian is in no mind for a scandal and with a callcms brutality that matches her own, he tells Mrs. Armine that she has failed. "I have no use for women wb° fail—and there are other women ! " Bella Donne, passes out into the night after seeing the shutters 0' the villa Androud closed in her face by Isaacson. -</p>
        <p>It is an enthralling play, magnificently plaled. Miss Georgina Wynter gives a compelling interpre- tation of the complex role of Mrs. Armine. She i 9 artless, passionate, seductive, or cruel as the cccasiou demands, and her emotional intensity will bear coin- parison with that of the creator of the role, Mret' Patrick Campbell. As Dr. Isaacson, Mr. E. W. Thorns 9 presented another very powerful piece of characteriss- , ton. His duel with Mrs. Armine, and the conquest of Dr. Hartley in defiance of medical etiquette, gave hilt great opportunities for effective craftsmanship whici were made the most of. Mr. Martin Lewis played Sige Armine very pleasantly, though we thought he ri'BB,9" little lacking in expression when the cruel truth of his wife's treachery was driven home upon him by her own callous confession. One of the most brilliantll watts- s Tye, as Ibrahim, the slender, effeminate, Eg9l3uccessful portrayals was that of Mr. Ernest49ne servant who has much to do with creating the tricii, atmosphere of Egypt in the play. " All what wants she shall have " he says repeatedly, and, t,i.,"0 phrase brings powerfully to mind the fatalism 01 East. Mr. Ivan Murray, as Mahmoud Baroudi, as dominant on the stage as he is in the stn and a better presentation of the emotio°sd. but ruthless Egyptian could not be desire- Quite a notable success is achieved ,13 Act 3 by Mr. Alfred Mcgahon, who takes „tile, part of Hassan, a guide to the temple on the Nile. volubility, persistence and servility are unmistakstV; true to lite and complete a finished study: of tat Eastern dragoman. Dr. Hartley, the physical]. Luxor, and Mrs Marchrnont, cousin of Nigel Allmti-ci are excellently played by Mr. J. Heribert sr3 Miss Agnes Knights. And now a final word as to the scenic effects 'vial which Mr. Alban B Limpus' Company tour. TbeY 0,9,3 only carry on the plot of the story but they convey ,o^o beautiful pictures, the glamour of the Nile and life board a dayahbeeah. The realism of each seenethle impressive, and it is added to not a little by eet droning of the Nubian sailors' chorus and the tor_er weird song of prayer which the Muezzin chants city and village at the devotional hours of the i?Tla The orchestral music, which accompanies the sc,la of the play, is also appropriately Orientai character.and was finely rendered under the conduc t ship of Mr. T. Boothroyd.</p>
      </div>
