      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>NOTES BY " MUST."</head>
        <p>NOTES BY " MUST."</p>
        <p>It was bound to come ! Warringt n's first home 'victory could not be long delayed in view of the form they had displayed against Runcorn, Wigan and Barrow. They were expected to beat Rochdale Rornets on Saturday, and they did so, though only by the narrow margin of 8 points to 5. A. year ago the score was 16 points to 7. Warrington's victory was very welcome, and the result of a fine game, but truth 'to tell the Wirepullers were somewhat lucky. Roch- dale might reasonably have claimed a share of the points on the day's play.</p>
        <p>The Hornets put one of the biggest and heaviest teams in the field seen at Wilderspool for some time, and weight tells in the long run. Warrington went off with rare dash in the early stages of the game and showed a superiority in pace and combination which gave them a useful lead. But in the second half the Rochdale men took command of the scrummages and the ball, and the biggest part of the forty minutes was spent within striking distance of the Warrington line. Rarely have the forwards been worse beaten for possession, and had the defence not been aided by a little luck the visitors must at least have equalised. They played fine aggressive football and were unlucky to get only one try,</p>
        <p>The game itself was full of exciterrent and thrills, and the play of both teams was of more than average excellence. If Warrington had a pull anywhere it was in the pace of the forwards, who frequently regained in the loose what they bad lost in the packs. Pearnley and Skelhorne were always to the fore-, and both men did some fine tackling. In the first half the home backs were getting the ball and made a good show, the opening try being the result of one of the finest ccmbined movements seen on the ground for a Beason or two. Nicholas and Rushton were playing clever football at this stage, and the new half back's debut was full of promise. Rushton, too, seems better at outside half than E c rum half. The defensive powers of the two men are somewhat doubtful, but one needs fuller experience of their play together to judge properly.</p>
        <p>Tranter was again the best of the threequarters, and the way he opens the game out with judiciJus passing is worthy of high praise. He is modelling his play on the best lines for a centre-threequarter, and will yet fill the place with distinction. Somehow or other Renwick does not seem to have got into the form of last season, and though be both won the same and saved it last Saturday he was frequently at fault in taking passes and looking after his wing. The " Skipper " does not really look in the hest of health and the committee might do worse than send him to Blackpool for a week.</p>
        <p>Warrington's weakest spot was on the wings, where both Woodburn and Waywell lacked finishing power. Each did some good work in mid-field, but they rarely looked like penetrating the defence, and that is what wing three-quarters are for. They should be the scoring arm of the team. At present they are not, and Warrington will have to find another good wing before the teams come up to expectations. More Weight forward would be useful too, as Saturday's match made very plain. The two new men are both on the light side. And now a word in season to Jolley. The young full back is not playing as well as he did a month ago, and on Saturday he too often waited for the ball to bounce. Then, too, ha is not running round enough, and getting his three-quarters on the move. He cdn do better than be has done of , late.</p>
        <p>-- The League table up-to-tes da ias follows: For. _Aest._</p>
        <p>Per-</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. C.l. T. P. G. T. P. c'nt'ge. Hunslet 7 7 0 0... 29 39 175...12 7 45_10000 Hud'field 6 6 0 0... 16 30 122... 8 8 40...10000 Dewsbury 6 5 1 0... 13 31 119... 8 9 43... 83'33 Wigan Hull Kit__ 6 4 2 0_ 20 21 103...13 13 65... 66 66 Ilroughton 6 3 1 2... 10 15 65... 7 8 38... 6666 Salford Swinton 6 4 2 0... 7 7 35. 4 9 85. 66•66 Hui I Hatley Leeds 6 3 2 1_ 19 13 77...10 6 58— 5813 Widnes 6 3 2 1... 9 15 63...10 13 59... 58.33 Wakefield T 6 2 2 2... 12 22 90—.13 21 439 50,00 St Helene— 7 3 3 1... 12 17 75...15 17 ea... 5000 Warrington 8 3 4 I. 6 8 36...13 11 59... 4375 Oldham 0 ^ 3. 1... 10 10 50.-16 10 62... 41'66 bradford .3..0rk It H 6 2 4 o. 14 16 76... 7 12 50... 33.33 Halifax 6 1 4 I. 7 12 50—.17 24 106... 2500 igbley " ,narrow 6 1 4 1... 4 5 23...13 14 68... 25•00 Leigh 6 1 5 0... 4 6 26... 6 11 45... 1666 rt.,tincorn Isrsmley 6 0 6 0... 5 6 28...20 29 127... 0060 'Coventry</p>
        <p>To day Warrington renew acquaintance with Widnes. and as it is some years since they won at Lowerhouse-lane, the prospect is none too bright. ,I3tzt Widnes are not performing as well as they were last season when they beat Warrington t/-5 at Widnes tind drew at Wilderspool. On current form Warring- are the stronger aide, and I am hopeful that they taaY rise to the occasion to-day as they did at Barrow 4nd Runcorn. They have a good side doing duty, 4kri.d the homesters will be somewhat handicapped by l)juries.</p>
        <p>•For the first time this season, Warrington will be 'vithout Renwick, Icho has "cried off" for a rest. bickenson will fill the vacancy, and with Brooks back 4Raht on the wing the three-quarter line should be quite as effective. Mestere returns to the forwards et, and the full side will be: Pr • u,Jolley, full back ; Brooks, Dickenson, Tranter and L'voodburn, three-quarters; Nicholhs and Rushton, "If backs; G Thomas, R Thomas, Cox, Fearnley, 'esters and Skelhorne, forwards.</p>
        <p>• The " A" team at borne against Widnes " A " will : Jordan, full-back ; Clare, Mclntyre, Carter and avwell, three-quarters ; Daiutith and Douglas, half- qei_lc, a ; Bevan, Critchley, Cooper, Naylor, Clark and '5, forwards. Hudson was selected, but theyoung tre had not quite recovered from the injury sus- '• 14ed at Wigan.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE CUP. I Warrington had a draw in the first round of the eashire Cup. but they are faced with a stiff V,°Position in Round 2at St. Helens, especially as 'le new Welshmen are not eligible. k The draw for the second round was made in anchester on Monday night, and resulted as follows: ,J3roughton Rangers v Rochdale Hornets.— Referee, W Tavlor (Hunslet) ; touch judges, H Ball (Wigan) Williams (Widnes). wigan v Oldham.— Referee, R Robinson (Bradford); (ech judges, J Chapman (Swinton) and C Gibson alford). flaStt,.lford v Widnes.—Referee, G F Dickenson 44" fax) • touch judges, H Ogden (Oldham), and A 080 u (Rochdale). Helens v Warrington.—ReferPe, 11 Edmondson z !gtdford) ; touch judges, F Ratcliffe (Swinton) and • 4,191c0e (Broughton). 'les to be played on Saturday, November 2nd.</p>
        <p>THE JUNIOR CUP. draw for the second round cf the Junior Cup "mr;ted Cadishead v Flixton, Runcorn White Star Sherdlev v Sutton, biker v Askham, tv°43,,ertc,rlv Salford St Bartholomew, Winton • . "lingers Dean Rovers v Swinton Park, Healey • Wa;dley, Wigan Athletic v Bedford, Spring Iv Platt Bridge Pemberton Rovers v Lilford, A,7e7 V Leigh Rangers. To be played November at t Peculiarity of the Senior Cup draw is that the tie th hreughton is identical with last season. Rochdale "Tn by 18 points to 3. Wigan and Oldham tri also drawn together last season at Central Park e gemi-fioal round, Oldham winning by a try to leg.t. Roth teams will have a difficulty in repeating the</p>
