      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>Warrington's First Home Win.</head>
        <p>Warrington's First Home Win.</p>
        <p>Up to Saturday, Warrington had not won a match at home this season, though they had gained tao victories on foreign soil, and there was great rejoicing when they finished a strenuous contest with Rochdale Hornets leading 8 points to 5. Both testne were at full strength, the Warrington side including, for the first time, Nicholas the new Welsh half back. Though the weather was showery,there was an excellent attendance, a crowd of 6,000 paying £9B for admission Cheaters stood down from the home forwards, owing to an injury, Bevan filling the vacancy. The teams and oincials were : Warrington.—Jolley, back ; Warren, Renwick Treater and Woodburn, threequarter-1 acks; Nicholas and Rushton, half-backs ' • G Thomas, R Thomas, Cox, Skelhorne, Fearnley and Bevan, forwards. Rochdale Hornets.—Reed, back ; Jones, Robinson, Williamson and Hopwood, threequarter•baoks ; Scho- field and Prudence, half-backs ; Wood, Dearden, Roman, Carter, Slade and Turner, forwards. Referee, E Tongs', Swinton. Touch judges, G Robinson, Runcorn, aud R J Pye, Salford.</p>
        <p>, Warrington opened against the breeze, but fine forward pl..y soon gave them a footing in the Rochdale half. Nicholas was early prominent with a clever cross-kick which resulted in Reed being captured at the corner-flag by Woodburn. Rochdale were bard- pressed, and the home backs flung the ball about with some freedom, Treater holding a little too long on one occasion when the wing man was well placed. A penalty against the locals enabled Rochdale to attack. Hopwood raced down touch and gave Jollay a warm handful near the corner. The full buck was unable to clear and the Hornets strove hard to force a way over. Robinson took a pass from Schofield and dashed through the home centres in thrilling style only to be brought down heavily near line b y Skelhorne. The tackle resulted in the Rochdale centre being slightjy lamed and having to go on the wing. Nicholas broke away from a pack close in and started a passing movement between Rushton and Renwick which took play to centre. The Hornets were soon back again and a penalty being awarded, Jones opened the ' score. with a ,n,eat goal. VVarrington were hard pressed until Iranter opened the way for the finest movement of the game. fie raced round the Rochdale forwards when close to his own goal, and then passed to Renwick. The latter galloped away with Waywell in attendance, and after some flue exchanges between the two, Renwick. doubled inside and handed on to G Thomas. Tne latter made headway before parting to Fearnley. who beat the Rochdale full back finely and raced over under the bar. It was a brilliant try and the cheering was loud and long when Renwick added a goal. Shortly alter Jolley nearly hias sßiodcelabl waiting for the ale man to rush allowingiotrwas a near thing, hut the ulboapsl ti to go gtothoedueacadhdv,adanont advantage goe f referee decided against the claim for a try, and Tranter cleared with a rare burst to centre. The homesters were now going strongly, and Woodburn made a long run on the right, but was bowled in touch near the line. Nicholns and .Tranter were both nearly over from the succeeding pack, and half-time arrived' with War- ington leading by 5 paints to 2 points. The opening of the second half was sensational, for Nicholas responded to the kick-off with a fine punt and follow-up along the touch line. Ile reached the ball before the Runt:Wale full back and passed to Skelhorne, who raced ahead and transferred to R Thomas near the line, the new forward saoring easily. The position was an easy one but Renwick missed the goal. Rochdale's response was worthy of all praise. Their heavy forwards dominated the pack and forced War- rington tack to tt eir own quarter. Here Jones was very energetic and atter a strong run Le threw wide to Robinson, who scrrad smartly at the corner, no goal resulting. The game now became very exciting a desperate forward, rtish with Roman and Woad in the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <p>'</p>
        <p>.• •</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, October 26, 1912.</p>
      </div>
