      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>CROSFIELD'S.</p>
        <p>Crosfield's met Winnington Park at Warrington last Saturday and a fast game ensued. It was not until late in the game that Winnington could claim any advan- tage, eventually winning by 2 goals to nil. This week Croatield's meet Swinton Park at Swinton, the team being as follows :—Goal, A Mellor; backs, SV Ft Ranch, E Paget; half backs. J Harrison. H sandy, A Hodson ; forwards, T Evans, T A Dean, A C Bloomer, B T Dixon, A B Lythgoe. Train leaves Central Station at 11.55 p.m. Referee, W Heywood.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>LATCHFORD.</head>
        <p>LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>The First Eleven met St. Helens last week and won by 7-1. The visitors deplored the absence of their usual outside right (Rudd), who had been chosen to play in the County Trial Match. On the other hand Latchford were strengthened by the return of Travis at centre- forward, and he led the attack well and also scored three goals. Dale put across a lot of good centres, which afforded scoring opportunities to the inside men, and there were several nice combined movements among the halves and forwards before the interval, when the score was 5-0. St Helens played up better and scored their only point, hut two more goals came to the home side before the end. The St. Helens full backs were in good form and could not be blamed for the defeat as they had too much work to do through their halves being unreliable. Moreover they played clean, gentle- manly hockey. Wilkinson gave a good display at centre-half, for Latchford and Isherwood also did well. The goals were scored by Travis (3), Minards (2), Pear- son and Wilkinson one each. The Second Eleven found St. Helens "A" short- handed so they lent the home team a player and fielded ten, which levelled the sides better. Latchford main- tained their improved form and put up their third successive win, the final score being 4-1. The St Helens goalkeeper proved to be in extra good trim or the score would have been much heavier than it was. He repeatedly ran out gamely to the forwards to prevent them getting in shots and his tactics were quite success- ful. He had no chance whatever ~ith the shots that scored. Latchford's goals were scored by Banks (3) and Hackney (1). Gibbs and Bradshaw at half-back for the visitors worked bard and made plenty of openings for the forwards. Laver on the right wing played a good game, also Eccles and Bruhl at full back. The First Eleven have no match to-day. The Second Eleven entertains Brooklands "B " at Latchford and their team ia :—Three full backs : Eccles. Bruhl, Mull; Gibbs, Bradshaw, halves ; Laver, Pearson, Hackney, Minards, Colton, torwards. Referee : Mr Dixon. Bully off 2-45 p.m.</p>
        <p>GOLF.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>WARRINGTON CLUB.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CLUB.</p>
        <p>The monthly bogey competition was played on Saturday. The results were as follows : FIRST DIVISION. E A Connor 2 down E L Robinson 3 e F S Horner F R Roberts J Irepherd W Dutton R M Roberts SI:coND Divis H E Bower 4 down F A Frost 0 Loithouse E A White A Warburton</p>
        <p>TOWN BLEMISHES. WHAT THE SMOKE NUISANCE HAS TO ANSWER FOR.</p>
        <p>In a lecture, on "Beauty in Civic Life," at a meeting organised by the Bolton Progre.ssive League and Hous- ing and Town-planning Association on Monday, Mr. Edward Carpenter spoke about the smoke nuisance. Ile bad heard, be said, Hist Bolton was contemplating a great architectural scheme of town-planning, hut he did not know what to say about it. Twelve years ago the public built a very beautiful town hall in Sheffield. i But he never looked at t now, it was simply a black mass, and one might Ls well look at a pile of soot. What was the good of spending hundreds of thousands on beautiful buildings if they were to be ruined by smoke immediately they were built? But there was realty DO reason why they should be ruined. The great mass of the smoke that disfigured our towns could be done away with easily if we would only spend a little money on appliances. As another help to restoring beauty, Mr Carpenter suggested that large tracts of moorland adjacent to the to us should be acquired by the municipalities. It, was necessary that there should be tracts of country which townspeople could range over freely. A hundred years ago, at any rate in Sheffield, these moors were the common land of the people. The landlords afterwards enclosed them for a mere song, but when the people had to acquire them again for reservoirs or such like purposes preposteroirs prices were charged.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>SPORTS</head>
        <p>SPORTS</p>
        <p>CARTOON.</p>
        <p>HE IMPS SHOOTING ON THE MOORS.</p>
        <p>van, looked serious for Warrington until Renwick and Rushton pulled them up at half-way. Tranter &amp;eared and Reed after misjudging the flight of the ball was lucky to get out of a tight corner with a host of the opposition in the vicinity. Jolley was inclined to take risks, and once in waiting for the ball to bounce be was captured near the line by the energetic Rochdale forwards. A movement between Tranter, Nicholas, Rushton and IVoodburn placed War- rington in a more favourable position, but the relief was not of long duration, for in the closing stages the visitors attacked in determined style. Schofield got away and had the line at his mercy, but when five yards from the rut he was robbed of the ball by Waywell, and Warrington escaped. The Rochdale men continued dangerous until the close, but the defence continued to prevail and in the last five minutes Renwick rolled Robinson into touch a yard from the line when the Rochdale man seemed a certain scorer. Final : Warrington, I goal 2 tries (8 points) ; Rochdale Hornets 1 goal I try (5 points).</p>
        <p>MANCHESTER AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. DIVISION I. RESULTS TO SATURDAY. P. W. L. D. For Mat. P. Wardley 4. 3 I. 0 37.. 24. 7 Rylands, Rens Pendleton Prestwich Flixton Barton 4. I 2.. 1 13.. 05 3 Ashton Eaerton Clifton St. Bart's</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>ST. HELENS FARE BADLY AT DARESBURY</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS FARE BADLY AT DARESBURY</p>
        <p>There was a crowd of about 3,000 at this match ,which was played et Dewsbury. St Helens were short of Turtill and Flanagan. Dewsbury at once took up a strong position, and Ernest Ware opened the scoring by landing a goal from a penalty kick. Price next started a fine movement, which enabled Rhodes to score a try, E Ware failing at goal. Milner put in a pretty indivi- dual effort which gave another opening to Rhodes, Ware spin failing to improve. Browning made a floe run. and Mavitty scored behind the posts, Molyneux failing at goal. Sharpies scored a clever try, E Ware failing to improve. C Ware managed to put on a couple of tries, and Price also crossed his opponents' line. Stringer improved two of the tries. On resuming St Helens were segressive,and Woods and Greenwood each scored a try, that of the latter being goaled by Woods. Just before call of " no side " Sharpies scored a really clever try. Result :—Dewsbury 3 goals 7 tries (27 points), St Helena 1 go al 3 tries (11 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>RUNCORN EXTEND OLDHAM.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN EXTEND OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>The meeting between these old-time rivals in the Lancashire Cup Competition at Runcorn produced a keen struggle for supremacy. That Oldham were able to win the game was due to their more finished methods when near the line, Runcorn invariably failing at the critical moment to do the right thing. Runcorn were handicapped through losing Lewis, who was off the field the greatest part of the first half. Oldham obtained the lead as the result of a good penalty goal by Ferguson, but this was equalised by Richardson before the interval by a penalty goal. The visitors appeared to find their feet on resuming and the game in this half was barely a minute old when following a tricky run by Holbrook, Cook dashed up and scored, Ferguson gosling. Tries were added by Holbrook and Williams, both of whom were allowed an easy run in. Runcorn, however, were still triers, and a tine dribble by Newall, Donoghue, and Inman ended in the latter scoring, for Richardson to augment. Result :—Oldham 2 goals 3 tries (13 points), Runcorn 2 goals 1 try (7 points). EASY CUP-TIE FOR WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Coventry were visitors to Widnes in the Dincashire Cup Competition on Saturday the match being played before 2,000 spectators. Coventry gave a very poor dis- play, and although Widnes put on such a heavy score, their form left a lot to be desired, Laws was the first to score, after passing by Reid and J O'Garra, the latter adding another try, following some passing by Reid and G Aspey. Pennington then ran in on his own, and Jones improved. The second half was very one- sided. Jones placed a penalty kick, and tries were scored by Laws, Cosgrove, and J O'Garra. Noon, and H O'Garra. Jones placed four goals. Result :—Widnes 6 goals 8 tries (36 points) Coventry nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>NORTHERN COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON " A " AT ROCHDALE.—Warrington sent a strong side to Rochdale and were unlucky to be beaten by penalty kicks. In the first half they had the best of the game, and Dickenson made an opening for Clare to score at the corner, Critchlev failing at goal. Hollinrake kicked a goal for Rochdale, and the teams changed ends with Warrington leading by 3 points to 2. Turning round, the Rochdale men were early seen to advantage and as a result of a passing movement Lawson scored at the corner, for Green (formerly of Warrington), to add the goal. Warrington speedily retaliated and the outcome of good passing and a fine run by Carter led up to Dickenson scoring near the posts, but Critchley failed to land the goal. Only a point divided the teams and desperate work was witnessed. Warrington repeatedly menaced the home goal, but their efforts lacked finish. Near the close Green placed a penalty goal for the home side, and the final score was: Rochdale Hornets, three goals one try (nine points) ; Warrington, two tries (six points).</p>
      </div>
