      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKINGMEN'S ASSOCIATION. N. A. CENTRE.</p>
        <p>The conditions last week for members were wretched Those who ventured far from town had very little angling, the rain spoiling sport in all districts. There is little doubt that had the rain kept off on Sunday last members would have had a better cstch than for weeks previous. After the five or six weeks of dry weather the rain freshened up the streams and fish were taking the baits offered better than they base for several weeks, but no sooner had an angler batted a hole when he had to run for shelter and he was luckey if he got three hours fishing in on Sunday. Long Barn is to be cleaned in parts this week, and those who are interested please give assistance.</p>
        <p>MICKLE TRA FFORD. The Gowy had a fair number last week end, seeing it is now getting late on in the ssason, but conditions were off as far as angling was concerned. Several members improved on their records of previous weeks, but nothing in the shape of weighers as taken. The rain poured down here during Sunday afternoon, and shelter is not to be thought of at the Gowy. One or two umbrellas were left behind—or what was left of them after the hurricane about 3.30, and anglers had a trying time to get hack to the village. The Workingmen's Society had several friends from Manchester. paying the Gowy a visit, but what they thought of it is best left unsaid. lhe morning looked so inviting that one or two came without overcoat or mackintosh. or light shoes. Evidently they did not know the Gowy and its banks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>NORTON.</head>
        <p>NORTON.</p>
        <p>A falling off of members here, and angling under bridge holes is not a pleasant experience, as the full force of the draft is felt there. The beat we can report is a catch of four roach and one gudgean which does not disturb those already weighed in. A VISIT TO NORTH IVALES. _</p>
        <p>This week-end a party are trying a visit t) Wales to try and change the set board. Members who have registered their specimens will feel all the easier when the party returns as it is the last week-end for the Board and those who are making the journey are men who usually come up to the table when the sets are being paid out. A. S.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>One of the most laughable e.ketches we have seen for some time is presented at the Hippodrome this week by Mr Walter Bird's West End Co. "Her Husband" is indeed a fine production and abounds in rich humour supplied in the main by Mr George Hill. as Jimmy Josser, a cowardly burglar. He is the typical clumsy law-breaker and in face of the wrath of his brother in cri=e, he displays a non- chalance which cannot fail to evoke roue of laughter. Five persons are involved in the sketch and each one disposes of his or her part in a capable manner. Miss G V Clifford as Jessie Moore, the doctor's wife, is responsible for the introduction of a little acceptable drama and the success of the sketch is attributable in a large measure to her finished acting. The wonderful changes in the fashions affords Cromo and Hamilton ample scope in the burlesque seena " rashione The " model " causes roars of laughter as he Iparades in the various outrageous Outfils feminine of course. His hobble-51'11f make-up waa perhaps the most humorous in its utter absurdity. The " The Little Puritan "is the title of a comprehensive sporty sketch ably presented by France and Stewart. The couple have a breezy style which is very success- ful. Happy Attxvood, the great little comedian, with a manner so unusually soon wins over the audience who are not slow to appreciate and are unstinted in their approbation. Jack Smiles, although billed as et comedian, is far more successful with his expert dancing than as a purveyor of comedy. His steps, brought the house down with their intricacy and swiftness of execution. Graver scores a decided success in a comedy ventriloquial act. Rowland is a clever juggler although this class of entertainment is fast losing popularity. No doubt if there were more of Rowlands' stamp it would have a successful revival. Daisy Desmond pleases immensely with her catchy songs and the captivating way in which she renders them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>Another capital programme has been arranged fo next week and will include :—Terry Twins, the dupli- cate comedians ; The Five Cliftons, gymnastic equilibriatic pe.formance ; Mr and Miss De Brean, Japanese entertainment with amazing Pffeot% ; Simple Smart, "The acrobatic sportsman "; The Great Lumars, comedy acrobats ; Guy and Graham, 'The Two Johnnies " ; Dick and Dorothy trio, music and dancing ; Louis J Seymour, the popular light comedian ; and Violet Romaine, vocalist.</p>
        <p>LEICESTER-SQUARE BURGLARS.</p>
        <p>Scrambling over roofs, burglars early on Thursday broke into three buildings in Green-street, Leicester-- square, but got practically nothing for their pains.</p>
        <p>FOR</p>
      </div>
