        <p>. 4.1 The stateMbpt ittioL m THE CASE OF the House of Commons THE DOCTORS by the Chancellor gthe Exchequer should' .rt'at an end at once to the difficulty with ltp, medical profession. It has seeme.d there has been only one fairly,' sotind argument on the side of the doctors; The Government pays Bs. 6d. per head for Meilleal supervision in the case of Post Office employee, These, it is said,, are picked men ; they are not likely to want 'much attendanep from th,;3` doctor. If; therefore 85..6d. per head is con- sidered 'to be a fair :pride tti *gm-0 0 Tgist Office men, it cannot be a rate Of ever-payment in cases in which there is likely to be a ranch larger demand for medical .attendance :' This Would 'be an indisputable argUnieUt if the iate of vayment for men employed in the Pnst Offi9e had not been Pitched, too high,: That, however, is the whole matter of debate, If the terms have been placed tOo high in 006 case where the whole cost does not ain§upt to anything considerable, it certainly Aloes! dot follow that the scale should be made the 'ame where the expenditure 'rune' into inAfions. There can be no doubt, WS believe, that the Post Office scale is too high. It is based on the usual generosity of, Governments, :In ' most cases in which taxpayers are concerned everybody gets a little more than he is entitled to—except the taxplYer blOself. The taxpayer pays over the .kingicies, as the ordinary-saying goes.' '</p>
        <p>When, hOwever, the waliefulness of otie Fl department of public life is proposed as a model in another instance in whicii,Vany thousands of pounds take the place ceta‘l'eW' hundreds, the case is altered entirely. What has to be considered is not What has been done in an individual instance?, bnt vir4a,t It is practicable to do over a greatlxenlarged area. The argument based on the payment made for medical attendence in the case of Post Office employes is, in fact, of no value what- ever. It would be as powerful on one side as on the other. That is to say, it would just as easily prove that the rate had been placed too high in this particlar case as that 141 medical payments under an Insurancei stem should be at the same rate. There;seemS: ,to be, no doubt that the rate was placed too high. The actuarial investigation that has been made went to prove that in the first place the offer made to the medical profession by the Chancellor of the Exchequer was not only much in advance of medical earnings from the Friendly Societies, but also of medical earnings from the ordinary patient. What the Government offered was, in fact, better than what the medical profession had been, if not contented with, at least willing to receive. However, the Chancellor of the Exchequer— and this means the Government--has not been eager to make hard terms' with the medical men of the country. The statement made in the House of Commons on Thursday and explained by Mr Lloyd George at con- siderable length elsewhere, disposes of the argument derived from the case of the Podi Office, even if there were any validity in it. It brings medical remuneration up to the Post Office limit. The medical faculty will in future receive some millions a year more than it has heretofore received from its dealings with the public "or its own," if we may be permitted to employ the phrase. It is about to do exceedingly well. Whether it will take advantage of its opportunity re- mains to be seen. " The fault of: the Dutch," wrote Canning, "Is giving too little and asking too much." The medical professoon has certainly asked too nit&amp; ' But when i comes to understand that it ought not ,</p>
        <p>permit itself. tn.bpllecl,hy the, political oppo- - tlxv4sTrttninaantit ,will begin at once it° apmTiate thd f4h4stt tit; h (LS teter had abetter friend'</p>
        <p>, 7 t THE WHllck , l' 'demonstration in favour Siavt TriArFlC. 'of the restoration of the , ..The . Parr Hall Criminal Law Amend- ment Bill to its-original form and its placing on the statute book, was remarkable for the , number and the earnestness of the people i who made up the gathering. Only a verY short notice and limited advertising we/ , possible, and yet fully 1 500 adult persons o - both sexes flocked to the Hall in erd.er' to record their unanimous opinion 111) favour of a drastic resolution wili„,,,, leaves , the House of Commons '- loophole for ' the admission of weakening amendments. It has taken nearly thirtl years to 'bring home, to the public mind tilt' enormity of this awful traffic in vice, tpoi , extent to which it has spread, and theiavau danger it threatens to the girl-life of our oval and other "civilized " nations. It is 0° late to moralise on the White Slave Traffic bfLage a product of modern civilization. Wila,' "e require is a means to stamp it out, eau tht Bill now, before Parliament offers the 111:as11 effective' facilities ' for . repression 'obi° have yet ' been ' 'devised in , this , countrY; The public is ,at last roused to tlille urgency of the question and demands passing of the original measure with a I'd much more certain and united than is hear on most of the questions of the day. ' lleistie,; at any rate, the Government unquestional;,' %lias„a mandate, and Wis a matter for 0,,,,'; gratulation that , the attitude of the Secretary inspire&amp; such high hopes of stle,c;ii, for the promoters and supporters of the There has been, and still fs, 'apposition 'of plenty, to the more revolutionary clauses the Bill. But some of the waverers are eV); jag round. Mr. Harold Smith, M.F., ",•ate! turned right-about-face just in time to wri` 0 to the meeting of his constituents. givil;,g ':',o expedient '1)0 ." irreivocable decigioa,,_ 'fe favour of the original Bill. Bravo! "TB-ke,,Tt is more joy in heaven over one sinner repenteth," etc. -</p>
