        <p>The protest meeting against the Disestab- . hshment of the, Welsh' Church, though so lavishly aonounced, idid not draw, eithet a large, or an eat49siastic audience, to Tarr Hall on Monday'tyning— There was an air of lassitude about the whole assembly, and the efforts of the speakers to fire the enthusiasm of their hearers was suggestive of a man trying to strike sparks out of a wet flint. The "'protesters" are supporting a dying cause, and the fact seemed to have impressed itself upon them sub-consciously. They would never acknowledge it, of course. The " last ditch " that Lord Dynevor is going 'to die in, will be made light of as an obstacle to the onward march of a free people.</p>
        <p>• * , • . I Latest reports regarding the salt.bearing ' area at Hiatley and Warburton indicate the , strong probabilty of great indastrialdevekip- ., Anenta in the near future. The analyst's '. report on the brine and the salt-bearing strata is exceedingly favourable and _fmnanciers are already seeking sites for the 'erection of a salt-producing plant. A banguine forecast suggests that ere long ileatloy will be the centre of a regular hive 'of industry, linked directly to the Ship Canal for expert p-arposes. Another decade may see this quiet pastoral area turned into a second Northwich or Winnington, with the smoke* and clang of industry replacing the verdant peacefulness of nature. .</p>
      </div>
