        <p>Parish Church Sale of Work. The annual Sale of Work in, connection with the Parish Church will' be herd ia Fairfield Schools, on Thursday, Friday and Saturday next The Infirmary. The Hon. Treatturer giatefully acknowledges the receipt from Mr. Robert Stone, Newton Park, of cheque value £2l, as a donation to the funds of tbe institution, and in respect of which Mr. Stone becomes Life Governor.</p>
        <p>Accident to Mr. Cyril .Greenall. Mr Cyril Greene% of Carlton Scnpop (a cousin of Sir Gilbert Greet:tall) was hunting with the Belvoir founds last Friday, when he had the misfortune to fracture and dislocate his collar bone. The injury was caused by his hone falling when the hounds were in full cry near Carlton Ashes. Royal Court Theatre* Travis Green's grand production " Queen of the Redskins" will be presented at the Royal Court Theatre next week. The caste includPs Mr Jerrold Reather as Bronco Bill and Miss Hilda Shirley as Wahneetah. There will be two yerformatiees nightly and the usual matinee orkThuraday. The Plcturedrome: This house increases in popularity as each week Passel' by. The principal pictures at the three changes next week will 138 : Monday ,and Tuesday, 'The great circus catastrophe," a sensational storrOt circus life sub a charming love interest; Wednesday. and Thursday The madman " ; Friday and baturday, 'The inner circle." . ANTII RA C ITE B2ovr,e, clean, cheerful, smokeless, no tomes.—Agent, J. S. G 4 LAND. 51, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. ' Promotion for Captain Crosfield.</p>
        <p>The local Territorial -Orders contain the following ti announcement :—The following extracts from "Loudon Gazette," dated Ilth October, 1912, are publislred for information : "4th Battalion Prince of Wales's ; Volunteers (South Lancashire Regiment) Captain Monorary Captain in the Army) George R Crosfield to be a major; dated Ist September, 1912." The Infirmary Ball. A welbsttended meeting of the Ladies' Infirmary Ball Candmi,tee was held at the Infirmary on Wednes- 'day, Mrs F Aylmer Frost in the chair.—lt was decided- ; to hold the ball at Parr Hall on Thursday, January 4 th , 1913 Mrs-- I-I-Shepherd Crosq, Mrs F Aylmer Frost, Mrs Henry Greenall, and Mrs W Peter Rylands were appointed a sub-committet to make the necessary ..arrangements. lady Greenall was re-elecied Presi- 'lent of the Bail Crtmmittee. Messrs G Ireland Blackburne and 3 E Greenall gre inga joint hon. secretaries. ;</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath • Butcher's Bon Killed.' The circumstances attending` the death of Ross 'Bailey, aged 18, son of John Bailey, butcher, of tStociton Heath, were investigated yesterday, by the Manchester City Coroner (Mr. Gibson).--The evidence 14hOwed that the yoith was riding a five-year -old show horse at Cheadle Holme when ,ti7o- rattling of some milk cans caused the animal t) bolt. Deceased tried to dismount and fell violently on his back in the road- Iv, ay. He was removed to the Manchester Royal infirmary, where he died on Wednesday afternoon, following an operation,—A verdict of "Accidental leath " was returned. , LAMPS, Oil SO:O7En Coke Stoves .—J. S. a-ARLAND</p>
        <p>47'4—Bridge at., Wartington,--Advt. Parish Church Men'a Bowling Club. ' The annual supper and prize distribution in con- taection with the above club was bald at the King's, fe on Wednesday evening last, when a good turnber sat down to'do justine to the admirable cater- ihß. A smoking concert followed the supper, presided over by the Rev A J Bratt and excellent contributions Were given by the following artistes : Mr F M barby and W Brandreth (pianoforte duet) and songs 1.?..9 Messrs P C Price, J 11 Draper, W Hopkins, F -noon and Walter Dennett. During the interval the Prizes were Tr, ssnted to the following : Messrs R W Ants, A Gret,ty, W Hopkins, S Yates, Samuel Tilling, James Burrows, S Watson and Walter Bennett, A vote of thanks was accorded to the Chairman on the 1 P2oposition of Mr Craik, seconded by Mr R T Harding, ' Atessrs F M Darby, and W Bratglreth accompanied in en efficient manner. Presentation to Councillor Steel. There was a large attendance in the saleroom of the lollue Bell Hotel, Hotsemarket-street on Saturday 'evening when Councillor T 'S Steel was pre3ented by the Warrington Branch of the Amalgamated Union of °perative Bakers and Confectioners with a handsome Silver afternoon tea set and tray," as a small token of I.aPect and esteem in appreciation of bis long connec- t'on with the *Dion as solicitor." Mr. Walter ilathbone (district secretary) presided, and among, Others present, in addition to Councillor Steel, 'were ' kr Frank Stringer, Mr Greeley, Mr Eaton (secretary tbe branch) and Mr •Oultram (treasurer). Many 4 2lateresting speeches were made and Councillor Steel in toply dealt with his six years work in the Town, ' Council and also introduced the .question of the • working-classes and the Town Planning Act. Several 'songs were rendered by members ç,f the Union '4erious shooting Accident. b A serious shooting accident befell Mr. Arthur "acock, one of the owners of the Fairbourne Estate, ar Barmouth, and formerly of Warrington, on • 65t0rday afternoon. A. number of sportsmen were • 'shooting on land bear the Barmouth If links, and 1e of the party, a Mr James Howell, fired at a rabbit. ‘tr Peacock was standing on the estate watching the °Petations when the rabbit bolted in his direction, : lad a shot was fired in a direct line with Mr Peacock's ;4teE!. He received the full force of the shot which lliCted serious injuries, resulting in the loss of 'Sight in one eye. Mr Peacock was speedily removed Pairbourne post tffice, and within half-an-hour •'uva. Dingle and Williarns,of Barmouth were in attend- . ;lee. Drs Russ Wood and Henderson motored to airbourne on Sunday from Shrewsbury. On inquiry last night we were informed that Mr Peacock was , still in a critical condition. ttiecrLErty.—Joseph Rodgers &amp; Sons' celebrated Knives ve4 Forks, &amp;c.—Agent, J. S. GARLAND, Bridge at., w 4rrington.—Advt. ntertainment at Cockhedge Mission. 4. 011 Monday last an excellent entertainment was ~„ven at the Wycliffe Cockhedge Misssion. Mr Holland Ti`a°sided over a large gathering. The programme t tcalghout was of a very high order. A musical •rt was given by Mrs Sephton' Misses Elsie Hewitt, Smith, Messrs Sephton and E Walker, as soloists; vrts Were given by Mr and Mrs Cephton, Messrs E • ‘; 0. ker and Sephtou. Mr Kilduff in a recital entitled , !very day life "delighted the audience. items were provided by Mrs Elliott in ;ketch "Bet spriggs and Election Day" Auckland who is a favourite with his d funny songs. The spendid talent all ; a request Or ,many encores. Miss A Trow 'weretthe'siYeompanists. 4 vote artidteil ililit`i3roposed by Mr G T by MY PLlferralL</p>
