        <p>Women's Suffrage. _ A meeting under , the auspices of the Wartington branch Of the National Union of Women's 'Suffrage' Societies will be held iii the Co-operatfib-Hill, 0&amp;, Wednesday evening. Full particulars,areiadverrisect, The Spiritualist Society. • On Sunday arld:Mooday last, there were ,c,iowded audiences at the Druids' Hall, the attrastion,being Mr' Tan Tyrrell, Said to be the most remitikable clair- voyant And cleirandient medium in the cctuntryi,l4e, gave displays of hie ,gifts st,tach meetiag, OVer fifty 'descrtpuOns of "dead ',' people, together witlit their lull naes,'S.red In &amp;cite cases dance' anecfatesf death and sometimes last earthly residence..,Sonie 'of those described were prominent townspeople, but, the generality were those in humbler walks Of life„,buit all were recognized. On Wednesday ' 'eveuirik 'it 'social evening ,was spent at the invitation of Mr W,Gilbert, a hard-working member of the Lyceum and the sonifaY, whose 21et birthday occurred recently. Over fifty were ' invited, and aftera splendut tea, 'games and competitions for prizes were entered' into. Mr'Stout sang appropriately •• Many happy returns of the clan" nd Mr Gilbert ;as made 'the recipient of Many useful presents, and was greeted with the singing of 'For .hes a jolly good fellow," , , 1, t Drowning, Fatality. . —aoroner nfighntise held an inquest at Warrington on TuesdaY'on James Nicholson (58), ' a slater, 13, Wellingtoiestreet. whose 'body was found in the river at Howley. William Nicholson, an insurance agent, of 9, Wellington-street, said up to eight months ago his father had followed his trade regularly, but since then bad. done Mall, jobs.' Ma mother had often reproached his , father! for not, doing regular, work and after the quarrels his father appeared to give way to drinking bents. He „isppeated to become stratige in his Intim:ter and that had been more noticeable during , the, past month. At 10-50 on Sattird,ay. night be saw his father enter the ashpli at his house and he asked' 'him what he was doing. He mumbled something in reply' and went away up Howley Fields,. Witness, thought nothing of it, because his father Used to wander up , and down. ‘ At that time his father, Was drunk," as he had been more or less during the ' week. The body, was recovered from the river, on Sunday, morning.— " Found drowned" was the verdict. Trafalgar Day—Essay Competition. The anniversary of Trafalgar day fell on Monday, and its observance was • principally marked locally by the` presentation of the shield and prizes given by Alderman Bennett,' for essays on a selected subject dealing with some aspect of national .4:lutyj: ,The shield, which was won by Charles Boardman, of 93, Thelwall-lane for an essay on the " Titanic " is to be held by his school (Christ Chtirch) for 12 monthfi.' The Orr prize essayist in the elementary, schools was •Eirtinit Fleming of 12, Manchester-road, who attends the 'airfield-Grls' Schoolcrehich ,had the honour of holding 1 thaehield, last 'year. The prise winters at the §scomplary School were :--,Seniors Harold Emerson, 44, 'N'illis-street, so-a 'Charlotte A. Furness, Fern Lea, Great Stihkey.,..'Junora Muriel Chidlaw, fiS, Manchester-road, and Dennis Cooper, 1.3, Butter- market-street. The ,two former essayists were rewarded by Alderman Bennett, ,and the prizes for the juniors were presented by Mr. Robson, the head- master. The subjects selected for the essay writing were : "The loss of the Birkenhead," "Harold, the last of the Saxon kings," and "' What can we do for England?" Alderman Bennett visited the schools and in presenting the shield to Boardman, who also received a number of books, he addressgd the scholars on the theme of British bravery and valour at sea. FOR MODERN FIREPLACES and Cooking Ranges CoTn bitting economy With simplicity try J. S. GattLsiin ironmonger, Bridge at., Warrington.--Advt. '</p>
        <p>t be:</p>
        <p>Bewsey. Adult School. r On Sunday morning last an address was given upon •the White Slave Traffic by Mr Wilfrid Rowland. asked those present not to shut their eyes'to 'ugly facts, but to try to realise that there was not only a vast amount of immorality around, but that there .v:as a highly organised trade on an international scale, which made vast profits out of vice. Social workers, who saw .most clearly the awful results of this damnable business, had sought for many years to strengthen the law, but bills had, been blocked time after time in Parliament. Just now there was every prospect of passing, a bill, and public opinion auaht to express itself with no uncertain voice if the bill, which had been seriously weakened in committee, were to become in its final form an effective means of stopping this iniquitous traffic. Further powers we're urgently needed. Mr Rowland told of the baits that are held out to unsuspecting women and pointed out that in this matter the poorer classes of the community suffered most. Wrong economic conditions had much to do with wrong morals. Besides further drastic legislation there was an imperative call for a higher standard, of morality,. All were at least responsible for their own share in the creation of the m0ra1i549363- phere". The evil deeds we deplored sprang fror4 eviit thoughts generated in' atmosphere that was fenl. ,T4sperpion on the, .IVennt itoupl2o. 94110 e problem. ,</p>
