        <p>two daughters with whom much sympathy is expressed in their bereavement. The only surviving members of his father's family are, we understand, his sister, Mrs T J Down, and Mr John Guest. The late Air. Quest was the sun of the late Mr4obn Guest, who for some years earned on the business of a tanner in Tanner's-lane. He was educated at Wesley College, Sheffield, and graduated at O.t. John's College, Cambridge, taking a high place in first-cla‘s honours. A. devoted member of the Wesleyan Methodist Chnich. Mr. Guest had the work in the foreign , mission field particularly at heart, and he went out to India as a missionary, rendering faithful service for nearly two years. Returning to England, about forty years ago he founded the tannery in Winwick-street, which he carried on with the assist- ance of his brother, Mr John Guest, who has since retired from the firm. Three or four years ago Mr. E. J. Guest left Warrington and took up his residence at " Lynton," Woodhill-ioad,'Colwyn Bay, where he took an active part in movements for the betterment of the people. The business at Warrington is now con- ducted under the name of "Quest Bros., Ltd.," by the deceased gentleman's sons. For many years Mr Guest was associated with Bold-street Wesleyan Church and had held all the offices open to a layman , in the Warrington and Frodsham Circuits. He. was an acceptable local preacher and his services were in great request in all parts of the cireuit. He occupied the pulpits at Bold-Street and Padgate Weslo an churches so recently as October 6th. Mr. Guest took a prominent part in building Bewsev-road Church and and vras its first sonietY steward. Mr Guest was fore- most in urgiug•the duty of Wesleyans to make some provision for elementary education in the Borough This led to the opening of Silver-street School in 1877 He Was appointed treasurer and held that office until the 'clay of, his death. In addition Mr Guest was secretary and Government correspondent for the last 20 years. He initiated and carried through three very coneiderable enlargements of the premises. Mr Guest was keenly interested in municipal affairs and from' 1877 to 1882 he served the com- mit 1, , r f on Couneil. He was 17 b) be o the Tvv 4apponned a lustic. of the peace for Warrington in 4885gehv. it we h •Itefulootfl:F,sto ee-vte•-,1-144; littncaster. tiqlteritrice 'c'ause" had in Mr Guest a zealous :'sdvoeate and generous supporter and he was ever</p>
        <p>ready to advance licensing reform—a subject of which' tie had made a special study. Realizing edap immense f importance of educational work, Mr Guest eavipured, to forward it to the utmost of hip power adilltipii tpthis wfrk‘foriSilverotstreet school* whi 3, ref4reiieg has 81'1'00 been made, -he was for:.,:a time- 11.. representative of the - local No4onformisi echoola (in the Warrington Education •,Commirtee. 'Mr Quest ' was closely ' ldeatitied with the British and Foreign Bible Society, and rendered it -considerable 'pecuniary assistance, his experiences a missionary in India convincing him of tha eminently useful, and indeed, indispensable char,acter of its woild-wide operations. In politics be was a Rtaunch Liberal who never hesitated to speak out on the aide of progress and democracy. Of an _unostentatnam disposition he did a great deal of good work jri a quiet way and a very large circle of friends will deeply mourn his death.</p>
        <p>THE FUNERAL. , . ' The funeral took place aftcawyn Bay on Tuesday afternoon, in the presence of a large gathering of rela- tives and friends. The chief mourners werP Mrs Guast (wftiow), Mr and Mrs G E Guest (s n and daughter in- lew),-.lllr It W Guest ;son), Mrs Anderson t(daughter), , Miss Guest (daughter), Mr John Gue-t (brother), Mr Arthur D 4WD (nephew), Rev F H and kips Clornall „(nepliew and neice), Ali ~1 McLaren (nephew). Amongst those present from the Warrington district were : Reit W. A.l3e,ckett (superintendent Werringtou Circuit); Nfry 'A Vac:cis (senior 'Circuit, Steward). Messrs .1 G 'Withetishaw and John Upson (ea-steward), iind repre- htnting Silver-street school and the Education Com- ,. itthe,' Rev F W Kernick," M.A. (late of Warrington, , ova Of Blackburn), Mr It H Posnett (Runcorn), aod Mr lirandreth (Helsby). :-- -, Au:impressive service was conducted in ,St John's ,Eviish Wesleyan Church 11,v Rev E Lightfoot Smith lis 4rlngtopian 1, and the Rev E Lloyd Jones gave In address b'eautlful in diction, and wonderfully skilful In its , analysis of Mr Guest's character. -He -Nsid.. be bad beep. mpre - closely, associated with 14r'Quettit- for the' . last '4O years thin any Outside his own family and had learnt :to know his very soul. He was, a true gentleman in that he never knowingly by word or deed hurt the feelings of another, spit lie was a true Ph/Isamu in that he carri,ed'sill his ;religious principles Itito' every t'sphere of life: -There wits a vein Ofinielancholis in his Beni which accounted for!much. It:Accounted tot( some ' timidity. MOO self-dhitrust: To some extentatthindered a e tell use of his great puivers. His whole life was a struggle agaisst temperament. By dispositiop he was tue itost man, to :AVE.'S any prominence. itti , humility would, have predeittetftnuch, of his usefuldietis. Yet;principla triumphed slid he tesulutely fought agvinst his own, tefeperament and putting ;ids own inclinations on one ski-he unflincningte pursued the with he believed to he right,-Mtlough by to doing he often caused himself much pain. The world WRS a poor,lopelier place for his :!-• Toe Rev:, William. Itlellort of •Coustay (forMerly of Warrington), alal took part in the service. The hymns "On tte Itrearrction_ morning!" iand ;"Therßiug of loti.e.'' Were' sang!._-,,'-_,, Many beatittful floria tributes were placed ;over the grave, incturtiug wreaths from the employees of Messrs -Groest.,.4lnret,"a the :staff .of Silver-street Wesleyan day school, and faun Silver-street Wesleyan Sunday school. ,s,-.• AN AIintECIATIO.N. -, 4.ffi the 'ff'estit of Mr----,(Tiist many will feel a deep seaS* of personal logs, There was a 'singular fascina- tion in his. character. Few men reveal the charm of chliviiian' gentleinanlineWto such a degree as it was maul/tested in ' his personality. His culture was evident in life's most. oidinary affairs. Strong con: victiotis never caused him to be intolerant. He 4vas. ;.refined -without fastidiouseess. If the- euccest of 'life is determined by...vrlitit a _man is he had been very !successful. Hi st career must be judged, by the lofti- ness Of his -tilts, and .by the linfinetsce he parted ,upon others.' '• HIS Stint was not lost lo '-blisiness. During , . , , 4risY first two years at Warrington , it waft 'my privilege to haitelong walks with hjui on Monday morfiltigs. He was a devoted Warringtenian ;!' everYthing relating ,to the town's welfare deeply .ciskicerned' hitch f Under 1 , his'guidancelmade my acquaintance with the lovely, I country in Cheshire, and alea'''.vittli the lii'siury of: Abe. town and /neighbouthood.:,,lly first' •!visit to a , .. (alum was in his 'company,' ,In • all ; our es m- versation l.' •I•• do • not '' remember an'...Occnik. -lien he -spoke disparagingly OfsaitOther's Ykiitle,hl.,' tone man did be • undervalue —himself. There was a ,vein of ,pessimism in his • nature' but it onlY' ;found filnpressi Ain regard to his own work. One who Vass an upright life in business, keeps his spiritual life fresh in an age largely materialistic, so influences .•trthers that they feel ashamed of slackness and are ' nstantly inspired for better service, has not lived in in. Amongst the most powerful spiritual influences io '''my life isl that of Mr E J Guest; and to my many Waitrons for'thankfulness to Methodiem is added still litibthee, for through its Citchit sisteirn'O'gae .bri,,ught into intimate association with MR of ,the .kindliest ,Cliiiittlan diameters, One of the truest type of gentle- , 'mOrs, toe found anywhere. •</p>
        <p>• TtloS. MILLER. MAGISTERIAL REFERENCE.• 'Prior to the commencement of the ordinary business at the Borough , Police Coust on Monday, tle.Mayor. (Cowcillor'Dr Joseph) who presided, said It was painful, duty to allude to the loss which, k he Benco had sustained la the sudden death ,of one of their esteemed coin-agues,. Guest: Aldrongh•.be had known him or 35. yews lc had never been his privilege to sit with ork.the bench. but he was quite sure his loss tirvultt pelt:only telt. From his own persons' knowledge; be knew Mr Guest tuba the very ideal oft real C iristian gentleman. Their hearts went out in sympathy to the sotrowlngrelritives who had been so suddenly bereaved. 'TOWN COUNCIL TRIM.' rE. ' Ate the meeting of the Town Council oq TnesdaY. • evening, the Mayor alluded to the late Mr. GROSE, who was -a member of the Council from 1871 4.t0 1832. !He was one of the of the oldest borough ustices, end Vail highly respected by all who knew him. He had licitown him ever since be had been in Warrington, and 'he could say that, he,was one of the kindest and roost courteous gentleman he had ever met. He . could sum him up by Saying that he was a thorcingh Christian gentleman ; and; while they might have diff :red from him in some things, they could not have otner than the most friendly and respectful feelings towards him. TIAN hear) He moved a resolution of condolence th thelamily in their sad bereavement. • . _Councillor ROBERTS seconded, and said he referred to tlie late Mr. Guest at the meeting ,of the Education Ciiminitte4 held on the previous evening, so that he 'ihed not say much about him now, especially as the shimmery the Mayor bad just given of Mr. Guest was as aPt and accurate as it could possibly be. (Hear., bear).— The resolution was carried in silence, all the members standing.</p>
        <p>TRIBUTES AT THE EDUCATION COMMITTEE At the meeting of the Edit Cation Committee on Mon- day evening the CHAIR.M AN Couucillor H Roberts) said they had suddenly lost a prominent and worthy citizen who was formerly a member of that body—Mr E J Guest. To him (the speaker) his death had come as a great personal sorraw, so unexpected and altogether sad had it been. Fur some years Mr Guest was associated particularly with the school at Silver-street as its correspondent and one of its managers and he was for a ehort period a member of that committee also. rhere were some who had been admitted during many years to the inner circle of his personal 'friendship, to whom his death came as a very sad blow. He (the speaker) could say, without any hesitation or reservation, that he had never known a man with higher id-tals as to public and personal service and private conduct, or a matt who tried to live up to those ideals more justly and thoroughly than his friend Mr Guest. Warrington had been singularly deprived during the last few months of the services of many distinguished citizens and among them, for purity of life and conduct and for a deaire to do all that lay in his powerlo serve the best and highest interests of his fellow men, the name of Edwin Guest would have an honoured place. Coun- cillor Itonerts concluded by moving a vote of condolence with the family. Mr A J JOLLEY seconded. Alderman BENNE rt said Mr Guest wax in every sense a odel citizen an-i an example of the nignest probity and rectitude. The vote wO3 passed in silence, the members standing. A METHODIST TRIBUTE. The Rev. F. W. Kernick writes in the" Methodist Recorder " : It will be with the keenest sense of loss that many ()flour readers will learn of the sudden d.,ath of Mr. Edwin Guest, late of Warrington and Colwyn Bay. Very much could be said of his long and valued services as a local preacher, of his unwearying hospitality, of his wide and generous sympathy with all aggressive work. As a citizen of Warrington he held a unique place in the kffection of all who sought the highest welfare of the town. He brought to the business life the principles of the highest type of English gentleman ; from his high code of honour he was never known to swerve, and those who dealt with him in buisness found the same courtesy and kindness which he exhibited in private life. Such an example was perhaps the greatest con- tribution he made to the Church. Modest and retiring, he knew when and how to make an absolute stand for principle. He always conferred benefits as if he were receiving them. Educated at St. John's College, Cambridne, he remained through life true to the best type of University life. He was especially the valued friend of the ministers, and most of all of the younger men- ThoSe who were privileged to know Mm,will cherish the memory of one who stood for all thkt tyas just and high-minded. Reverently we think ot those from whose home &amp;Ante been Ai suddenly' withdrawn, and Pray that the Divine consolation and peace may be theirs; '</p>
        <p>„</p>
      </div>
