      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>. MR. lt.H. CROSFIELD – A I ' ` 't • 71‘I, IMPORTANT IrNd6Rll' E4t" '" NT POLITICS.</head>
        <p>. MR. lt.H. CROSFIELD - A I ' ` 't • 71‘I, IMPORTANT IrNd6Rll' E4t" '" NT POLITICS.</p>
        <p>THE DANGERS OF PROTECTION AND CONSCRIPTION.</p>
        <p>As briefly reported ins our last issue, Mr. A. H. Crosfield 'delivered an important speech on Friday of last week at the distribution of prizes in connection with the recent bowling main for the Liberal workers in the various wards.— l'he proceedings took the form of a highly successful smoking concert, presided over by Councillor John Wilkinson. Mr. A. H. CROSFIELD, who was greeted with cheers, prefaced his remarks on the political situation with a reference to the delightful musical programme which had been submitted. He said the renowned ancestors of his wife—and it might be interes'ing to the assembly to learn that her mother's maiden name was Homer :aid it down as an axiom that they could not base education on anything better than athletics and music. "Well, we are in line with them this evening" com- mented Mr. Crosfield, "for we are combining politics and music. Everybody in Warrington must have been impressed with the wonderful progress that musical culture has made in our own, time—in fact I regard it as one of the most encouraging signs of these day s —(hear, hear)—and we are accustomed to really splendid musical treats, as I have reason to know, because I have heard so many of them in the last five and twenty years. But I do think that we are excep- tionally fortunate to-night, for every single item has been a gem." (Applause.) Having expressed the satisfaction which it tiff irded him to stand f ice to face with the stalwarts of the Liberal Party in Warrington, Mr. Crosfield said that from a lad he had been brought up as a Liberal, and be dare say tAAtiLY TRADITIONS - had often a good deal of influence on Folitical develep- meat. At any rate, he felt -an umnixnd Dad deep satisfaction in having the honour to assist, in a small way, in carrying on the same kind of progressive work which, in earlier day. and 'under different'con- ditions, his father and grandfather fought -for. (Loud applause ) " Whatever ether people may choose' to do," declared Mr. Crosfield, "1 for my part am not ashamed to say that that weighs With me very deeply and strongly. (Applause) Stich traditions add• to the pride one feels in being a member' of a party which not only sees and realises the complex problems of modern times, hut gets to•close grips with tfiem-,-- (cheers) —and advances towards a solution, thanks to 'the real, practical, patriotic and most blessed work which it has done for the nation." (Loud cheers.) Proceeding, Mr Crosfield- said that an additional incentive to be up and doiiig in the great and good work of progressive Libenlistri wee to. be found in the. fine example set by men who, in point of ability, industry and energy, were second 'to no other men in the kingdom— he alluded to the 'splendid ,band of leaders at the head of the Progressive party to day (Loud cheers.) The Cabinet were not 'sparing them- selves,,Jmt were working unremittingly, morning, noon and night, as no Ministers pf this country bad ever worked before them.. (Applau;e.) Was it not I worth While to give men of that stamp a fine and 'resolute hacking? '(Applatue.). To uching upon the strain involved by six succeasive Autumn Sessions o Parliament, Mr Crosfield 'pointed out that in the past inucla'of that extra Work 'had been wasted, but they now knew that the legislative efforts of thy) House of, Commons would no longer be thrown away.(Applause) The outcome of the Lords' destructive policy was that arrears of work had been piled up, leaving, seveial mea-ures of first importance to be grappled with in a• 'comparatively short period. People of a reactionary disposition were saying, " How different to the charm- ing times when Mr Bmfour promised his followers in the Rouse of Commons that they should not 'be burdened viith ' over-much legislation or work! " (Laughter.) Mr Balfour gave them the promise, and. he kept it—(laughter)—and be (Mr Crosfield) did not know that they as Liberals had any particular reason to quarrel with Mr Balfour on that score, judging from the samples of work they bad bad from the Tory 'arty. (Hear, bear). But if Mr. Balfour kept his promises it did not follow that the Tory Party kept theirs. He had a pretty good recollection of the election of .IBQS, which was tvon-;—to a very-large extent at any iatti—on the premise of Old Age Pensions (Hear hear, and a vice. "They never gave them though").' Exactly, but they had plenty of opportunity to give them. They had ten years of power. after that election, but they never faced the problem which they had previously declared was so simple that one had only to go round the corner and the first Tory election agent one met would explain the whole thing. (Laughter). Then ' there was the election of 1900, which they would not forget in a hurry. (Hear, hear). The electors were told tliat it was a bad plan to swop horses while cros•-ing a stream ; that the country was in the midst of a big war, and that it was only right that the Tories, who had got the job in hand, should finish it. And they were assured that their votes were wanted, not for the carrying of controvertial measures, but to finish the war. (Hear, bear). And yet no sooner were the Tories returned to power on the war issue than they introduced the Education Bill and the Licensing Bill—two of the most controversial and extreme party mea-ures imaginable. (Hear, hear). So it really came to this : What the Tories promised they would do, they didn't do; what they promised not to do, they promptly went and did: (Laughter and hear, hear). And these were the people who, with their usual effrontery, professed such virtuous indignation because the Government sprung upon a startled and unsuspecting electorate—(laughter)—a policy which in its essentials had been considered by the c,untry for only the last sir and twenty years. (Laughter and Hear, hear.) Mr. Crosfield next alluded to the 'fact that during the few years the Liberal Party were in office- from 1892 to 1895, they were not really in power. The Lords had by that time practically formed themselves into a Committee of the Carlton Club, and for Liberals it had simply become a case of PLoUGIIING THE SANDS. With this' brief interval the Tories ware both in office and in power fJr twenty years. and he would ask his hearers to drive home in the constituency the kind of record they had to show for it,—a record of legisla- tion in the interests of the classes and sects and privileged groups at the expense of the general body of the people. (Hear, hear). To cap it all they had the raging. tearing propaganda started in 1903, and had it succeeded Pr, tection would have been infinitely the worst and most serious example of the kind of legislation he had referred to, Protection had a proverbially demoralising influence where it took root, and it would seem that the mere effort "to introduce ,it had a baneful influence of the same kind,. At any rate it seemed significant that the opening of the Protectionist campaign coincided with the introduc- tion of the system of quasi-slavery—forced Chinese labour—in South Africa - a tolerably sordid foundation one would have thought, on which to found a British Imperial policy in a new colony. It was also per- fectly well known in the House of Commons that, once they had got the yoke of Protection firmly settled on the shoulders of the people, the great bulk of the Tory Party in the House were ready to go in for that insidious, dangerous and, in this country., UNNECESSARY AND ILL-ADVISED POLICY OF CONSCRIPTION. (Hear, hear). There was an old saying "Forewarned is forearmed" and he did not make that statement without having made enquiries when he was in the House of Commons. He gave that as another illustration of the kind of reaction and bad influence that seemed to dog the footsteps of Protec- tion. Only the other day they had men who claimed to represent traditions of good breeding in England deliberately considering whether to reduce the whole I House of Commons to a bear garden and 'shout down every speaker opposed to them. And now they actually had the leader of the Tory Party indulging in language which was deliberate encouragement to the law-breakers—not of Ulster but of the little Orange faction in the North-East corner of Ulster—language which was a perfect disgrace to him. (Applause. They had not long to wait for the fruits of such tactics In Belfast to-day there were some hundreds of capable skilled workmen, engineers and shipwrights—some of them Protestants and some of them Roman Catholics— and ought it to make any difference which they 'ere? —(" No " and cheers) —who were out of employment. ("Shame.") Many of them dare not go back to work in that one Orange centre. And yet some of the Orange- men had the impudence to claim that Belfast was the only place in Ireland where opinion and speech were free. The whole thing was a striking illustration of the demoralisation of the Tory Tarty, What a come- down; what a disgrace for the Party which used to claim that it stood for the Constitution and for law and 'order. They bad got to drop that claim now, for they had been making it clear that to gain their own ends they were willing to throw law and order, even</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>, . I the Constitution heel; to, flail winds. If tbey were going to try-7and. the For 4 'ite:doetiune that a'veiininall,minority was to have right to control an enormous majority, all he could say was that it meant the end of Constitutional, government—(applause—in fact,rif they succeeded to would nearly be the end of civilised government. (Applauss.) Ho ever, there was a limit to the amount of nonsense lvhich the Government would stand—(hear, hear—and Orange law-breakers and those who encouraged them had better mind what they were about. Compared with these disgraceful proceedings, and such a thoroughly rnedifyiug record, what a. contrast and what a relief to turn to the work and record of the Liberal government since it came into power in 1906. First they had what Sir Wilfred Laurier called "that shining example to the world,, Free Trade vindicated rind defended." (Applause.) Then came that splendid work of Sir Henry Campbell Banner- man in South Africa—(applause)—when he brought the blessed healing influence of self-government to bear upon a country which had so shortly before suffered the prolonged agony of a most terrible war. His policy was crowned with a success which had impressed the whole civilised world as an example— never to be forgotten—of what genuine Liberal foreign policy could do. (Applause.) Mr Crosfield laid stress on the Trades Boards Act which laid down the prin- ciple of the.miniruum wage, a principle which was destined in more directions than one to be rightly and considerably extended. (Hear, hear.) That was a question just as important for women as for men—of literally vital importance, in too many cases, for both and the operations of the Act so far as it went were being carefully watched in many a country on the Continent which was looking to England's lead to solve some of the worse problems in regard to sweated and underpaid labour which had disgraced the industrial world of Europe' and America. (Hear, hear). Having alluded to the Government's work on behalf of the children and the ,aged, 11r. Crosfield also claimed that they had done justice to National Defence in respect of every legitimate demand that could be made. Not only had they done that, but they bad paid their way. (Applause.) They were not , leaving undischarged burdens to be borne by posterity through the system of borrowing, which the Tories seemed to fancy, nor had they added to the burdens of the people who could, least afford to .bear them. (Applause.) They had also paid off a great deal of the National Debt. (Applause.) In a final summing up of the Government's work, Mr. Crosfield said that looking at it from ,the, standpoint of sound finance or commerce-,-which 'Counted- for so notch to a com- munity like Warrington -or of the eimplY•invaluable political and social i reforms in :which they were engaged, he submitted that the Government was worthy of their continuous and strenuous support. (Loud cheers).</p>
      </div>
