      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>WARRINGTON READING CIRCLE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON READING CIRCLE.</p>
        <p>" Social Conditions in Provincial Towns."</p>
        <p>Tile newly-formed Reading Circle met for the first time on Monday last at the daces of the Guild of Help, when Mr. Rowland read a paper on " Social Con- ditions in Provincial Towns. Portsmouth, Liverpool, Worcester and Cambridge were the places under dis- cussion.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. RowLAND pointed out that often one special factor determined the bent of ;tt town's character, and gave it some special problem. Thus Portsmouth, the naval port and arsenal, depended on a Government programme. It could not as a centre of national defence carry on a trade like Southampton. Liver- pool's chief difficulty was the prevalence of casual labour in connection with the work. Worcester suffered from the distribution of charitable doles, and Cam- bridge from the alternations between term time and vacation. Warringtonians who studied the difficulties and triumphs of other towns could not fail to benefiet. Thb knowledge of others might prove the shortest route to self-knowledge. Much might be learnt from the pioneering work of other towns often originating in some private venture as Btc. baths and wssh-houses from the efforts of a humble woman in Liverpool. Cambridge had taken a lead in the provision of school nurses, dental clenics and lady health visitors. Liverpool had been a pioneer in the supply of sterilized milk and in the voluntary notification of consumption. The benevolent side of police work bad also been developed on excellent lines. The most noteworthy of recent social movements in Liverpool bad been the establishment of a Council of voluntary aid, which was co-ordinating all kinds of philanthropic work. It was subsidized from the rates to the extent of .£6OO per Eytnum. The experience of t'aese and other towns might be used for the benefit of each. A very useful discussion followed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>EARLESTOWN CO-OPERATIVES AND INSURANCE.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN CO-OPERATIVES AND INSURANCE.</p>
        <p>in the House of Commons on Monday, Viscount Wolmer asked the Chancellor of the Exchequer whether the Insurance Commissioners had now received an application from the Earl•stown Industrial Co-opera- tive Society, under sub-section (7) of section 47 of the National Insurance Act, to enable them and their employees, to whom they had guaranteed six weeks' full wages during sickness, to avail themselves of the reduced contritutions provided for under the Act, and whether be could ape his way to grant the application, since it was demanded by both parties. Mr. Mastermnn : An application was received from this society under sub-section 7of section 47 of the National Insurance Act-on the 17th inst. The Commis- sioners communicated 4ith the rociety on the 18th inst to ascertain the views of the employers as required by the sub-section, and when the replies have been received they will at once consider the propriety of issuing a special order.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>MAYORAL TREAT FOR ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>MAYORAL TREAT FOR ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Sir Joseph Beecham, before ending his third year of office as Mayor of St. Helens on November 9, Vern give the townspeople yet another musical tteitt. On November 7, at the Town Hall, the Beecham Orchestfa will give an up-to-date programme of music, and the Ma}or will invite as many townspeople as can be com- pressed into the hall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, October 26, 1912.</p>
        <p>hue P.S.A. Society. There was a very good attendance at this meeting on Sunday, presided over by Mr Farrar. The Rev T T James, M.A., of Sheffield, gave an address upon "Stop the gap." He said he had been reading a most interest- ing book, in which the words to the memory of a good man were "He stopped the gap." That was a most sugrestive and a most desirable attitude to be in— willing for any service to the Master. Work was wait- ing to be done, and needed men to do it. The men in the Brotherhood needed looking after ; men outside wanted someone to speak to them words of sympathy and cheer. Christ wanted willing workers. There were many gaps waiting to he filled up. Mr Payton gave an organ solo in masterly style. Mr McEachern and Mr Healey took part in the meeting. One new member received a very hearty welcome. The meeting was very encouraging and closed with the rousing hymn "I will Alow anywhere."</p>
