      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>WARRINGTON WOMAN'S</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON WOMAN'S</p>
        <p>BACKACHE AND DROPSY</p>
        <p>Mrs. H. Daley, of I, Booth-street, Liverpool- road. Warrington, says :—" My kidney trouble started with severe pains in my back, which were so bad that I was obliged to walk about with my back bent. After a time dropsy set in, my body began to swell all over. The legs swelled the most, but there was also swellings under my eyes. " I had bad headaches, and was subject to nasty dizzy bouts. The pains in my back and loins seemed to wear me out. I got into a nervous, depressed condition, and quite lost my appetite.</p>
        <p>"I had been a martyr to these troubles for some years before I tried Doan's backache kidney pills, but I bad not taken many doses of the medicine before I could tell that it was doing me good. The swellings gradually grew less, and at last disappeared altogether; and my back became easier as I kept on with the pills.</p>
        <p>"By the time I had used three boxes I was quite cured. I don't know what I should have done without Doan's pills, for I had tried lots of things before I used them without finding any ease. (Signed) Hannah Daley." OVER FIVE YEARS LATER, Mrs. Daley said :—"I have not had any return of the old trouble beyond a twinge of backache now and again, and then I have taken a dose of Doan's pule, which have always removed the pain."</p>
        <p>1 About 500 GRAINS OF POISONOUS WASTE and 3 PINTS OF WATER are filtered out of the blood by the kidneys every 24 hours. That is, when the kidneys are active— When they are weakened by a chill, overwork, worry or any other cause, they leave some of this poisonous waste and surplus water in the blood— Then you are likely to become dropsical or rheumatic; you may get pains in the loins and back, twinges of i sciatica, or the sharp, stabbing pains of lumbago. The water becomes cloudy or gravelly, and leaves the urinary channels burning and sore. You may be obliged to get up three or four times in the night. 1</p>
        <p>Doan's Backache Kidney Pills help the kidneys to throw off the poisonous kidney waste and surplus water, and are therefore of great value in dropsy, back- ache, gravel, rheumatism, lumbago, and sciatica, as well as in disorders of the urinary system. This remedy is solely for the kidneys and bladder—it is not intended for constipation or indigestion, but only for disorders arising from weakness of the kidneys and urinary sy stem.</p>
        <p>In one sized package only-219 a bar, or 6 boxes 1319; NEVER SOLD LOOSE. Of all chemists and stores, or from Foster-McCle!lan Co., 8, Wells-st., Oxford-At., London, W. Don't ask for Backache or Kidney PHlL—ask for DOAN'S Backache Kidney Pills. and MAKE SURE the name DOAN'S Is on the box you buy.</p>
        <p>"MAN op THE LAW" IN TROUBLE.—Horace Fletcher, 32, Cheetham Hill-road, Manchester, was summoned for failing to deliver up his railway ticket when requested at Central Station on September 22nd and also with impeding one of the Company's servants in the execution of his duty on the same date. The case against the defendant (who did not appear) as out- lined by Mr Pinfold for the C LC, was that on the arrivel of the 10.2 p.m. train at Warrington the collector saw Fletcher, who although he had a ticket, would do nothing more than show it without giving the collector the opportunity of examining it. After the collector had expostulated with him and the station inspector had been called he still refused to hand over the ticket for inspection. The defendant became exceedingly rude and remarked that he knew more of the law than all the station staff put together. In consequence of Fletcher's behaviour the train was six minutes feta in Manchester, and this might have seriously inconveni- enced passengers. Defendant had said the train was overcrowded but in evidence it was proved that the train was only reasonably laden. A porter gave evidence and said the defendant had said" lam a man of the law and believe in having my rights." There were only six passengers in the defendants' carriage. Fletcher was fined 40e including costs, or a month's imprisonment in each case. ANOTHER WINDOW SatAsTiErt.—Archibald Robinson, 71, Dallam-lane, for breaking two panes of glass at the above dwelling-house was fined 20s and costs. Mary Robinson estimated the damage at 53. From the evidence it appeared that the prisoner threw a lib weight through the parlour window and a 21b weight through the bedroom window. The latter struck an inmate of the house on the cheek severely cutting it. THEFT OF RAZORS AND BOOTS.—James Savage, 3, Chorley-street, was charged on remand with having stolen two razors on the 11th OctJber from 33, Green- street, the property of Charles Lee. He was also charged with stealing two pairs of boots value 155 sd. from 71, Buttermarket-street, the property of Louis Fleming. The Chief Constable said that about one o'clock on October 11th the prisoner went to Mr Lee's shop and offered Mr Lee a pawn ticket relating to a pair of boots for sixpence. Mr Lee declined to purchase the ticket and on the prisoner leaving the establish- ment the two razors were missed. Mr Lee gave chase and found his property in the prisoner's inside coat pocket. Inquiries were made and it was ascertained that the prisoner had also stolen the hoots from Mr Fleming's shop.—Prisoner who said he did not remem- ber anything as be had been drinking all week was sent to prison for 14 day's on each charge. YOUNG GIRLS ASSAULTED.—John Mitchell, Corpora- tion labourer, 5, Little Weiot, was charged with having assaulted two little girls in the Central Picture Palace on Saturday evening.—Mitcbgl pleaded guilty.—The Chief Constable stated that the defendant was at the Picture Palace on Saturday evening and in his immedi- ate vicinity there was a little girl aged 12 with two other children aged 5 and 8. Defendant assaulted two of the children. The defendant had said he had been drinking and be (the chief) had every reason to believe that was a truthful explanation of his conduct. The defendant had been employed off and on by the Highways Department for 25 years and it was clearly a case where he had had too much drink. To allow children to go to picture palaces alone was not very satisfactory.—Mitchell was fined 20s. including costs. WEDNESDAY—Before Mr J W Hayward (in the chair), Councillor C Jackson Holmes and Mr W T Williams.</p>
        <p>EX-CONSTABLE IN THE DOCK.—William Thomas Smith,a stranger, was charred with stealing on or about October 22nd a gent's bicycle the property of some person unknown.—The Chief Constable stated that the previous afternoon the prisoner went to Mr Dendy, a cycle dealer, 73, Knutsford-road and asked him for a tyre. He also asked if Mr Deudy would purchase the machine and said he had been discharged from service, and had no use for it. He asked £2 10s for it at first but Mr Dendy became snspicious and thought he would see what was the lowest price prisoner would take for it. He bartered with him and the price got down to 13s. Mr Dendy was more suspicious and was coming down to the Police Station, when he met Detective Sergeant Reddington. Prisoner said he had purchased the bicycle at Preston three months ego, but did not know from whom. He had made numerous varying statements In regard to it. He had also stated that he had been discharged from a constabulary not far away. 'They had every reason to believe that the man would be wanted by the Crewe police.—A remand for a week was granted. MORE STREET BETTING.—Edmund Victor Suthers, 12, Clegg-street, was charged with having loitered in a public street for the purpose of betting. Prisoner pleaded guilty. P.C. Skelton proved the case and stated that at noon the previous day he was in Back Melville-street when he saw three men hand slips to the prisoner. Another also handed him a slip and the prisoner had a long strip of paper in his possession. The man when taxed with the offence commenced to struggle but when witness got the strip of paper out of the prisoner's hand, be said "All right I will come</p>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>DEBATE ON HOUSE OF LORDS</p>
        <p>REFORM.</p>
        <p>Government's Narrow Escape.</p>
        <p>The adjourned discussion on Thursday evening, of the Tory Government's measure for the reform of the House of Lords proved exceptionally interesting—so much so that the House apparently forgot that such a thing as a time limit existed, and allowed the debat3 to he pro- longed until close upon ten o'clock. On a division being taken, the Government narrowly escaped disaster, the Bill being approved by 24 to 23. The debate was re-opened by Mr L WARBURTON (C., W Bristol), who said they must have some power above the House of Commons which would drive that assembly into a reasonable frame of mind. (Laughter). Mr P O'Bittax (S St Pancras) said the members of the Tory Party were always on the look-out for a" last ditch" or a fort to which to retreat when the con- stituencies were against them and from that security to destroy the efforts of the Liberal party in the House of Commons. The present Bill was constructed to cor- sohdate and perpetuate the strength of the Conserva- tive party in the House of Commons. It allowed the representatives of the people to select only a small proportion of members of the Upper House. rf the Prime Minister had suggested that the whole of that House should he elected from the counties he (the sneaker) would have supported him, but he was absolutely against the constitution of the Bill as sub- mitted. Members of the Tory party were fond of speaking of the great work done by representatives of the noble houses, but they ignored the handsome pay- ment they had received. The condition of the House of Lords and of the country in the past was such that very few men had the opportunity of entering the public service but the sons, sons-in-law and other mern- bets of aristocratic honses. It ill-became the Tories to throw cheap sneers at the officials who had recently been appointed to look after the interests of democracy. If there was to he an Upper House at all, it should be responsible directly to the constituencies.</p>
        <p>Mr W WATSON (§.. E. Birmingham) said the Govern- ment's idea of a referendum Was that measures with which the Lords did not sews should be referred to the people. The Socialists idea was that everything should be referred to the people, and the Lords referred back to their own homes—and stay there, as far as legisla- tion Was concerned. (Laughter.) The Douse of Lords was supposed to check hasty legislation. It could not touch finance or foreign affairs. What else mattered? Only the economic interests of the working people—and the Lords were there solely and always to prevent the people ever getting on, and to delay legisla- tion. The Bill gave power to one class alone. Mr MCDERMID (C., Paisley) : It does not destroy the House of Commons. Mr WATSON said that in the whole course of its history the House of Lords had done nothing to prove that it was of any use to the people. The Rev E C E CARLETON (C, Truro),said the Govern- ment wanted to protect the people in their rights and to secure that they should have a voice in the govern- ment of the country. Under the Parliament Act they bad no voice at all. (Laughter.) The only right they bad was to elect a Parliament once in five years. The members of the Government might be sheer, unprin- cipled despote, but all the people could do was to sit still and wait five years, in the hope that something would turn up and that they would have a change. Formerly the Lords were able to insist that the people should be consulted when the importance of the occasion demanded it. The value of that security had been made obvious by recent events. Look how helpless the country bad been under the Insurance Act! (Laughter.) That measure did not raise much enthusiasm. (A VOICE: "Wait and see.") Dare they hold a meeting in support of it in Warring- ton ? (" Yes.") The Socialist candidate for the Newton Division said it was an unclean thing and similar statements were made at the Trade Unions Congress. In order to pass the Parliament Act the Liberals did not scruple to make demands from the Sovereign and exact pledges from him which were in direct violation of the pledge given by the King to the people at the Coronation, in the sight of God—and that only three months after the Coronation had taken place. Mr KILDUFF (N., Dublin Harbour) : Is it in order to speak against the King in this respect ? Tne Rev EC E CattueroN : lam reciting historical facts. There is a very serious religious side to this question. What wore they to think, he asked, of a Minister who approached his Sovereign to tell himn that his oath safeguarding tbe liberties of the people was a meaningless formality? It was a profanation of religion and added to the culpability of the Minister. Mr R OSBORNE (L., Wellington) maintained tint the Upper House should be composed of businessmen. The Bill was a reaction to the state of things which prevailed before the Parliament Act, The nomination of representatives by the County Councils was said to be direct representation of the people, but his experience was that the election of a County Councillor was looked upon more coolly than the election of a Parish Councillor. If " one man, one vote" was good enough for a referendum, it should also be good enough for a general election. Was it natural to assume that the rights of the people would be protected by a body which had not to seek election? Mr S SIDDELEY (C., Blackpool) said he had been struck with the transparent insincerity of the Opposi- tion speakers. (Laughter.) When one heard their arguments, one wondered bow it was that the two patties were at loggerheads at all. (Socialist "Hear, hears.") The Liberals agreed that they ought to have two effective Chambers and that the will of the people should be the final tribunal to which anyone could appeal. Referring to the delay in carrying out the preamble of the Parliament Act, the speaker said it had become necessary to reform our ethical category. We should in future have to speak not merely of honourable and dishonourable men, but of •` honourable men, if time permits." (Laughter.) It was argued that the House of Lords had been permanently in opposition to the will of the people, yet Lord Haldane had said "when- ever the nation has been in earnest with a measure the House of Lords has, in the end, been no permanent obstacle." 'Mr O'Brien : In the end ! (Laughter.) Mr J lisurtY (Socialist, Colne Valley, expressed surprise that any body of intellectual men should try to reform an institution so dilapidated and in such a decaying condition as the House of Lords. Each section of the House admitted that the institution was bad, so why not abolish it altogether instead of trying to patch it up?</p>
        <p>- CotinCfflor HAWTHORN (L., Montrose) said they had bad single Cha.nber government whenever a Conserva- tive Ministry had been in power. He believed that in the whole history of Parliament there was only one instance in which the Lords endeavoured to interfere with a Bill sent up by a Liberal Government. The Liberals asked for the same privileges when they were in office, as were extended to their opponents. He failed to see where the Rev E C E Carleton's grievance came in, for the King, as an honourable man, had only stood by his guarantee to create Peers if the country decided in favour of the Parliament Bill. The present measure perpetuated the hereditary principle and, in a modified way, the Tory control of the House of Lords. The speaker quoted a long list of progressive measures which the House of Lords had obstructed. The Rev E C E CARLEToN t HOW did all these measures get on the statute book ? Councillor HAWTHORN: Because of the will of the people prevailing. The Rev EC E CARLEroN : Wasn't that sufficient ? Mr O'BRIEN : It takes too long for them to get there._</p>
        <p>J hicDrumtD (C., Paisley) pointed out that the House of Lords had already passed resolutions embody- ing, in effect, the provision of the present Bill, which was based on" trusting the people." The Tories did not object to the Irish vote, but they objected to an organized minority ruling a mnjority. (Laughter and a VOICE : " Ulst-r for instance.) THE LP:ADER OP THE OePosrrioN (Mr J Jarman) asked how it was that the Tories had introduced the Bill if, as they contended, the House of Lords had always been right and had never done the evil things imputed to it ? If there was no injustice, there was no necessity to alter the constitution of the House. The chief objection to the referendum was that they could not get the people to vote on the single issue before them. The PRIME MINIsTER (Mr E A White), in replying to the debate, said the first Chamber in a democratic country was the most important and had the greatest power, but the Conservatives did not consider thdt that Chamber should be uncontrolled. On a division being taken, the Bill was passed by 24 votes to 23. There will be no meeting of the society next week, as the room is required for election purposes. The following Thursday the Government will introduce their Insurancel3ill.</p>
        <p>Bengal Lights Cause Fire. A fire, which is supposed to have been caused by boys discharging Bengal lights, broke out on Saturday afternoon in some stables in Napier-street, belonging to Mr. John Berry, milk dealer, Altrincham. Superin- tendent Turner and other members of the Fire Brigade at once turned out with the engine Captain and found the stables burning fiercely. A good supply of water was available and the flames were very soon extinguished, but not be!ore some damage had been done.</p>
