      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>SATUTIDAY.—Bofore Mr J W Hayward (in the chair). Alderman Tinnion, and Messrs A .1 Jolley and T Pemberton. " THEFT OF TrtorsEßs.—Robert Gavin, of no settled abode, who had a number of previous convictions, was sentenced to three months' imprisonment for having stolen on October 10th a pair of trousers, value 75., the property of William Barrick, from 14, Stanley-street. MONDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Alderman Smethurst, Councillor Wilkinson, Messrs S Wright, W Roberts, W Furcese, T Locker and G Pilling. STREET BETITNG.—MiCheeI Mitchell, of Lower Ship-yard, was charged with loitering for the purpose of betting in Bridge-street on Saturday. The prisoner pleaded guilty.—Detective-Sergeant Holland said that in consequence of complaints received as to betting in Bridge-street, he kept observation in the street. From 12 45 to 1.15 on the day in queation he saw the prisoner .enter the Lion Vaults four times and then went away in the direction of Friars Green. He then came to the corner of Ship-yard and he, the officer, saw a man give prisoner something which he placed in his vest pocket. He arrested the prisoner, and on searching him found a betting slip relating to six horses running at Alexandra Park on Saturday, and wrapped up in the paper was 7s 6d in cash, lie also found another slip relating to horses running at Newmarket on Friday, and is the slip was also some money. In answer to the ziaarge charge prisoner replied. It is quite right, sir. I have not been doing it long." Be bad also said he had been taking them across the green to Sis. The Mayor: Who is Sis ? Sin Mather, Sir !—Mitchell was fined £5 and costs, £5 7s in all. BROTHERS' EXTENSIVE THEFTS.—Two brothers, Charles and Francis Kent, of 14, Winifred-street. were charged on remand with steeling on October 12th, a pair of slippers value 2s 9id the property of Jos. Frisby, from a stall in the Market. They were also -charged with having stolen, during the past twelve/ months from stalls in the market, articles value £1 Ils 9d. the property of John Himsworth and others. Charles Kent was also charged with having stolen, during the past twelve months, a quantity of articles, value £2 95 64 from the Cheshire Lines Railway Company. Mr Steel appeared for the prisoners, who pleaded guilty. The Chief Constable said that on the 12th October, the two prisoners were seen at the boot stall of Mr Frisby in the market. They were examining slippers, and later one of them Was seen walking away after placing a pair of slippers under his coat. Mr Frisby went to him and got the slippers from him. The prisoner was brought to the Police Station, where he admitted the theft. It appeared the younger prisoner, Francis, required a pair of boots, and his brother said he would get him a pair. 'The younger covered the action of the elder one. Inquiries were made, and on searching the house of the prisoners a large number of articles were found. Prisoners were taxed with the theft of the articles and admitted having stolen them from the Market. Charles Kent also admitted pilfer. log from the Railway Companies premises. —Mr. Steel said Charles Kent was a married man with four children, and foerteeo years ago came with good testimonials to the Midland Railway Company, by whom he had since been employed. One wondered for what earthly reason a man in a permanent situation should take the paltry articles. The defendant had said it seemed to be a mania with him, and perhaps if the man was in a better position in life it might be klepto- mania. He had always been of a saving disposition He hoped they would not commit the prisoner to gaol. He thought a thrifty saving nature was at the bottom of it all.—The Chief Constable said the younger man had been in prison for stealing a bicycle.—Bach of the prisoners was sent to gaol for one month. TUESDAY—Before Mr J Rymer Young (in the chair), and Councillor Jackson Holmes, A SERIOUS OFFENCE.—Ernest Wright, 32, Aikin- street, was charged with having obstructed P.O Nicholls in the execution of his duty on Saturday.—The officer said he was called to a house in Allan-street, where a woman was afraid her husband would do her some injury. He was compelled to arrest the man, and Wright commenced to interfere. As Wright was drunk he requested him to go away, but he continued to inter- fere, and allowed the man to escape into his house. He had also incited the crowd against the police. Four officers had to be called before the conclusion of the affair.—A fine of 108 and costs was imposed or in default one month's imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>St. John's Presbyterian Church. Harvest Thanksgiving Services.</head>
        <p>St. John's Presbyterian Church. Harvest Thanksgiving Services.</p>
        <p>The annual harvest services were held at St. John's on Sunday, the preacher morning and evening being the Rev. William Reid. There were good attendances at each service, in the evening the church being quite full, when Mr. Reid chose for his subject the parable of the sower—(St. Matthew xiii., 18.) The men's hearts were hardened by the inevitable routine of life, which pressed hardly on them all, the christian minister as much as any. But if that hardening routine was necessary— forced upon them, let them strive to keep it to the external things of life, furthest removed from the vitality of their lives and hearts which should be as pure and free from hardness as possible. The Father's remedy for the hard ground was the plough which went deep, but when God set to work with this plough it was in mercy, not in judgment. There was one kind of soil only from which a harvest could be realised, and it was that which was not hard, thin, or crowded with weeds. It was the good soil. The harvest of God had to be realised in the way the Master bad set before them in the parable from the I good, clean soil. If the soil is hard the plough will hive to go through it, it it is thin the deepening must take place, if it is crowded. no matter how valuable the interests are they must be forked out. By continual conflict, looking unto Jesus, striving in His name, was the only process whereby the thirty, sixty and a hundred-fold was to be realized, in God's good time, and in His way. The choir, under the leadership of Air. A. G. Lessey, sang the anthem On Lord, how manifold are thy works " (Hamby), and also gave a fine rendering of the gleaners chorus " Out in the golden corn-fields " by H. Ernest Nichol. Mrs. James Snailbam was the organist. The church wes prettily decorated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>Wycliffe Congregational Church.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe Congregational Church.</p>
        <p>The Rev T T James, M.A., of Sheffield, occupied the pulpit, morning and evening, at this church on Sunday and also spoke at the P.S.A. meeting in the afternoon. In a little address to the children he explained how we came to count by tens. Before our ancestors learned the industrial arts they had acquired the habit of counting by means of the fingers and toes. In an interesting way he showed that the good gifts which , God put into their lives could not be counted. Like the stars in the heavens and the sand by the sea-shore, God's gifts to his children could not be enumerated. The text of the morning discourse was" My soul wait thou only ..mon God, for my expectation ie from Him" (Psalm lxii. 5.) He showed that the problems of life which surrounded the Psalmist were still with us, and the solution of the Hebrew poet—" My soul wait thou only upon God"—was one which was still the best for us. Proceeding to show that the religious life was to be one of expectation, he said there was a kind of religious life, or what called itself such, that was not expectant and there was also a Rind of expectation which was placed anywhere except in the place where it could be of any avail. Dealing with the first of these two classes, he demonstrated how Christ's work itself was hindered by the lack of expectation or faith of those who sur- rounded Him, and said the only life that could achieve anything had about it the spirit of faith, confidence and hope. In regard to the second class he asked them to realise from whence power came. Apart from passionate belief in the presence and power of a living God there was no hope for the day to which we were looking forward. In the fdith of Cromwell's Ironsides —" The Lord of Hosts is with us "—they could build a social fabric that would stand the test of time and be permanent in its power. The subject of the evening discourse was the walk to Emmaus (Luke 21o.) He contended that the Christian life was a companionship with the living Lord. It was , more than a law and an ideal. There were laws and r ideals before Christ, but He became the personification of these things and so the Word of God became flesh. In the Christian fdith and in Christ's personality they had not simply an heroic leader who came into the world and passed away and is no more amongst us, but they had the living presence of a personal companion who joins Himself to us and makes the journey with us. The preacher instanced how John Morley recognised the personality of Christ in making the Christian faith a living force. In Christianity there was personal com- panionship with a living Lord, and the realization of that companionship was dependent upon our own choice Christ only came where He was wanted and our religious experience therefore depended upon our will. If they constrained Christ to enter into their lives it would revolutionise their conduct sod Vloughts.</p>
        <p>"How Stands It With Religion To-day?" • There was again a crowded congregation at Cairo- street Chapel on Sunday evening, when the Rev Dr Stanley Mellor preached the second of his course of sermons on "Religion and Democracy." His special subject was "How stands it with religion to-day ? " He said that religion in the world to-day was supposed generally to be represented by " the Church," a com- prehensive term which covered a not inconsiderable number of included organisations, differing sects and varying denominations. Reference was made to the di visions within the Church and the preacher said they were not the worst feature in the oase.Far moreserious, farmore difficult to deal with was an apparent inability to realize what genuine religion meant and implied, and a persistent refusal to face the facts of existence fairly and to note the gulf that lay between the ideal of religion and the actual condition of things. The serious thing with the churches to-day was that, with them and for them, religion tended more and evermore to ' become nothing but a mere form and pretence, a little ' thing, without depth and without breadth, content to serve merely parochial interests and to make loud protestations of " doing good" in ways wnich never touched the fundamental facts of life and never reached the central source of social wrong and social injustice. Instead of being the greatest thing in life, religion, as far as the organized churches were concerned, seemed veritably to be the smallest. The public preaching and profes- sion of religion added scarcely a single individual to the ranks of those who were in revolt against the whole existing order, and yet, if religion were properly under- stood and properly realized, the churches should be the greatest aud strongest centres of discontent and unrest that a society could possess. The fault lay partly with the clergy and ministers, partly with the laity of the Church. The members of the churches to-day, such few as there were left, were drawn mainly from the more comfortable classes, from the ranks of those who were naturally prompted by self-interest to beileve that the present order of things was good, and to maintain that order ; and, horrible to think of, they expected their ministers and clergy to encourage them in that belief and practice. They had no desire to be made uncomfortable. Were they not doing what they could—dispensing charity, showing kindness to the poor, supporting the cause of social reform, sending missionaries to the heathen ? That was their continual plea. They had little or no desire for enlightenment,little or no wish for genuine knowledge ; their conferences and congresses were,es a rule,eacked with"safe,"men,who could be trusted not to say anything violent or discom- forting or likely to offend the the tender susceptibili- ties of the more wealthy members. The man who, unable longer to contain himself, did, with boldness, grapple with the truth of the existing order, attack the system of social organization, proclaim the fundamental cruelty and injustice of our whole method of social living, such a man was viewed with suspicion and,sooner or biter, given the cold shoulder. Members of churches, in these days, as a rule, did not care for the preaching of revolt and the genuine gospel of discontent. Ministers and clergy were not without their share of the blame and the responsibility. " For myself " continued Dr Mellor, " I cannot con- ceive how any sincere minister of religion could, for long, pursue trig work without being faced by the most, painful facts of social existence and without being compelled to see not only that those facts are utterly antagonistic to religion, but also that that there may be and certainly ought to be a way pf escape from them. One duty of the minister of religion is to preach the love of God and the divine sonship of humanity. How can he fittingly discharge that duty in the midst of a society which tolerates poverty and lives by economic injustice and wrong. If be is sincere in his gospel, the logical outcome must surely be open and complete discontent. True it is that,where Churchmen gather together, as they have been doing recently in Middlesborough and Manchester, much talk just now goes forward concerning industrial unrest and the evils of our social state. But all such talk seems to proceed on the assumption that the present economic order of life is the foal one, that society must always be com- mercial and capitalistic, and that such is the right and proper state of things. We listen in vain for the word that shall deny, totally and absolutely,the justice of the present order. We listen in vain for the voice that shall declare against the possession of the materiel resources of life by the few. We wait apparently in vain, for the responsible Churchman who shall lead a</p>
        <p>new crusade against riches. We do not bear, even, the murmur of the Utopian visions and the whispering of dreams. All that we get is advice to the employer to pay a living wage, and advice to the worker to do a fair day's work, but these pieces of advice to which, by the way nobody listens, do not go within a thousand miles of the real root of the matter. The Church to-day still preaches contentment where she should be creating discontent, and urges acceptance instead of revolt. There is, in her midst, no vision and no great resolve." No church could hope tor continued existence which refused to take arms against the existing order, and that not half-heartedly and by the offering of palliatives and cures but while-heartedly by the undertaking of destruction and reneAal. From the pulpits of this land there should be thundered, Sunday by Sunday and week by week, stern denunciations of the injustice, the moral degrada- tion, the open wickedness of our present mode of living ; denunciation uttered in the name of true religion, born of passionate longing for God and good- ness, inspired by prophetic vision of the coming age of fellowship and justice and love.</p>
      </div>
