      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>Latchford Baptist Church. Annual Sale of Work.</head>
        <p>Latchford Baptist Church. Annual Sale of Work.</p>
        <p>Die annual s-a-l-e—oi;or—k-in-cc;ne:ti;;;Vith the Latch- f,n-cl Baptist Church, was bold in the schoolroom on Thursday. The scene was a gay and inviting one, for the various stalls superintended by willing helpers, bad been arranged in a very attractive manner. The opening ceremony was perforded by Mrs J A Rausome, of Hill Cliff°. There was a large attendance, over which the Rev John Yonge presided. He was supported by the Revs J S Cnedburn (pastor), H Ogden, W Reid, G Curtis, Mr W T Wilkinson and Mr 8 Al_binso n. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The Rev J SChedburn reads letter fromhismother tothe effect that she wished them joytul realisations of their highe at expectations. Proceeding, the Pastor said one of the many things he had to be grateful to his people for, was that they so studiously and considerately relieved him of all the worry incidental to the financial affairs of the church. However, if it were tot for the ladies and their willing consecrated activities, they would be in a bad way. They had no seat rents at their church ; their fiscal policy was established on a volun- tary basis, and their revenue consisted of free-will offerings. In that sale they were not begging, but bargaining on reasonable terms. He hoped that sale would be a means of binding them together In one common object—the extension of the Kingdom of Christ in that locality. (Applause). The Chairman who had watched the progress at Wash- lane for 35 years, said that in their pastor they had a man whom they could highly respect and value. (Hear, hear). It was a very happy thing to come to a church and bear of its prosperity, growth and peace. Those were very desirable characteristics of a church, but not always to be found. They were usually found at Wash. lane so far as he could remember. Dealing with the friendly relations existing between one denomination and another Mr. Yonge said whatever differences in faith and practices might apparently sever them, they were all one in heart and all working with the same object in view. Mrs Armitage had sent a donation of .C 1 is. through him as she could not he present. 1 Mrs. Ransome, in declaring the sale open addrtssed herself to the women and said it was time they took a greater share in the public events in the town of which they as well as the men were proud. Those who were privileged to be at the White Slave Prafic meeting the previous night must have realised what a great deal public feeling could do. She thought it was largely owing to women that that meeting had been called. (Applause.) They had a great power and she thought they ought to take a greater interest in things apper- taiinng to the well-being of the community and the government of the town. They bad to pay their rates and taxes and it would be better, if instead of grumbling they took more interest in the objects to which they were devoted. If.they did so when the time came for a representative to be sent to the Town Council they would see that be was the best men. They should also take an interest in the schooling of their children—get to know the teachers and cultivate symptthy with their work. At the present time, she was sorry to say, a parent very seldom saw a teacher unless he or she was going to find fault, That was not the best introduction.</p>
        <p>. Mr W T Wilkinson Proposed a vote of thanks to Mrs Ransoms end the Chairman. He hoped Mr Yonge would continue to come amongst them on that occasion every year as he had done for 35 years. (Applause). Mr Albinson seconded and said be hoped Mrs Ransome would cause the people of Latchford to take a broader outlook oh things. The vote was carried unanimously and Mr Yonge replied. Mrs Ransome and the Chairman received bouquets at the hands of Miss Amy Grounds and Miss Bertha Huskett. Master Isaac Wilkinson also banded a bouquet to the Rev J S Chedburn. The proceeds of the sale amounted to £74 11,ed. " The Church of the Future."</p>
        <p>"The relationship of the child to the Church" was the subject of an interesting address given by Miss Greenwood of Bolton, at the afternoon meeting held in Friars' Green Church on Saturday in connection with the Autumnal Convention of the Warrington and District Christian Endeavour Union. The Rev A J Wigley of Southport presided ovens good attendance and in the course of his opening remarks said the sub- ject which was going to be dealt with was one ot vital importance. Miss Greenwood prefaced her address by referring to the two subjects which seemed to be predominant at religious functions and in the religion papers. On one hand there were gloomy statistics about a decrease in Church membership, accounts of leakage with regard to the transfer of Sundae school scholars to the Church, a deploring of indifference and apathy on the part of men and women to the interests of the Cuurch. Side by side with that, and now gaining the ascendant was the discassion of some aspect of the child in its relationship to Christ and the Church. The Church of the present was formed of the children of the past generation. There was overwhelming evidence to prove that the men who were indifferent to the Church were the boys in the last generation who 11 had broken away from the Sunday school 1 about the age of twelve. The wave of religious enthusiasm reached its height during adoles- cence and during that period the individual either went towards religious enthusiasm or indifference. The Church was awakening to the importance of its young life. If they did not win the boys and girls things would be wrong at the fountain head. The supreme duty of the Church was the soul-winning ot her young people. (Hear, hear.) To do that they must lead the children in the line of their own Interests, because only by 80 doing could they meet with success. The boy had imaginations of daring and a vague desire to excel; he had his interest in the concrete and was full of hero worship. They should make Christ his hero and then his own instincts would lead him nearer to Him. They should just get to know the child, his interests, char- acteristics and instinctive tendencies and then follow up their teaching on lines whioh would appeal to his nature. In conclusion Miss Greenwood said the hope of the Church was in the child. Let their society supplement the work of the Sunday school, offering to the young people the opportunity to develop their devotional gifts. Let them see that they did not live for themselves, but that they rendered help to all their fellow-creatures who needed it. If they kept on work- ing in the interegts of the children then the Church of to-morrow would be realised. (Applause). In the course of an interesting discussion much of a useful nature was said in advice to Sunday school teachers. Miss Greenwood remarked that the personality of a teacher or a preacher gave a tone to the atmosphere in which he or she taught and /preached. At a further stage in the discussion the Chaiiman said be did not believe in the junior endeavour meetings being open to all and sundry. Tea was then served in the schoolroom and a large number congregated at the evening meeting which was I presided over by Mr A E Hoyl. He was supported by the Rev A J Wigley and J Fleming and Mr A I Mounneld. An eloquent address was delivered by the Rev J Fleming on the general outlook of the religious world. He eulogised the great and useful part which was played by the Christian Eudeavour union and urged them to keep up the spirit of enthusiasm It was only by hard and strenuous work that their hopes could be realised. The Rev A .7 Wigley also addressed the gathering and two solos were ably rendered by Mr Gilbert Smith.</p>
        <p>New Church Lectures. The Providence of Cod. ,osd The Rev J LTowartb, of Bolton, delivered the of the the course of lectures in the Masonic Hall, 0, The day evening last on "The Providence of God. toe lecturer referred to the frsquent use of the pod fortune, misfortune, chance, mischance, good loo: foe ill-luck, pointing out that the frequent and comunlome of these terms was evidence of the recognition of bout power hovering over, entering into, and circliog all the affairs of men, something which is beY°l3.r to reach of men, does not come within their pow"ctire control. This ruling and guiding power is ever abeit working for the salvation of men and always for t d it eternal good. This is the Divine Providence.stl it must ever act for the preservation of all creationt'ior, has been in the past so must, it be for all since like the Lord from whom it procee7toe is infinite and eternal. It would be absurd to _Ass pito that after the creation of man the Creator retirs"„o on the depths of His creation and left everything t° bed its own way. Revelation assures us that still the sit fie of the Lora is from everlasting to everlasting, t," tbe is loving unto every men, that He Is kind 0.13 so unthankful and the evil, and that He sendetb o,ld cieo the just and the unjust. The creation of the Nor'inisot a means to the end—the making of man as a reo.rf se offlGod's love and wisdom endowed with 1301 rOe reciptocate that which he had received in acts ofs and service. The final end in end was the forrai" heaven of angels from the human race. Man is en" oid with faculties zapable of endless development,oce• expansion and power to bring them into active ss'escli The attainment of the angelic state is within tbe role all, providing evil is shunned as sin against God' to is the burden of revelation. All its teachings 5. The lead upward and onward to such high attainment oro freedom of thought and the freedom of choice sl to constituent features of man as a living soul and I;ebed highest purposes in the creation are to be accoraP ied then these two must be protected and Presesa ie ' inviolate. It is of the Divine Providence that In 00 sustained in this state of freedom. And althougb_4o Lord rules all for the best, never in the least toso interferes with man's freedom, and whatever is c'word in the exercise of his freedom, when realised in,_.ch ie or act, adds to or takes from that manhood wsi mot being built up and established within him. Manto do be preserved in liberty, and as none are compelltioo good neither are any forced to do evil. The CO of freedom demands this. The evil therefore "vided exists is permitted though not divinely Prc)schilst in the order of the providence of God. And in liberty is maintained, for every one strives to isi'e to liberty, he is always n the condition of being sb„ be provided with good. The next lecture is on Problem of Sin."</p>
      </div>
