        <p>HOW WOMEN ARE " SW EATED." .1 Mrs. Alderton, of Colohester, gave a striki 00101 ng I •ng 0 of the "sweating " of women workers et a meetliitio 1 the Braintree and flocking Women's Liberal AssqA MO. In a town not very far from Braintree, sn';oote , Alderton, she found a Woman making most e 3 005 1 nightdresses at 2,d. each. She noticed til 0000 garment had a email red mark in the neck, and ht time afterwards she saw in a fashionable shoP dress hearing the identical red mark. Tke P,ric one guinea ! Mrs Alderton bought the nightorn.l3_Sd bill ^ had it made up into a parcel, aith the receiPliono- ' inside. She then went st once to tbe ortter. , facturer, and asked him if he could not pay ?is lessen' ! a little more than :Id. for makiog Dightto por that _nowt; The manufacturer MIS exceedingly sorry was so poor, but he was afraid he could not PaYTrosea . farthing • they got so little out of the nigh,t" ed the Mrs Alderton untied her parcel, and disineY.Dagef,r elaborate garment and the receipted bill! The re,;"Dot; evidently nonplussed, made some excuse. „wise- declared Mre Alderton," my intervention vvaere • r.,eceir we tive of good,for in a short time the women ing sd. instead of 21cl for similar work."</p>
