        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>THp LOCAL ELECTIONS. aticlilow is the time for the resurrection of those smooth Th Pleasant words " economy " and " efficiency." tilPle hardy annuals have been constantly revived at icipal elections as far back as I can remember, the they are still as serviceable as ever. All the Fame rates continue their upward tendency. Taking country as a whole the number of householders „.40 have to go to prison because they are unable to their rates is appallingly large. By-the-bye, the oitoell,bishop of York, whom I reported when he was a th,;aie-lecturer commends the payment of rates as Bttnplest and most elementary way in which every he!tert has an opportunity of contributing to the welt- Of his fellow-countrymen. It scunds well t'etlgh, whatever else the burdened ratepayer ey think about it. On the other hand, Lord and Selo makes a bid for our sympathy .with the er extreme but perhaps not altogether unjustfiable rertion that if we lived in Laputa we could not have Tr,ised a more curious system of rating than our own. pa4ls reformer insists that something which does not 1 from the point of view of municipal economy is Vent • ter , and that urges that progress in sanitary an,,clua is regulated by the degree of sound, educated, tie Popular sentiment behind it. And so the discus- Ors merrily on. As I have had occasion to write 4.4 another place," I would fain see the mass of ,pathetie burgesses for once in a while roused to the tvoractical and intelligent interest in the affairs of the elcmnity which the present situation demands of all i'tilectl Citizens; failing that, one cannot hope for the forProvement in our methods of local government 41 which there is confessedly plenty of room. LITANT- LIBERALISM. li1.1.!18 Lancashire and Cheshire Federation of the k;tional League of Young Liberals not very long isatted an epitome of municipal reforms to be Bed upon municipal candidates ,• and Ho: one corried it as a very hopeful sign. Their principal 117xtenendations may thus be summarised :—That he' Rousing and Town Planning Act should be rto ri rilPtly and vigorously administered. That moth- t4- Palities should be urged to purchase land freely for the Purposes of parks and open spaces. That to abate the° Bcuoke nuisance fines for the emission of smoke tra,ll,ld he increased. That profits on municipal w'ing should be restricted to a reasonable return on 4 capital actually invested. And yet—and yet— about the rates ? But these would not be ected by the following changes, and most Liberals tintlet concede their desirability :—That the distribu- cf wards should be periodically revised so as to ,C lire equal electoral districts on the basis of votes 4111:, the register. That the Aldermanic Bench should be ished. That every opportunity should be afforded 'cuncillors of eliciting information by means of ‘lleations in Council. That full and free access to to tra_ittee voting lists should be given to all rate-</p>
        <p>WOV F•N VOTERS. the subject of " Women and municipal affairs," I,4lae_e,lT and suggestive comment was made ,in last 1,0" editorial columns. It is indeed matter for ader that the lady agitators who are worse than ktt!ling their energies and wealth in endeavoming to ' leve the Parliamentary franchise by really criminal kilthods so lightly ".regard the opportunities undoubt- toY afforded by access to the Town Council and (irds of Guardians for the furtherance of the cause k 67 profess to hold so dear. Can it be, as an Jaerman writer put it a while ago, that after all the ber of women who really care to vote is about t7lal to the number of men who like 0 put the baby hgeeP ? l'I(E NUISANCE. 413.1 t has already been remarked that there is a strange I,oBenee and allusion to this pressing local problem 1.; tho addresses of municipal candidates in War- ettnBt°ll, although the town suffers notoriously from ,Jessive and preventable smoke from its long tytatleys, this constant and needless pollution of the wrsphere being unmistakably injurious to health 9 not insist on the more general introduction of 'Dke consuming appliances. One reads elsewhere tt,,a •I'Da having four boilers in ordinary operation, thneunaing about 50 tons of coal in 21 hours, and yet ;tete is said to be no smoke. It is the carbon that tothe source of the trouble ; burn the carbon, they us' and there will be no smoke. We are Itath • oritatively assured that all that is required k a free admixture of oxygen. In " Cassier s tzaazine," Mr. John T. Franklin has recently ained how this result can bn achieved, and much !tiri tlg of fuel effected. The importance of prevention ktaseen from his remarks that smoke issuing from a ti,,ek represents coal bought but not consumed ; it;43l spent, but heat not obtained. This kind of 4811ment should appeal to the most selfish of lien- 11—those whom none but base and sordid motives ati theinfluence. Mr. Franklin recommends especially i anderfeed mechanical stoker. Hie main point is that wipition temperature is not reached prior to contact 16'h the boiler, combustion failure is the result, and D: Part which has not reached the ignition point q,4es out into the flue and from the chimney in the Pe of smoke. Anyhow, Warringtonians who are toeteised in mind respecting these things, and are tt;,,llvinced of the necessity for improvement in the Cction indicated, should make the most of the vt'nee which the approaching elections undoubtedly 0,415 for educating the public and bringing pressure 410 ear upon candidates. No doubt this pape will by simultaneously pegging away at the question. 14111 STOCKS &amp;c. kr 1. • Thomas Dutton of Manchester has, in the tth.c'tkshire Weekly Post " of Saturday last, a letter itt;e4 contains matter of some local interest. It is jaswer to a previous contribution by a Mr. Millar. kjiract the following from the body of the corn- -1,4L. jeation :—" Mr. Millar is, I think, in error in "38 that there are stocks and whipping posts at item • tut in Lancashire.' There is not, so far as lam I,4l'cii Limm in Lancashire—there is a Lymm in eshire, where there are stocks and an ancient cross, 42dIewer steps of which have been hewn out of the (11rock ; but there is no whipping-post." Towards 4: close of a letter much too lengthy for reproduc- -41; la extenso Mr. Dutton describes the barbarous Ist7lll2naent which was used for the punishment of \;;VI down to comparatively recent times. He adds bliale yews ago I remember seeing one of these tll,;',es in the museum at Warrington. Probably it is</p>
        <p>v'tere, still." - j,SATILE MR. YOWNROE. Tp,ne 12Ian in his time plays many parts, %nd Mr B S tlat.rirea, who, as prospective Parliamentary candi- Of par the Middleton division has spent three months !kitlue alleged summer in residence at Todmorden and tcf ed of many things, appeared before the Workers terntleational Guild there the other evening, and read a tkirehensit e paper on The modern drama." An tltitinriend there sends me a copy of a local paper con- tl4 ,ff a report of the address. Here, according to 19 Mr Townoe's conclusion of the whole matter . NI object in this paper has been to outline the t)f COI of the drama from delineation of action to that Lptirlitlet and more spiritual events, and to show lli,v, hold. the active and psychological drama depend on i3te leg the interest of a varied audience. There is a fUtUre on such lines for the British drama, for in VarlY of the best creative minds will find an outlet kaitheir imaginative genius. In the words of Mr Gil tttil'o,„ In the reign of Elizabeth the drama outshone in the reign of Victoria the novel crowded to he drama These are signs that a literary era is . • the f ancing in which the drama will again regain to 41,T till its position as a literature." ItlfEß NOTE. • • Marltlng for a paper published on the morning bitoti'lving this latest performance by Mr. Townroe, the ter journalist to whom I am indebted for the l)tll'Ilet• above referred tq had the following among sti Observations on the general subject: —An old '4 4a 4Y has claimed that the drama is the most for Zed pleasure of a polished people, "almost wholly tharle.ff their manners, and having no inconsiderable thatß ln their morals." Later writers have also held °l,taltthe ultimate purpose of the drama is moral—to I,'ete) to purify, and to correct. Time was when would have thought of challenging this. In -0;z11(1.• as in ancient Greece, the native drama had etaa:lgle in religion; but there is much in modern ,latlT.ei,to accentuate the broad fact of divorce between tlaq e. 4 laci stage. The taste for dramatic representa- htq2.6 indeed universal; but many who for good and Datrolent reasons seldom patronise mcdern theatrical ..%,...t_teances, and chiefly depend for their knowledge</p>
        <p>upon the private reading of plays, turn again and again with increasing instruction and delight to the marvellous production of the prolific Elizabethan period. We cannot but agree that the twentieth century has few really great names to show in this department of literature. SOME SLIGHT DISAGREEMENT. From the closing sentences and the foregoing I infer that Mr. Townroe's Todmordon audience were not absolutely at one with him in his whole-hearted admiration for the modern drama, although the lecturer had been careful to select for commendation only such writers as Yeats, Barrie, Galsworthy, Pinero, Shaw, and the like. After all, therefore, there was not adequate room for anything like serious difference of opinion. As to the strength, not to say severity, of the opposition of which the people of those parts are capable, a revelation assuredly awaits Mr. Townroe. So far his tour in the Division has been but a succession of agreeable picnics ; and hope tells a flattering tale. I know that district ; I worked there some years ; and the young politician who has set Out to woo that constituency in the Conservative interest may take my weld for it that he is destined to a rude disillusionment when the critical test comes</p>
