      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>i CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB. ....-,‘,-..",,-..-",‘"•,.......,--</head>
        <p>i CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB. ....-,‘,-..",,-..-",‘"•,.......,--</p>
        <p>SOME NEWSPAPER MISPRINTS.</p>
        <p>Misprints are always happening in news- papers and some of them are decidedly funny. I have just got two from America both of which are genuine mistakes. A Chicago paper was describing the cargo of the Steamship " Alaska " but instead of saying that she carried a quantity of oats said that she had on board "forty thousand bushels of cats."</p>
        <p>The most extraordinary mistake I have seen for a long time was made in a Buffalo paper. In America after a President has been elected he publicly takes an oath. This newspaper said that it was a most solemn spectacle to see the President step forward and take his bath in the presence of ten thousand spectators. The printer only slipped one letter but what a shocking result. A BRAVE IRISH BOY.</p>
        <p>Two or three months ago there was a remarkable demonstration in New York, when quite two thousand people gathered to escort from the hospital to his home a boy named Peter Murphy, There was a railway smash in New York City and one of the sufferers was the hero of our little tale. His feet and legs were caught beneath the engine which had telescoped the car. He had worked one leg free and was about to pull the other loose when the roof of the car fell on both legs. While he hung there in agony the Chief of the Fire Depart— ment came along, and Murphy begged him to lift the timber off his legs. "If I do that," said Farrel, "the , roof will fall on the other side. There are women there." "I didn't think of that," said Murphy. "Let it stay. I'll stand the pain. So he waited, a long, terrible half hour, till his fellow-sufferers were iragged from under the ruins. No wonder that two thousand people escorted the crippled hero from Bellevue Hospital to his home in New Rochelle. It was a tribute to a really fine bit of courage.</p>
        <p>TO SLUMBERLAND.</p>
        <p>There are fast trains without number to the land of</p>
        <p>Happy Slumber. And swiftly through the twilight do they fly; But the slow accommodation, one that stops at every</p>
        <p>station, Is the one we like the best, my dear and I.</p>
        <p>There's a chair-car in connection, always going our</p>
        <p>direction, And as sounds the warning whistle, " hit-to</p>
        <p>whoo-oo ! " Off come tiny shoe and stocking, and we're rocking, rocking, rocking, In our little private section built for two. There's a wait about a minute for a solo by Miss</p>
        <p>Linnet, Or a concert by the tree toads 'mid the leaves ; Or we lean far out to follow some belated homing swallow, Spec ding swiftly to its rest beneath the eaves. Thus at last with loving parlance, draw we near the Bridge of Silence, And the Moonman swings his lantern round the bend. Then we slower go and slower, and a little head drops lower, _</p>
        <p>the —1; • For ridge once crossed we've reached our journey's end.</p>
        <p>Mere are fast trains without namber to the land of</p>
        <p>Happy Slumber, And swiftly through the twilight do they fly; But the slow accommodation, one that stops at every station, Is the one that we like best, my dear and I. THIS WEEK'S STORY.</p>
        <p>A park policeman, seeing a youngster standing on the brink of a pond, accosted the boy. "What's the matter, Tommy?" he queried. The youngster pointed to a hat which was bobbing up and down in the middle of the pond. "My bruvver—" he sobbed. In a flash the courageous constable divested himself of his coat and plunged into the water. He reached for the hat and dived for the lost lad. He came up, but with the hat only. "Can't find him!" he gasped. " Where was he standing when he fell in?" The boy gasped. "He ain't fell in," he said. " He's over there. I was going to tell you he throwed my hat into the pond, but you wouldn't lemme finish 1"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>RECKLESS MOTORISTS.</head>
        <p>RECKLESS MOTORISTS.</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, October 26, 1912.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Unequal Punishment of Offenders.</head>
        <p>Unequal Punishment of Offenders.</p>
        <p>An important circular dealing with punishment inflicted for offences under the Motor-Car Acts has been issued by the Home Office for the information of magistrates. The Secretary of State," it says, "has bad his attention called from time to time to what is alleged to be the 'unequal manner in which the Acts are administered, and, without attaching uniue importance to general allegations of that nature, it seems to him that, occasionally at any rate, hardened and deliberate offenders are treated too leniently, and less serious offences meet in some instances with uncessarily severe punishment." As to serious offences, it is given as Mr McKenna's opinion that power to suspend a driver's licence or to declare him disqualified for obtaining a licence for a given period supplies the most appropriate punishment, and is most likely to be effective. Heavy penalties are not to be imposed as a matter of routine, and trivial l offences not due to any grave negligence way properly bee met by .y Whenawa ju warning gstices and payment of costs or a ight ntdo not [think it proper to s take a lenient course regard is to be paid in inflicting penalties to the means of the offender. Under the head of dangerous driving the circular adds : "There can be no doubt that a person who drives, for instance, at a furious rate through a narrow village street may properly be proceeded against in a case where no proof can be produced that there was any person present in the street whom his driving actually endangered."</p>
        <p>CANADA'S THREE PRAIRIE PROVINCES. Are estimated to have 357 million acres of arable land, of which barely 15 millions of acres are under cultivation. Yet this fraction is estimated to have produced this year 450 million bushels of grain, 200 millionslof which are wheat. The methods of cultiva- tion during the last 30 years have undergone a surpris- ing transformation. In the eighties the settlers on the Wtstern prairie broke the land by oxen drawn ploughs, now 6,000 motor traction engines with 10 and 12-gang ploughs are breaking new soil for next year's crop. The railways are trying hard to keep up with production, The Canadian Pacific Railway built 580 miles last year and is now constructing 870 miles more. The Canadian Northern added 380 miles last year and has 600 miles under construction. The Grand Trunk Pacific expects to add 600 miles to its system this year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>WARRINGTON NONCONFORMITY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON NONCONFORMITY.</p>
        <p>ITS 250th ANNIVERSARY.</p>
        <p>w,</p>
        <p>THE ORIGIN OF THE</p>
        <p>Dr. Owen was succeeded in the ministry byl one whose youth would stand in marked contrast to his ripe years and riper experience for John Seddon's ordination fell upon his 22nd birthday and he entered then upon the work of his first, and, as events were to prove his only pastorate. His family appears to have belonged to Ringly near Bolton, but his father was minister of a Nonconformist Church, first at Ormskirk and then at Hereford. In this city John Seddon was born on December Bth, 1725. He received his education with a little ' company of students that met under the care' of Dr. Robertson iat Kendal, four at least of whom did useful service as Nonconformist ministers. A short period at Glasgow University completed his training and he came to Warrington to his ordination on December Bth, 1747. From that date and through twenty-three years Mr Seddon ministered in the new chapel inheriting therein not only a fine building but a mass of tradition which must have grown around the venerable and firm old scholar who had pre- ceded him. _ _ _</p>
        <p>There is little to tell us whether he proved a worthy successor and whether the work grew under his hands. His letters and manuscript do not suggest the attractive preacher though he is said to have been popular. In what he wrote he appears heavy and wearisome, but he had gifts of perseverance and patience which were to count in the story of Nonconformity and which made his sojourn in Warrington most memorable. It was the zeal of John Seddon that brought into existence the Warrington Academy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>'ARRINGTON ACADEMY.</head>
        <p>'ARRINGTON ACADEMY.</p>
        <p>At the same time it was designed that the Academy should "give some knowledge to those who were to be engaged in commercial life, as well as in the learned professions, in the more useful branches of literature, and to lead them to an early acquaintance with, and just concern for, the true principles of liberty, of which principles they must, in future life, be the supporters." With this broad programme and with ess than £5OO in l'promises, the Committee began to make choice of suitable tutors and a suit- able building.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>OLD ACADEMY.</head>
        <p>THE OLD ACADEMY.</p>
        <p>GTOI4 AcADEIIIY,-1757</p>
        <p>teltrniAd frigut fiell)nnotavntl iitahh ,</p>
        <p>or iwtturo e'is!"</p>
        <p>Taikua</p>
        <p>Lord Willoughby, the most eminent among the Nonconformists of that time, had been elected President, and John Seddon was chosen secretary. The choice of building is well known, and the pile at Bridge Foot that has in recent years regained some of its old prestige and beauty, is one of the most familiar, as it will one day become the most prized of Warrington buildings. Its old surroundings have gone and can never be recalled. An old student who wrote in the "Monthly Repository" in 1813 says that" a considerable extent of garden ground" was attached to it and that "a handsome terrace ' walk on the banks of the Mersey" gave it a "respectable collegiate appearance." Youthful affection may have exaggerated its beauties, and it is not necessary for us at this hour to decide whether or not the Academy was a beautiful home of learning. It is enough that it inspired the affection of Anna Lititia Aikin, and drew from her the stately poetic description which Warring- tonians must always prize.</p>
        <p>.4., - ------.4' ' ';',-Z,-:,„. _-,,..:-..--- - - .-.-...;,... -,_------ ..- ... • ------_ 1 ..,-, ',..,-...-:,..rs ... ...„,„..-...........ati....=,...."2•?:::_.ii, 21'1 -,:::.:1,-;7 ' • ''. --N'.s ~..... 7.17-att............m.A.. ~_.7,..,-_._- _•.+_ ...._,7, ...;....;,,„, 7... , ' -!::::ttN':-.--r.-7 -A.:l`; :', • -,:a.--- ; S. El [6l {B : -... f - i - ." ..7., ..... ...,- ,•,-.,g`,..4 t'lk -'7- ' • N't fo, ," ~ II -1 11 TN .;,. Arbil ~i '•i' :-., ~1--...-••-•••• • •-1ZI•j. -: ' ,-;..61 ~ ' 7 A-• - k-0,,,f-,•.-.-.. 4 .1,','.141i, ~. 1,l IN_ ' 1. ,iti 'l--,,„ .!‘i, ,iim- ,: ~.,, • ' ' ._., i,,, ._ il; 4 ,ri,--...91, ......„_thcm,--ii, ,s .14.... aliilt.,,,a_ I' ''''IIIIIII/hIIIII'? .Ftil 2-- - ~...-f.,7,-c-L.,•52-twA-.1-i;,,g,N1,:1110-mrarg it... -Amu? Ir onninotillriiiiiii .: , . ....,,,,ii. - ...,..,,,•:.:_;„,----2:::-..----,7,5-1-1; • _ , • , TEE WARB111: • , ‘4!.Atalo terwrd ikk nw,iv,y oi ( mart) 5, jice ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>DR. JOHN TAYLOR.</head>
        <p>DR. JOHN TAYLOR.</p>
        <p>In making choice of tutors the Committee attracted to Warrington a man whose repu- tation and learning were sufficient to make the Academy a place of universal notice. It invited as Divinity Tutor Dr. John Taylor, of Norwich. This very capable scholar was born in Lancaster in 1694, and became a Nonconformist minister. During eighteen years spent in a pastorate at Kirkstead in Lincolnshire, he laid the foundations of a learning which was to make him famous. Passng thence in 1733 to Norwich he became minister of the Octagon Chapel, and in 1754 110 published his most famous work, a Hebrew Concordance in recognition of the Ivalue of which the University of Glasgow had conferred the degree of Doctor of Divinity.</p>
        <p>That such a scholar should have been attracted to W arrington, and should have been willing to relinquish so important a Church shows how seriously the Academy with its programme for the education of the ministers of the future was taken by the lead- ing Nonconformists. _ _</p>
        <p>As tutor in Mathematics and Natural Philosophy the choice fell upon John Holt, of Kirkdale, and Dr. John Aikin shortly accepted the post of tutor in Language and Belles Letters. Each of these teachers received a salary of £lOO a year and a fee of two guineas from each student who attended his lectures. Houses were secured for the tutors and they took the students as boarders at the rate of £l5 per annum for those who had two months vacation and £lB for those who had no vacation," but these terms did not include candles, fire and washing, nor did they include the luxury of tea. *Of Mr. John Holt we know little save that he had been the minister of a Dissenting Church near Lancaster and afterwards for many years the head of a school at Kirkdale near Liverpool. DR. JOHN AIKIN. Dr. John Aikin, in token of the impress he left upon Warrington, merits .some notice here. He was born in London at the end of 1713 where his father, a native of Scotland had been long in business as a linen draper. After an education under Dr. Doddridge be passed to Aberdeen University returning to Dr. Doddridge to became his assistant. His next sphere was at Leicester where he became minister of the congregation, but a fall from his horse gave an injury to the breast which caused him • to spit blood and disqualified him for regular pulpit work. He conducted a school at Farndon for a short time but having married he removed to Kibworth where he gathered a flourishing school.</p>
        <p>Here was born to him his talented daughter Anna Letitia and when he removed to Warrington he brought with him the maiden who was to spend here her happiest years and receive impressions which were to be described in imperishable verse. Mr. Seddon recommended to him a house in the Buttermarket "handsomely sashed to the front, with a flight of five steps to the entrance "and Dr Aikin took up his abode there in August 1758.</p>
        <p>A MODEST BEGINNING. The Academy opened its doors on October 23rd 1757. Three students appeared with two others after the first month and these five were all that the Institution during its first year could show. But among them was the honoured name of Thomas Percival, a fact that would have consoled the tutors for their scanty classes could they but have read the future.</p>
        <p>Disappointment there certainly must have been for the learned Tutor in Divinity. Hei had left an important church to come to Warrington and for a whole year his Divinity class had but one member. How that solitary desciple, John Drake, received the abundant stores of learning which Dr. Taylor could unlock for him we do not know</p>
        <p>I F your child is fretful, it is most likely that his stomach is out of order. Remember that Steedman's Powders are unrivalled for regulating the bowels. This medicine contains no poison or narcotic,</p>
        <p>It is only recorded of him that be died soon after his course at the Academy was over. Three others were placed under his teach- ing in the second year and the number may have increased in the year that followed, but so far as can be ascertained not more than eleven students entered his class., _</p>
        <p>Dr. Taylor was in hits 63rd year when he accepted the post and barely four years of life were left to him. These closing years were far from happy. Possibly he was too old to adapt himself to a new course of life, and he perhaps lacked the patience with youth which the position demanded. His class- room manner was dictatorial and he was impatient of contradiction. Great as a scholar he was not great as a teacher of youth. Worst of all, he declined interference from the Trustees, and in 1760 friction arose which lasted until his death in 1761. Dr. Taylor's great repute gave him influence and many of his friends withdrew their help. Thus early in its career the Academy received a blow from which it never entirely recovered.</p>
        <p>Dr. Taylor died on March 3rd 1761, and was buried within the Chapel of Chowbent. A tablet there contains an inscription pre- pared by his friend Dr. Enfield. Near to this place lies interred what was mortal of JOHN TAYLOR, D.D. READER.</p>
        <p>Expect no eulogiTi-m-Ir-OM this atone; enquire amongst the friends of LEARNING LIBERTY AND TIMM. These will do him justice. Whilst taking his natural rest he fell asleep in Jesus the 4th of March 1761 Aged 66. Of the three first appointments to tutor- ships two appear to have been unfortunate, but in Dr. Aikin the Academy found a most valuable teacher. Prudent and kind, his influence compensated in some measure for the harshness of his colleagues, whilst his learning and activity inspired confidence and drew students to his classes. The asser- tion is perhaps correct that he was the one man who made the continuance of the Acadamy possible. It began to flourish when he joined it, it declined with his declining strength and it fell asunder three years after his death.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>rrfy, WARRINGTON ACADEMY.</head>
        <p>rrfy, WARRINGTON ACADEMY.</p>
        <p>This institution has interest quite beyond the ordinary to Warrington folks; it gained moreover a place of interest in the general history of Nonconformity and made Warring- ton,in the Dissenting world at least,a notable centre and the "Athens of the North." We must not fail therefore in a tribute to the man who was mainly responsible for it and without whose unflagging zeal it would never have become a reality. The work done by Dr. Owen in his first Academy may have suggested to his successor the possibility of its continuance. J3ut other events which were occuring made the matter of education one of urgent concern. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>In 1750 Dr.Doddridge passed away and his Academy was removed from Northampton. Two others who had devoted themselves to the education of ministers also died and their institutions were thereby brought to a close. One was Dr. Rotherham who had been tutor to John Seddon at Kendal, and Dr. Latham who survived him three years died in 1754, leaving no successor to carry on his valuable work. _</p>
        <p>The concern which was felt among influential Nonconformists at the closing of these useful schools gave Mr. Seddon oppor- tunity to put forward his bold project. His proposal was to establish a Warrington Academy and make Warrington the home of learning for the DissentinglChurches.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>INITIAL DIFFICULTIES.</head>
        <p>INITIAL DIFFICULTIES.</p>
        <p>Such a proposal made on behalf of such a town naturally awakened the susceptibilities of other towns, and when Mr. Seddon began his campaign of explanation and begging he found difficulties that must have exhausted any ordinary store of patience. "How he worked and wrote and explained and begged!" says Mr. Bright, who wrote a delightful little history of the Academy. His letters certainly are a very memorial of patience. Taking Manchester first he tries to stimulate the merchants to some interest lin education. Liverpool is the next point of attack and Birmingham follows. In October 1755 he writes that he has canvassed these towns and now purposes to appeal to "our friends in London.' And his letters are models of explicitude and of a courtesy that borders upon servility. Moderate responses came to these appeals for subscriptions. Warrington sympathisers promised £3l, Manchester promised £94, Birmingham £44 and Liverpool £46. In 1754 the total had reached £3OO and in June 1757 after three more years of patient agitation a meeting of subsdribers was held and a resolu- tion passed that the Academy be established.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>WARRINGTON VERSUS -ORMSKIRK.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON VERSUS -ORMSKIRK.</p>
        <p>But other difficulties besides that of endow- ment had to be compassed, the chief of which arose from the choice of Warrington as the home of the Academy. As one of Mr. Seddon's correspondents said, their " richer neigh- bours" were not likely to be over zealous in a cause which would bring so much honour to Warrington. The "richer neighbours" unfortunately had a rivalry of their own. Liverpool thought that the choice of War- rington was a move on the part of Man- chester and in a printed manifesto urged the claims of Ormskirk as against those of Warrington as a place when youth could with best success be "liberally educated." In the eyes of the Liverpool pampleteers Warrington was a place of hopeless and unholy bustle, and of distractions that would be fatal to student and tutors alike, as com- pared with the sweet and placid Ormskirk. Liverpool continued to object until the very last, and when the resolution which fixed upon Warrington was submitted to the meeting it was set forth that "for the present and as a temporary settlement, Warrington is the most convenient situation for the Academy."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>GENEROUS AIMS.</head>
        <p>GENEROUS AIMS.</p>
        <p>_ The Academy as its promoters planned .‘ _ _ _</p>
        <p>was to be an "'Academfcal Institution which should unite as far as was possible the advantages of the public and more private method of education," which should be fitted for the equipment of ministers on the principle of them being "free to follow the dictates of their own judgments, in their enquiries after truth, without any undue bias imposed on their understandings."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>(To be continued.) A. M.</head>
        <p>(To be continued.) A. M.</p>
        <p>DOG-EATING CRUELTIES IN GERMANY.</p>
        <p>Writing on the consumption of dog meat in Germany, the " Frenkfrirther Zeitung " (September 18th) says : " The most dog meat is eaten in Saxony, according to the statistics. Chemnitz has always been the town whore the most dogs were slaughtered for meat. But here statistics prove very little. . . . because they remain far behind the sad reality. According to the latest official statistics, 69,400 kilogrammes (67 tons) of dogs' meat was eaten in Saxony last year; but the statistics embrace only the smaller part of the slaughterings, namely, only the dogs that are killed in the slaughter houses. The number killed secretly for human food is far greater than this. Such killings are often carried out in the most brutal manner. Many such dogs are stolen. Only a short while ago the Dresden Society for the Protec- tion of Animals warned owners against letting their dogs run around loose, since they were enticed away for slaughtering oftener than formerly. These killings must, therefore, be carried out very secretly. Often the dog's mouth is tied shut,then be is stabbed to death, beaten to death with a club, or strangled. . . Many an old dog, and such as have become sick or too fat, are given away by their ungrateful owners -to be slaughtered, and a few are bought; but the most of the dogs for table consnmption are obtained in dishonest ways. In Chemnitz several years ago there were restaurants in which dogs' meat was used in preparing food, and no secret made of the fact, and they are probably still in existence. At that time the sellers of dog meat there obtained a part of their supplies from other towns. The meat cost at that time 60 to 70 pfennigs per kilogramme ; but it is dearer to-day. Such meat is now eaten not only in the big towns and industrial regions, but also in the country districts. Many families are glad now-a- days when they can get anywhere—and they are quite indifferent as to the means employed—a fat dog without paying money for it. Even cats are eaten more than formerly. Owing to the present high prices of meat, many poor families put into a pan or pot anything that is meat. Many such families at present regard dog. meat as a special delicacy. Even the fat is used ; according to an old superstition it cures consumption. Owners of hunting preserves and fishing waters com- plain that poaching and fishing-stealing have grown worse than formerly ; also, doubtless, a consequence of the dearness of meat.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>BREATHLESS WITH BRONCHITIS. – --- A Chronic Victim's:Alarming Sensations.</head>
        <p>BREATHLESS WITH BRONCHITIS. - --- A Chronic Victim's:Alarming Sensations.</p>
        <p>" PErs TOUCHED MY CIIEST AS NO OTTIER REMEDY HAD DONE."</p>
        <p>A typical illustration of the way Peps are daily overcoming bronchial troubles is given by Mrs L. Smith, 53, Hardman Lane, Failsworth, nr. Manchester. "I was continually having attacks of bronchitis," Mrs. Smith said to a reporter. 'My chest was always cold, and felt as if a great weight was pressing on it. I often thought I should choke. A pain used to catch me right across the chest, as if something was clawing at me. These spasms left me quite exhausted. At times I couldn't lie down, for fear of suffocation. "I was unable to sweep or move my arms in any way without having to gasp for breath. My children and neighbours often used to come in and find me on my hands and knees leaning against the wall, exhausted. I had a terrible cough and the phlegm was nearly solid. "Two winters ago I had a doctor coming every day for months. Ile said I had bronchial-asthma. My husband at last got me some Peps, the new breathing remedy which everyone was talking about. "Alter taking a box or two of Peps I certainly felt better, and had less pain in my chest and less discom- fort in my breathing. Peps touched my chest as no other remedy had done, and as the feeling of tightness wore off, my breathing got easier and more regular. I didn't cough so much, either. I persevered regularly with Peps, and my chest and throat got quite strong again. "I was able at last to do my work and go out regu- larly. All through last winter I kept well, thanks alone to Peps, and expect to do so this winter, not- withstanding the wet summer. I never go out in the cold without a Peps tablet in my mouth; it keeps out the raw air." - - -</p>
        <p>Get a is. lid. or 2s. 9d. a box of Peps from your chemist and It them safeguard you this wintry seaso</p>
        <p>ComisriciAt FAlLURES.—According to " Kemp's Mercantile Gazette," the number of commercial failures published in Great Britain and Ireland during the quarter ending Saturday, September 28th,was 1,803. The number in the corresponding quarter of last year was 1,871, showing a decrease of 68, being a net decrease in 1912,..0f 306.</p>
        <p>EDUCATION COMMITTEE,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>EXTRA EXPENDITURE ON COUNCIL SCHOOLS.</head>
        <p>THE EXTRA EXPENDITURE ON COUNCIL SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>Epidemic Sickness.</p>
        <p>There was little business of publio interest at the monthly meeting of the Warrington Education Com- mittee on Monday evening,the chief point raised being that of extra items of expenditure incurred on the Council Schools. Councillor II Roberts presided and there were also present :—Mr A J Jolley (deputy chairman), Aldermen J Smethurst and A Bennett, Coun- cillors C Jackson Holmes, R Henshall, T Birtleg, J Dickenson, 0 Archer, W H J Jenkins, J Pemberton,and J Wilkinson, Mrs C Jackson Homes, Rev C Briggs, Messrs C Parker,J Upson,R T Ifairolough, J Charnel!, E Plinston, 0 Walker, J Moore Murray (director), R B Roberts (assistant director), and J Fairhurst (borough treasurer). THE LATE MR. F. MONKS. The CHAIRMAN alluded to the death of Mr F Monks who was chairman of the committee for the first two years of its existence and prior to that was the first Chairman of the Technical Instruction Committee. He laboured unceasingly for the cause of education for a very long period of his active public life. He had the highest ideals in regard to educational matters and influenced the events which had reference to that very important question in the beet possible manner, thus placing that committee and all educa- tionalists in the town under a deep debt of obligation for his magnificent work. They might claim that, as an educationalist, his services were of greater value than they were in any other public capacity. Mr Monks and those who were associated with him years ago laid the foundations of the present educational administration in this town, and many things which had marked the success which had accom- panied educational efforts in Warrington owed their Inception in a great measure to his loyal and unmiti- gated zeal and effort. Councillor Roberts moved a resolution recording high appreciation of Mr Monks's work and expressing sympathy with the family. Alderman BENNETT, as Mr Monks's immediate successor in the chair, added his tribute. He learnt his his first lessons in educational administration at the feet of Mr Monks, who sometimes showed a salutary impatience with some of his (the Alderman's) ideals, but he always realised, when they differed most. that Mr Monks was animated by the profoundest interest in the welfare of the town, and a sincere desire to make the committee serve the highest public pur- poses. Absorbed as he was in busineas affairs of the greatest magnitude and importance, he never let those monopolise his entire attention, but realised that he ad a duty to the people among whom he was reared, nd to the town to which he belonged.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>LATE MISS HETHERINGTON.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MISS HETHERINGTON.</p>
        <p>_ Conncillor ROBERTS moved a similar resolution in the case of Miss Hetherington who for 43 years occupied a prominent position as a teacher and for 28 years of that period served the town in the position of mistress of an infant school and latterly as mistress of the Beamont school infants' department. He thought he was not in any sense introducing any new or improper procedure in suggesting that the committee should pay a tribute of respect to her memory and appreciation of her worth and work which must have had a marked and permanent influence on the lives of the little ones entrusted to her care. Mr. JOLLEY seconded the votes of condolence which were passed in silence, the members standing. AUDITOR'S EXCEPTIONS.</p>
        <p>- -- At a meeting of the Finance Committee the report of the District Auditor of the Local Government Board upon his audit of the committee's accounts for the year ended on the 31st March, 1912, was submitted, and it was noted that the auditor had taken exception to the item in respect of the expenses incurred by the visits of the members of the Education Committee to industrial schools at which children from the Borough are being maintained by the Local Education Authority. The Law Clerk was requested to seek from the Local Government Board for the future guidance of the Com- mittee, a definite ruling on the question of the legality of such expenditure. Mr. FAIRCLOUOJI asked who was going to be responsible for the expense. It was hardly fair that members should be debarred from receiving their expenses when they bad done certain work. Mr JOLLEY : That matter Is under discussion between the local authority and the Local Government Board. MINIMUM PERIOD OF SERVICE FOR TEACHERS The Director of Education was instructed to prepare for submission to the School Management Committee at their next meeting, a draft circular to the Managers of the schools in the Borough upon the question of the place of residence and a minimum period of service in respect of teachers hereafter appointed to the schools. The minutes were passed without comment. EXTRA EXPENDITURE ON COUNCIL SCHOOLS. The Sites and Buildings Committee had sanctioned several items of expenditure on the Council schools. They included an electric bell Installation at the Bolton Council School at a cost of £9 108 ; the repair of the playground at the Bolton Council School, at a cost of 38 9d per superficial yard ; and the railing off of a portion of the girls' and Infanta' playground at Evelyn-street School for the purpose of yard accommodation for the caretaker. A report by the Surveyor in regard to the repair of the roofs of the Beamont Council school was submitted,and instructions were given to the Surveyor to have the work done in the manner suggested by him. Mr UrsoN asked for some explanation of these Item. He could understand the necessity for them in the case of old schools but all the buildings in question were recently erected. Almost monthly they had some items --in some respect serious items of expenditure—on buildings that had just been completed. The Evelyn- school was only opened in July and the responsible persons must have known that a yard was necessary for the caretaker's house. Now a part of the playground was being taken for that purpose. It seemed to him that there was some oversight or negligence that required explanation. Mr FAIRCLOUGH said the omission to provide adrying ground for the caretaker was an oversight and a small portion of the playground would now be railed off for the purpose. Councillor PEMBERTON said the necessity for repairs to the roof of Beamont school was a very serious matter. A roof, if properly constructed, ought to last forty or fifty years. It was a great reflection on some- one that new schools should want so much money spend- ing on them already, but if the ordinary layman ventured to express an opinion on architectural matters he was" jumped on." Mr FAIRCLOUGH said it was not the fault of the committee as they had a clerk of works to super- intend the work. They had a good builder and a good architect but amongst the three of them the work was not carried out in a satisfactory manner, as far as the roof was concerned. The committee regretted that it was not found out until the time during which the builder was responsible had elapsed. The minutes were approved. EPIDEMIC AND SCHOOL ATTENDANCE. On the minutes of the Medical Inspection Committee . being submitted, Mr FaincLourort said he was surprised to see there were more infants absent through sickness than there were at school at the present time. The DIRECTOR said the failing off was due to an epidemic of chicken pox and mumps—more especially mumps. It started at Latchford and had gone round the town.</p>
        <p>THE GAMBLING INCUBUS. MEN WHOSE VOTES OUGHT TO GO TO WOMEN. Mr J L Paton, High Master of the Manchester Grammar School, speaking at an anti,gambling demon. strat ion at the Co-operative Ball, Bury, said there was nothing in the country which so much worked against the education of the working classes and against the political intelligence of the working classes as that incubus which they bad on their backs. When a man day by day concentrated his attention upon the sordid and dishonest details of racing tables and tipsters, that man's mind was unfitted for the use of the vote, and if he bad his way he would disfranchise him and let a woman have his vote instead. No habitual gambler was a good man, and no tipster was a man he could trust with half-a-crown. A resolution in favour of the Betting Bill now before Parliament was adopted unanimously.</p>
      </div>
