      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, October 26, 1912.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, October 26, 1912.</p>
        <p>WELSH DISESTABLISHMENT. A LORD WHO WILL FIGHT TO THE LAST DITCH.</p>
        <p>"Envy, Hatred and Malice."</p>
        <p>" Adversity makes strange bedfellows" was the saying nct inap ly applied to the platform at the !aymen's meeting of protest against the Welsh Church Bill at the Parr Hall on Monday evening, for the principal speakers were a Peer and a workingman— the Right hon. Lord Dynevor and Mr. W. A. Wheel, of Llansamlet, Glamorgan (a member of the Steel Smelters' Trade Union). The tone of the speeches was uncompromising, Lord Dynevor declaring that he would not be satisfied unless the Church retained 20s. in the 1 of her endowments and that if the Church defenders were defeated he would rather be beaten "in the last ditch," With the banner of the Church still waving over his head. The audience, which included a large proportion of ladies, filled the body of the hall and the side galleries but there were plenty of vacant places in the back gallery. Mr El R Armitage presided and on the platform were Sir Gilbert Gfeenall, Bart. and Lady Greenall, Messrs F A Frost, J J Whitley, RI Wynne Jones, J CORM), 0 Lofthouse, W H Stansfield, Segar Owen, G Venn, F Pearson, E Bradshaw, G H Smith and other prominent laymen. The CHAIRMAN dealt with the contention that the Welsh Church question should be decided by Wales alone by quoting the axiom that "the whole is greater than its part." The Bill was an endeavour to uproot the tree, the seed of which was planted by their ancestors, and to break the trust imposed upon them. Their motto must be " Let us stick to our heritage"— (applause)—not in the spirit of religious intolerance, because that was not the spirit of to-day,either from the clerical or the lay s'cle of the controversy, but rather as a sacred trust which they dare not and could not, in honour, renounce. (Applause). Lord DYNEVOIL said the Bill proposed partly to disestablish the Church, must unreasonably to dis- member it, and most drastically to disendow it. Deal- ing with Nonconformist disabilities, his lordship said their first complaint was that Bishops sat in the House of Lords and they objected to them being there. He I should have thought a far better way of dealing with that question would be to let Nonconformist repre- sentatives also sit in the Howe of Lords. (Applause.) Personally, he would welcome them there. He would also point out that, under the Bill, only the four Welsh bishops were forbidden to sit in the House of Lords. The English bishops would still be there so that if that were a grievance, it still remained. The Govern- ment had pledged themselves—although they did not seem to be in a great hurry about the matter—to reform the House of Lords. Then would be the time, in his opinion,to decide whether the bishops shou'd sit in the Upper House or not. They were told that the Church would gain by Disestablishment and that it would be free. Without wishing to hurt anybody's feelings, he maintained that the question of whether the Church would gain or not was not a question to be decided by Nonconformists, but one for the Church alone to decide. (Applause.) If anybody said the Church would be free he at once knew that he bad not read the Bill. The measure provides that no member of the Church in Wales might in future attend Convocation at Canterbury. When they had it laid down by Act of Parliament what a Church might not do, he denied that there was any freedom in the matter. The constitution of the future Church in Wales and the area in which the Church might carry on its ministrations were also laid down by Act of Parliament. How could anybody say that that was a free Church? He went so far as to say that there was NO SUCH THING IN THIS COUNTRY AS A FREE CHURCH.</p>
        <p>unless they could show; hirn—S.— Church which had no trust deed and no endowments. If any Nonconformist denomination attempted to turn to the right or to the left by a single hair's breadth, from its trust deed, it was open to any member of the congregation to bring the case before his Majesty's judges and they would soon put back into their proper places those who had attempted to violate the trust deeds. He claimed that the United Free Church of Scotland was not only estab- lished by law but endowed by the action of the State. The Church of England and Wales had never been established by any one Act of Parliament. The Estab- lishment had been one of long, slow and often silent growth. All that Parliament ever did was to sanction and recognize a Church that was already in existence, Parliament never created the Church ; it would be much nearer the mark to say that the Church created ' Parliament—(applause)—for Wales tent her representa- tives to Convocation at Canterbury for two-and-a-half centuries before he tpnt a single representative to Parliament. Dealing with the favourite argument that the Church's property was national property, Lord Dynevor said that if that were so, he called upon the State to produce her title deeds. They could not be produced ; they did not exist. (Applause) If the Church's property was national property, why bother about an Act of Parliament ? Why not go straight to the Law Courts and ask to have returned to the nation the money that belonged to the nation? Obviously the Government would not do that, because they knew that there was no judge in the United Kingdom who would not hold that the Church's property did not belong to the State. (Applause). Mr Asquith did not hold that the State gave tithe to the Church ; he said it was originally a private benefaction. There were some who held that Henry VIII. took away money from one Church and gave it to another. Mr Lloyd George held that view. (Laughter.) What a pity it was that before he made that statement in the House of Commons, he did not read Mr Asquith's speech, for Mr Asquith said ;" I am not one of those who hold, as used to be currently assumed, that the legislation of Henry VIII, transferred the privileges and endow- ments of a National Church from the Church of Rome to the Church of England. I think that view rests upon imperfect historical information." MR. LLOYD GEORGE HAD BETTER. GO BACK TO SCHOOL —(laughter and applause)—and be would there learn that, according to his own Prime Minister, his views rested upon imperfect historical information. (Applause). According to the Liberationists' 350 years admitted possession by the Church was a bad title but 25 years possession by Nonconformists made a good title. Having contended that the Government Bill ensured to the Church only Is. 6d. in the 2—not Gs. Bd. as stated by Mr. McKenna—Lord Dynevor said he did not care whether Mr. Mc Kenna proposed to leave them 6s .Bd. cr 15s. in the ;he would go on fighting until the Church retained 20s. in the 2. (Loud applause). After calculating existing life interests, 220 clergy would be left without one farthing of endow- ment and 75 would be left with less than ss. a week—a pretty minimum wage for this Government to bring in! If tithe was national property why was £30,000 a year still left in the hands of lay impropriators ? It was only another proof that the whole of the Bill was framed out of "ALL HATRED, MALICE AND UNCHARITABLENESS." towards the Church. (Applause.) Alluding to the objects—libraries' museums, &amp;c.—to which the endow- ments were to be devoted, Lord Dynevor said that to take away money which was dedicated to the service of God and give it to secular bodi,.s was one of the most monstrous proposals ever put into a Bill. (Applause). Had they realized that if the Bill became law, it would mean the closing of many churches in the mountains of Wales ? Those parishes were very poor and could not afford to pay a resident clergyman, but, owing to a certain amount of endowment, those churches were able to keep open their doors, lie was sorry to say that the Nonconformists had realized their difficulty in the mountains of Wales—that the people were too poor to keep a resident Nonconformist minister. Pass that Bill and they would be closing many and many a church door, and depriving the poorest of the poor and the sick and dying of the ministrations of a clergyman. There were some who held that a Church was better off by means of working always on the voluntary system and that it should not rely upon endowments. He agreed in principle that the voluntary system was an exceedingly good one. Every- body should give as much as he could to whatever denomination he belonged. But that would not bring in enough and the Nonconformists, he was glad to think, had realised that fact. He did not think he was over-stating it when he said that the Wesleyans had a capital'endowment sum of £10,000,000. The Congrega- tional Union a short time ago had a sustantation fund of £143,000 and only the other day they were putting forward a further appeal for a capitalised sustentation fund. The Baptist Union were making a mighty appeal to their adlierents to give them £250,000 in order that their unmarried ministers might have a salary of £lOO to £l2O and their married ministers of from £l2O to £l5O. He wished them all God's blessing. Hehoped they would get all the money they required; he knew from personal experience how very inadequately many of them were paid. But would those who held that a Church was better oft without endowments put their principles into practice against themselves and disendow themselves ? " (Hear, hear). lie said " disendow themselves," because Church people had no intention of taking a single farthing away from them. (Applause.) He held that the Government had no right to dismember any Church, whether young or old, against the wishes of that Church. How did it hurt the Government or anybody else, if Churchmen in Wales wanted to remain united with Churchmen in England? The proposal, to his mind, was a most mean and unjust one, and was absolutely unworthy of a great nation like ours. (Applause.) Who was going to gain by the disendowment proposals. Obviously the Church was not. Would the Noncon- formists gain ? No, none of the money, when it was taken from the Church, was to be given to them—in fact, if the money was to be taken from the Church, he would far rather that it was given to the Noncon- formists ; he should feel that money which had been dedicated to the service of God was still going to be retained for that service. (Applause.) £173,000 a year was a mere drop in the ocean to the State—this year's Budget was for £l7B,ooo,ooo—but it meant a mighty lot to the Church. (Applause.) "Mr McKenna in a speech at the Queen's Hall "concluded Lord Dynevor " said 'lf Churchmen come to me in peace they will be met in peace.' My answer to Mr McKenna is</p>
        <p>THE CHURCH is NOT IN THE MARKET, and if you want peace you can easily get it by with- drawing your Bill.' (Applause.) Whoever heard of those holding and defending a citadel, going in peace to the attackers without a fight ? I can assure Mr McKenna lam not going to him in peace. lam going to flght—(applause)—and if we are beaten, I would rather be beaten in the last ditch with the banner and the flag of my Church still waving over my head.' (Loud applause). Lord DYNEVOR then moved the following resolu- tion : "That this meeting declares its emphatic pro- test against the Bill to disestablish, dismember, and disendow the National Church in the four Welsh dioceses." _ . _</p>
        <p>Mr NV A WHEEL, in seconding, referred to the follow- ing statement in the speech delivered at Warrington last week by</p>
      </div>
