      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>THEATRE OF THE WAR IN THE I BALKANS.</head>
        <p>THE THEATRE OF THE WAR IN THE I BALKANS.</p>
        <p>We reproduce above a sketch map showing the nature of the country around the town of Adrianople, its outer forts, and, as far as possible, the disposition of the armies of the countries concerned. The numerous forts that guard the town have for many years been prepared by the Turks to offer a stubborn resistance, and demonstrate the great importance Turkey places on its defence. Many experts describe it as the key of the defence of Constantinople.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>MR. TOM WING :</head>
        <p>MR. TOM WING :</p>
        <p>"Touching upon th;Cineraion"i4 the Disestablish ment of the Church in Wales, Mr Wing said he saw on the walls in the town a bill advertising a meeting to oppose Welsh Disestablishment. He wanted to ask those people, candidly and considerately, what was the use of holding a public, meeting if they would not allow public opinion to express itself ? A public meeting to protest meant that Parliament should listen to their prayer. The people of Wales had already spoken plainly on the subject—as plainly as any portion of the king. dom." It was very strange, commented Mr Wheel, that be bad lived in \Vales nearly fifty years but he had yet to learn that the people of Wales wanted Disestablish. went or Disendowment. (Applause.) He had worked in Wales, he had addressed hundreds of meet- ings in Wales, and he had spoken to thousands of his fellow-workmen on the question, but could not get a decent reason from them as to why they voted for that question. If it was placed fairly and squarely before the Welsh people, as the specific issue without any side issues, there would be no majority for the Bill. (Applause.) He was a Radical, but on this question he put his Church before his politics. (Applause.) Nearly 600,00 Welsh people had signed the petition against the Bill—thousands of Non- conformists had signed it in defiance of the chapel screw which, they might take his word for it,had been applied in many cases. Appealing to those who believed that the Radical social programme was the right one for the country, Mr Wheel said the danger was that unless this Bill were shifted out of the way, it was going to shift Radicalism. (Applause.) Personally he attached far greater importance to Establishment than to Endow- ment. If they took away Establishment from the Church, then, for God's sake, let them establish one of the Nonconformist bodies. The unassailable facts elicited by the Royal Commission were that the Church was doing a good work to-day, that it was the only religious organization in Wales that was making any headway, and that the Church in Wales was making more rapid progress than the Church in England. The Bill could not do any good,—unless making "jobs " could be regarded as doing good. To his mind, how- ever, it was a rotten policy to take away the clergy- man's eirnings to make "jobs " for pals. Having described the Bill as dishonest, cowardly and un-English Mr Wheel wound up with the declaration that it was an attack upon religion generally and not upon the Church in Wales alone. (Applause.) The resolution was carried unanimously. Mr 0 LOFTHOUSE, in moving a vote of thanks to the speakers, said he was old-fashioned enough to believe in the validity of the Eighth Commandment and to believe that it was still binding upon all men, even upon Cabinet Ministers. (Applause.) How any men of reputed honour and honesty could bring in a Bill to take Church property away was almost incon- ceivable. The measure sought a Parliamentary right to do a moral and legal wrong and, as such, he could not believe it would be carried. (Applause). Mr. BROADBENT (Lymm) seconded. He said signs were not wanting, that conviction was being carried Into the minds of their Nonconformist brethren and that the Bill was being killed. (Applause). The vote was heartily passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>FEARFUL ITCHING ON LIMBS.</head>
        <p>FEARFUL ITCHING ON LIMBS.</p>
        <p>Heather Villa, Conon Bridge, Ross-shire, N.B.— "Two years ago I suffered an itching on both my lea and arms. It would be that bad at times that I would stop scratching myself. The fearful itching on my arms and legs Just almost pained me mad. I would scratch at them until I would take the blood out, then my skin was the same as if it was burned it Was so sore. I got all kinds of ointment but they were of no use, so I saw the Cuticura advertisement in the paper and I got a cake of Cuticura Soap and a box of Cuticura Ointment. I got easy with the first applica- tion of them and before I finished them I was a lot better so I bought another cake of Cuticura Soap and box of Cuticura Ointment and got alright." (Signed) Donald Morrison, Dec. Ifith, 1911. Cuticura Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists everywhere. A sample of each with 32-p. book post free from nearest depot : Newbery, 27, Charterhouse sq., London ; R Towns &amp; Co., Sydney, N. S. W.; Lennon, Ltd„ Cape Town; Muller, Maclean &amp; Co., Calcutta and Bombay ; "Cuticura," Bolton, U.S.A. Tender-faced men should shave with Cuticura Soap Shaving Stick. Sample free.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Bazaars.—The bazaar season has again begun. Like poverty, with which it has a real connection, it remains with us more or less throughout the winter months. Institutions of all kinds,—churches,chapelsand charities —find it a ready means of replenishing an impoverished exchequer. A Bishop has said rather sadly, that a bazaar seems the only way of raising money. And since money must be bad, the bazaar, and perhaps the whist drive, must be tolerated. It affords a pleasant social oppor- tunity, end enables people to give in kind, what they could not, or would not, give in money. Women revel In this particular form of amusement, but the mere man goes because be cannot help himself, and when he finally departs, with the resolve to return no more, he carries with him, as has been said, " the comforting knowledge that his loss hes been the everlasting gain of the object for which the bazaar is held." The side shows are semetimes of a kind which does not harmonise well vrah the aims of the promoters and undoubtedly lends occasionally to needless expenditure on the part of those who cannot afford it. Still criticise it as we may, the bazaar fulfills a useful function and succeeds in raising considerable sums of money for deserving objects, albeit in a circuitous way. The Divorce Laws.—The Divorce Law Reform Union of which Sir Arthur Conan Doyle is president, and Miss Stella Brown acts as secretary, has no great faith in Royal Commissions. There was a Commission appointed in 1858, when divorce was first permitted ; and even now, sixty years later, men are accorded a privileged position under the law. The report of the Royal Commission is expected early next month, but whatever it may propose, it is thought there will still be much work to be donel and the Union proposes to carry on an active campaign with the object of rousing publics opinion on the subject. The Union aims at placing relief from unhappy marriages within reach of the poor, as well as the rich, and is altogether opposed to separations on the ground that the parties concerned should be either married or unmarried. The separation period might be, at any rate, limited, as it is in Norway. If husband and wife want to part, Norwegian law grants them a divorce without enquiring into the reason. It only requires that they must go before the Conciliation Board, and that a year must elapse before the divorce is made final, and the entire cost of the procedure need not exceed 5s to 105. Letter-Writing.—Recently Dr Arthur Holmes, of Pennsylvania University, recommended the exchange of love-letters between students, as a means of acquiring the art of composition. The Professor, no doubt, was thinking more of the men than the women, for when it comes to letter-writieg, the " mere man " invariably shows his inferiority. The average man expresses him- self awkwardly, and with difficulty; hole too careful of his dignity, and is impeded by self-consciousness, or is perhaps stupid. There are, of course, exceptions. Samuel Richardson, the novelist, was one, and his gift of letter-writing was often placed at the service of young women, for whose correspondence with their sweethearts he often furnished not only the words, but the sentiments. He lived amidst a coterie of feminine worshippers, and it was his experience as a" complete letter-writer," that led to the publication of" Pamela." In those days of costly postage, people expected some- thing worth paying for fiben they received a letter, but cheap prepaid postage has altered all that. If one is writing to a friend, and is at a standstill with the pen, the simplest way out of the difficulty is to imagine the presence of that person and proceed as one would talk. The Smash-Everything Policy.—The Conciliation , Bill was defeated in the House of Commons this year solely because some twenty-five or thirty avowed suffragists voted against it, as a protest against the window smashing campaign, and it is apparently because Mr and Mrs Pethwick Lawrence recognise the futility of such tactics that they have been compelled to part company with the Pankhursts. At the Albert Hall last week Mrs Pankhurst, who remains at the head of the militants, once more preached the gospel of "smash everything." but whatever virtue violence may have had at first as an impassioned demonstration, as a habit it ceases to be dignified or impresaive and with- out a shadow of doubt it does infinite harm to the ' cause of woman suffrage. There is now little chance of carrying a woman suffrage amendment to the Franchise Bill. The only party in the House of Commons that i 3 I at all united in favour of woman suffrage is the Labour Party hut it has now incurred the wrath of the ultra. militants, because it still votes with the Government. It is not by tactless tactics such as these, that great causes are won, but, apparently the scenes of violence we have witnessed, degrading as they are to womanhood as a whole, are to go on indefinitely. Winter Hats, Veils and Chenille Fringe.—Small head- gear is quite the rage in Paris. The bowler shape has proved so becoming, that it will be worn throughout the winter. Quite a new shape has a broadish brim that resembles the shape worn by men. Round crowned bats in Austrian velour, are proving attractive. For country wear the red bat with black brim and rice versa is smart, and goes well with the severe type of suit. Veils are still worn over hats, and these are very grace- fully draped, The small veil that only covers the face is also worn. White hats require white veils and quite a novelty is one of net, bordered with crepe de chine. A revival to be noted this winter is the old-fashioned chenille fringe,which milliners are using for the trimm- ing of the now plush hats. It is bunched up and allowed to drop over the brim and the effect is excellent. It also borders sashes,and finishes muffs,scarves and stoles.</p>
        <p>Old Lace for Waistcoats.—Tbere are many one-piece dresses that simulate the tailor-made, and these possess a novel feature in the shape of a quaint .little waistcoat which shows for two or three inches below the waist- band. Any piece of old brocade makes charming little waistcoats of this kind,so does cretonne,stamped velvet, damask, or any rich material. Sometimes a waistcoat Is simulated by means of strips of old lace mounted on satin. There is always a guimpe of mousseline or net above the waistcoat. High necks are the rule this winter, even the tailored revers are quite high, and as the small opening at the neck leaves but little room,the jabot will he banished—a matter for regret. Material Muffs.— Velvet, plain, corded or stamped makes the most delightful material muffs. They are generally of the floppy, huge kind, that are so fashion- able this year. A fur mull may be small or big, but a material muff must be gigantic In its proportions. These often have wide frills of taffeta or chiffon down the sides and all round the muff. Some are very limp, others are well padded, as is almost everything that concerns dress this winter, there is a wide choice in shapes, styles, and trimmings. Evening Muff.—The evening muff Is a very pretty accessory that ought to be warmly welcomed, for it is both sensible and comfortable, besides adding not n little to the effect of the toilette with which it is worn. Made in chiffon, of the same or a contrasting colour to the dress, these dainty adjuncts are trimmed with woollen flowers, and perhaps a little fur, to match that on the dress. Swansdown rnakes a charming trimming for these evening muffs. The flap shape is often used for large muffs for day and evening wear. There is a small bag inside the muff for the hands, and the flop is only slightly padded, and this is certainly the easiest shape to make at home.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>HOUSEWIVES NEED ZAM-BUK.</head>
        <p>HOUSEWIVES NEED ZAM-BUK.</p>
        <p>Hands and Arms once Covered with Eczema.</p>
        <p>Miss Nellie, A. M. Fisher, of 3, Malt House Cottages, Ha%erten, Devon, suffered so severely from itchy sores on her hands and arms that she couldn't work. Thanks, however, to a spendid Zam-Bnk cure, she is to-day working cheerfully, free from the torturing outbreak. To a " Western Times" reporter Mies Fisher said :</p>
        <p>" Itchy pimples first came on the backs of my hands, which got very sore and inflamed. From there they spread up my arms and all over my hands and fingers, which were a sickening sight. The itching was so fierce that I lay awake hours in bed suffering terribly. I couldn't do my housework,and daren't put my hands in water because of the awful burning and smarting sensation that followed. If I got hot my skin seemed to dry up, and smarted unbearably. " I was told I had a severe attack of eczema, and was advised to try different ointments, salves, creams, etc. The hard, inflamed eruptions got worse, how- ever. I was tortured day and night, and only found relief when I began to use Zam-Buk. It was reading of other cases which Zam-Buk had cured that caused me to get some of this new balm from the chemist's. " I should never have believed it possible, if I hadn't seen it myself, that anything could work such a wonderful change as Zam-Buk did in my ease ! In a very short time time the intense itching had gone and the inflamed pimples were dying away. I kept up the Zam-Buk treatment, which was so soothing that I got plenty of rest and sleep. All inflammation was finally drawn from my skin, the pimples disappeared, and my bands and arms were quite healthy and free from disfigurement. "Thanks to my splendid Zam-Buk cure, I now have no pain or irritation, I can do all my work again, and water and heat have no bad effect at all on my skin." No housewife should be without a l/I1 box of Zam- Bilk with which to dress her hands and bare arms daily and keep soreness away.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>CHESHIRE COUNTY INSURANCE COMMITTEE. SANATORIUM BENEFIT.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE COUNTY INSURANCE COMMITTEE. SANATORIUM BENEFIT.</p>
        <p>The Cheshire County Insurance Committee desire it to be publicly known that they are prepared to receive applications for this benefit from persons who are entitled to receive such benefit. The committee are, however, considerably restricted in the matter of the particular form of benefit which can be given to appli- cants but every case coming to their knowledge will be carefully considered on all its merits and every endeavour made to provide a form of treatment suit- able to the case subject to the regulations of the Insur- ance Commissioners and of the Local Government Board It should be clearly understood that the Insurance Committee can incur no responsibility whatever in the matter of sanatorium benefit unless application for benefit has been made in the proper manner and all the preliminaries laid down by the Act and the regulations of the commissioners have received compliance. Applicants should in the first instance communicate with Mr F T West, 100, Northgate-street, Chester, Clerk to the County Insurance Committee who will then forward the necessary forms of application, &amp;c.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>Rev. R. M. Mitchell and Increases of Salaries.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held at the Workhouse on Wednesdarr Mr E L Bent presiding. There were also present Miss Broadbent, Revs. T It Matthews and A Mitchell, Messrs A J Jolley (deputy-chairman), W Gil', W Houghton, R Lee, G Mather, A Brooks, W Furness, A Hughes. J A Rushwortb, M W Seton, T Ritchie, VY C Brooks, A Bottomley (clerk), R I Partridge (assistant clerk), W.. 1 Brown (master) and C E Lawrence (suPer- intendent of Cottage Homes). THE COST OF STIMULANTS. The Rev A M MITCHEEL salled attention to an account of Messrs Rowson and Sons for wines and spirits. He pointed out that the amount charged for. the month of September was £l5 9s 9d and he wishe,e, to know whether the account was a monthly one. IL so, it would come to about £lBO a year. He knew of a Union with about the same number of inmates in whi • the annual expenditure on stimulants was LI 17s 7d.; Was the amount for September to be taken as average ? The CHAIRMAN : The account imendered. Do yoR move that it be not paid or referred back ? The Rev AM MITCHELL: I don't want to depri anybody of his money, but I want information about / Is this a monthly account? The CHAIRMAN: That is so. The Rev A M MITCHELL : Is this the average? The CLERK : I think I can safely say "No can hardly term it a monthly account. The maste orders these supplies when he is running short of stock! The account in question may just refer to one moo but be may go for a couple of months or more befo he orders any more. You would have to get tb• yearly total and average it if you wanted to see ho much the monthly average is. The Rev A M Mitchell : I asked for a year' statement but it was refused. That is where it corn • in. They can get statements in other Unions, but vi cannot get them here. The CLERK : You can get anything from me on tb direction of the Board. The Rev A M MITCHELL : I am not finding fan! ' with you, lam finding fault with the Board. 1 thin this Board has erred very grievously in not grantin the return I asked for. I hope presently they Wl' come to a better mind. THE FRoPOSED NEW BUILDING. A deputation consisting of Messrs George Matht, (chairman of the Building Committee). A Brooks, w. C Brooks along with the architect (Mr S Owen) an the Clerk attended at the offices of the Local Govern., meet Board on October 11th with reference to tb proposal of the Guardians to erect a building at tb Workhouse for the accommodation of male and female epileptic patients. Before dealing with the plans sub°- mated, Mr Chapman (chief clerk to the Local Govern. ment Board) mentioned that the Mental Deficiency BM which was now under consideration and, in hi opinion likely to be passed, would take awe from Boards of Guardians the care and control o imbeciles and epileptics and,uuder those circumstance* he asked the deputation to request the GuardiartS carefully to consider the advisability of proceeding lA-, the present time with the erection of a building ' specially designed to accommodate this particular class of patient, as in the event of the Bill passing in It present form, when the transfer was effected, the Guardians would be left with a building on their hands which could not be very well adapted for the accom- modation of any other class of patient. They were fully aware that the present accommodation was most inadequate and unsuitable and that better provision was urgently needed, and they suggested that a building be erected more after the style of the aged and infirm block, so that at any time it could be easily adapted for the accommodation of other classes of patients. The Building Committee recommended that the architect be instructed to prepare plans of an alterna- native scheme on lines suggested by the Local Govern' ment Board.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said it meant practically starting over again with the building scheme. The Rev A M MITCHELL said it seemed that the Local Government Board were not very keen on the achertler and under the circumstances it might be wise to defer It and hand it over to their successors in April. The lexpense was very serious and the undertaking was a very large one for an almost expiring Board to take in band. It was saddling their successors with a responsl‘ bility which they would not altogether appreciate. , The minutes were approved and it was understooa that the question will be further ccnsidered by the Building Committee. INCREASES OF SALARY. REV. A. H. MITCHELL'S PROTEST. Mr 0 MATHER, in accordance with a recommends; tion of the House Committee, approved at the last Board meeting, moved that the salary of Mr Samuel S Hack, head attendant on male imbeciles, be increased from £3O to £35 per annum. The Rev A M MITCHELL moved an amendment that', the salary be increased by £lO. Mr Hack had the, most trying work of any offieer in the House and he had put in three or four years' service. Miss BIiOADBENT seconded. Mr JOLLEY said it was highly improper to re-intro' duce the question after it had teen discussed on It, merits and a decision arrived fit. Miss BROADBENT said she would like Mr Hack t., have a £lO advance, but as the majority of member° were in favour of £5 she fell in with that. The amendment fell to the ground and the advanoee was agreed to. Mr MATHER then moved that the salary of Dr WOl4 Megaw, resident medical officer, be increased from £1 to £l5O per annum. Mr SETON seconded. The Rev A M MITCHELL said he did not kn" whether the Board appreciated the humour of linking the two resolutions together. In the one oar, they had a man who had served them for Ye" and they gave him £5, while in the other ease a MIL who had been with them barely six months was :•'' £25. He did not know whether the member,: were aware that there was an officer in the Union vivo" had served twenty years and had never had an advani It was said that the doctor was not sufficiently Psj; He (Mr Mitchell) did not say that he was, but be say that the doctor, so far as his memory served hil°o was glad enough to accept the position and he osnr, e there as a beginner. He received £125 a year and email.' men ts, making a total of £l9O, which was very good t° begin with. Having argued that efforts should havt been made to induce some competition for the Mr Mitchell said he saw no earthly reason why, fall,„,,e_'°° a medical man, a lady doctor should not be appoin'—t: es he had been done in other Unions. He did think it was claimed that the new doctor was keelslee expenses down. The bill of Messrs Howson and 01 for stimulants seemed to indicate an increase. At to last meeting it was mentioned that the doctor was tg,. be given facilities to pursue his studies and atten? lectures. How much were those facilities worth There must be some money value behind them, or wbei b. should the Finance Committee have dealt With t , point? Mr Joetelt : These facilities are not on the minute UP The Rev A M MITCHELL : But they came Mr JOLLEY : They are not on the minutes Of morning. The CHAIRMAN : They are omitted because they We dealt with and settled at the last meeting. The Rev A M MITCHELL : You are going to ~g4f., £l5O a year to a man who will be more or less a timer. The CHAIRMAN ; I would not like to say that. ife el The Rev A M ercueLe : Ile is not going t° Igoe% _ the whole of his time to the work. You are gilnow him these facilities to go away, and I want to who is to be responsible. Mr JOLLEY (warmly) : Mr Mitchell has nledesj' number of etatements which arc very misleading unfair. He is wasting our time and I formally 01°' that the question be now put. The Rev A M MITCHELL: No, no. Mr MATHER second it. ,el5O The Rev A M MITCHELL: You are giving year for a half-timer. The CHAIRMAN: The Board perfectly understetta- the position you are taking up. sident The Rev A M MITCHELL: Originally a re essery. medical officer was appointed because it was nee time. to have a doctor on the spot the whole of tue_ Now you are going to pay a doctor who will be a timer £l5O a year. t° The CHAIRMAN: It is tot) bad. I asked eon allow the Board to deckle the question. They g 0110 understand the position you are taking up. Or • The question was then put to the meeting., the Mitchell protesting against the closuring discussion. thee The increase of Dr Megaw's salary was. approved, Mr Mitchell being the only dissentleare EARLESTOWN GROCERS' GRIEVANCE. Littee At a meeting of the Newton Relief Con letter - letter was read from the Earlestown Grocers: ,A,7,tidest tion with reference to a grievance as to the diStr,,l'eughi' of poor relief tickets. These, it was contencleo_, Dro- to be allotted to the different tradesmen in erin_7,4`.o to portion, as far as possible. It was resolved toll3ber 14fr. the effect that the Guardians are of opinioen ,rint,ereste Bowes (relieving officer) had acted in the b oiven of the poor, and further that in future ordels,u_e4tioal to one shop for a month at a time, in alPa order.</p>
      </div>
