        <p>LYIVI3I.</p>
        <p>.rt,,„ th, ". ORA RY A UTHORITY.—Amongst other items i4fts",Pleted at the Sale Police Sessions on Monday was kt of a temporary license to sell intoxicating liquors "Jolley Thresher Inn," Broom Edge, which was anted to Mr Herbert Munro. itrUITIVE METHODIST ENDEAVOURERS.—On Mon- uight the members of the Primitive Methodist ftbnior C.E. Society were pleased to receive a visit 0;11 Mrs Redford of Manchester, who conducted a 44168 of successful mission services at the above church IpterlY two years ago. Mrs Redford gave a most be resting and helpful address on "Stocktaking," thlng as the basis of her remarks the words "What bast iied4 in the house? a pot of oil," (H. Kings, iv. 2). Her e ress was enriched by the narration of some vivid 441)eriences she had encountered in the course of her pv ugelistio work. The Rev RW B Whiteway presided er an excellent attendance. lla_ll-rRCJI ARMY VAN.—One of the Church Army vans Peen in the Lymm parish for the past few weeks, u'le to remain until the end of the present month, its position will be taken up for three weeks in Ce!,.°nehtrington Parish. The van is in charge of tin Pickersgill and his able assistant Mr. Christian, hetet their praiseworthy efforts have up to the present 10 chiefly centred upon the Statham and Newfield thWities. Excellent work has been done by the officers t:tigh visiting homes, distributing wholesome liters- k!, holding missions, and lantern services and lectures 1)0th children and adults. ryi c.,VESI" FESTIVAL.—The harvest sermons of the Ruer-lane Baptist Church were preached on Sunday et I, Cop Y the Rev Howard James, 8.A., of Brighton Grove eo„."3ge, Manchester, whose discourses have been highly tkanented upon. The interior of the Church had been tiCkefelly decorated with flowers and was well attended oth the morning and evening services. At the %Liter service Mr James preached on the subject of 1.‘„,?5,3" (John iv. 7 and 8), and Miss Mary Brundrett ;!_?; Pleasant rendering of the solo "There rises ,a qr.. The theme at night was "The New Birth, ' ' lii., 5, and the soloist, Mr Gordon Dale, who sang 4.24 of Love." Miss H Brundrett was an efficient etruist. The collections were in aid of the Chur3l, -nsey lilto • sh kTit or MRS. S. J. Kittqzts.—The main topic of Itersation in I.ymm on Thursday was the surprising unexpected death of Mrs S J Kenzie, of Lymm While. On Wednesday night Mrs Kenzie went to a tLt drive which was being held in connection with 36,0andy-lane Club and whilst participating in the ip„"ls was seized with, as was thought,a faint. Recover- a few minutes she signified her intention of con- her game, but in a short while was seized with Vond and more serious attack. The" drive" was üßht to an abrupt conclusion, Mrs Kenzie was taken 641:44 end the doctor sent for, but the seizure was fatal Ihe Passed away at 2 o'clock on Thursday morning. Itol.'3?rne years Mrs Kenzie has kept a glass and china in the village and was a well-known caterer at of the local functions, one of her orders being 1)(it annual Primrose tea. Ihe deceased had no children teti'7,as devoted to her husband, who has been the 14ent of many expressions of sympathy.</p>
