      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>DRUIDS' PRIZE DISTRIBUTION.—There was an inter- esting gathering in connection with the Order of Druids at the Penketh Institute on Monday evening, the occa- sion being the distribution of the cup and prizes awarded in the recent bowling main. The Gilbert Greenell cup was woo by Mr W Goulding and it was presented to him by Mr Griffiths, master of the Council school. The District Officer, Dr Murray and others were present. A very enjoyable musical programme was submitted. WESLEY GUILD.—The Penketh Wesley Guild com- menced its winter cession with a social on Saturday. The social section committee had charge of the arrangements and right well had they worked to secure a success for the opening night. There was an attendance ofclose upon one hundred The meeting having been opened with a hymm and prayer offered by the Rev H Middlewood (president of the Wesley Guild), the Vice-President (Mr G Carman), gave the friends a hearty invitation to join the Guild. The musical part of the programme was opened with a quartette by members of the Penketh Tannery Prize Brass Band. Songs were given very efficiently by Miss Dodds, Mr Elliott, and a duet by Messrs H and A Plumpton. Mr W Cross played a selection on the mandoline. Refreshments were provided, Miss A Davies having charge of the catering and Mr T H Ambler of the games, these two being the joint secretaries of the social section of the Guild. Mrs T H Ambler had charge of the decoration of the tables and Mr I Hampson decorated the room with the kindly assistance of the whole committee. Miss Mary Watson was the accompanist. The company dispersed about 10.30 p.m. after having an enjoyable time. Mr G Dale, Silverdale, Penketh, is the general secretary. BAND OF HoPE.—On Friday evening at the Penketh Wesleyan School the members of the above had a pleasant time owing to the kindness of one of the greatest friends of the Band of Hope work—Mrs Dodds. The programme consisted of songs by a number of the members. Refreshments were handed round and games were afterwards indulged in, prizes being given to the winners. The officials of the Penketh Band of Hope and Mrs E V Walker were also present and assisted in helping to make it an interesting time for the children. Miss Baines was the accompanist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN CHURCH.—In common with the whole the Wesleyan Church, special children's services we held on Sunday last. After the sermon in the monde the preacher, Mr J Bailey, referred to the sndde decease of Mr E J Guest, 8.A., J.P., and to the special regard in which he was held by the Padgate congrega- tion both as a talented local preacher and as *Christian gentleman. The flag on the church was flown at half- mast, and at the conclusion of the service the organist (Mr Weaver) played the Dead March in Saul. During the afternoon, the Rev E Morgan gave an address and in the evening occupied the pulpit as preacher.</p>
        <p>HARVEST FESTIVAL.—The annual harvest thanks- giving services were held at Christ Church on Sunday. The preacher in the morning being the Rev Canon Stevenson and in the evening the pulpit was occupied by the Rev G C Briggs. The anthem for the morning was Simper's " Let us thank the Lord our God," in which Mr S Oddy and Master James Young toek the solos. In the evening service the choir rendered the anthem "Thou shalt show us wonderful things " (Spinney), the sweet voice of Meister J Young, as soloist being again much appeciated. Mr Blackwell presided at the organ with his usual skill. The interior of the church had been tastefully decorated by the ladies of the congregation, those responsible for the several features vying with each other in friendly rivalry for the best effect. Large attendances were the order of the day and good collections were taken in aid of the Church Fabric Repair Fund. VISIT OF REV. TREVOR H. DAVIES.—In connection with the Wesley Guild, the visit of the Rev Trevor H Davies, which took place on Thursday evening, had been lOoked forward to with pleasurable anticipation for a considerable time—in fact ever since his visit a year ago.—ln the afternoon a good number in the church listened with close attention to an eloquent and thoughtful sermon.—ln the evening the rev gentleman lectured in the large schoolroom to a large and sympathetic audience on the subject " The Church, its modern problems and challenge."—ln the course of his address, which was a model of choice diction, appro- priate anecdote and sparkling epigram, the lecturer said one of the features of the age was "Impatience of personal restraint." The extravagance of luxury is as bad as the exigences of poverty. Puritanism is behind all happiness. There was more real happiness under Oliver Cromwell than under Charles 11. What no one had ever tried was to put a Puritan inside a cavalier. When that happened they would have the ideal man. They needed the liveliness and buoyancy of the cavalier with the restraint of the Puritan.—Dr Ferguson, who presided, said it was the first time he had occupied the position of chairman at Padgate, but be hoped it would not be the last,—A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to lecturer and chairman, on the proposition of Mr Alderman Arthur Bennett, seconded by Mr F Wright, and submitted by the Rev E Morgan. PAD G ATE DISTRICT NTJRSI: ASSOCIATION.—It is to be regretted that the general public do not evince greater interest in the District Nurse Association, the annual meeting of which was held in the Wesleyan school on Tuesday evening. Mr W F Newton presided and besides the members of the committee only a hand- ful of subscribers was present. The report presented showed that the year's work had been very arduous and had involved considerable anxiety to the officers and committee owing, principally, to the breakdown in health of Nurse Street and the difficulty of arrang- ing a temporary service pending the appoint- ment of a permanent nurse. This has now been done. Nurse Montgomery, who rendered splendid work and by her kindness and skill endeared herself to all in the district during her late term of service,has now returned and been re-appointed. The statement of accounts shows that the Association is paying its way but the need for further support is urgent. Despite the fact that for about three moi.ttbs there was no nurse available nearly one thousand visits have been paid to the sick and if one could but realize all that the attention of a skilled nurse means in the sick room surely the funds would be forthcoming to place the Association on a sound financial basis. The President Mrs Newton) the treasurer (Miss E Richard- son) and the members of the committee were thanked for their services and re-elected for the current year. Thanks were also recorded to Mr J R Young, Messrs Bellian and Davies, Bennett and Bennett and others for assistance rendered and gifts of linen, &amp;c. CONCERT.—A very successful concert was given by the scholars of the Church school on Thursday evening For several years, this once highly popular entertain- ment has not been held but upon the announcement of its resusitation the ready sale of all the available tickets proved that \ the interval had not caused the event to lose any of its popularity. The withdrawal of the curtain disclosed for the first items—" Melody " (Ruben,tein), and " Don't forget the old folks " (Judi), a large ensemble of beautifully dressed scholars, who sang the two songs in a charming manner. The infants were responsible for several items which called for applause,They, included "Fisher Lads "Good even- ing." " Poppies and a selection of nursery rhymes. A recall was de mended and granted for " Swedish folk dances "by 3 number of junior scholars. It need hardly be said that seven 1 oys ,entered with zest into the "amateur barbers," tinpr actions provoking much merriment. "Tie musical farmer," a song by senior scholars, was recived with much applause. An operetta "Santa Claus," was presented in a very effective and charming manner, and called forth warm encomiums from those present. After an interval of five minutes, sixteen girls presentedon a very beautifulynanner a series of evolutions, "Moments" Lheir gay attire and graceful movements combining to make a brilliant effect. In the song " Mynhur and his little Dutch lady,' Mr Wilfred Povey and Miss Nora Blackwell hit off the Dutch characteristics to a nicety—but surely their voices were English. Local characters were ably handled bv Mr Wilfred Fryer alias Larry Lauder in his song "For me! for me!" The concluding item, an operetta, "The Persecuted Policeman," was presented with great verve by a num- ber of elder scholars, the character acting being parti- cularly fine. During the programme the Vicar referred appreciatively to the efforts of the teachers and parents in the work of instructing and dressing the scholars. The concert was repeated, if anything, more successfully last evening. The proceeds are on behalf of the children's prize fund. Padgatonians all hope for the annual occurrence of this popular entertainment.</p>
        <p>SIDESLIP INTO GLAMORGAN CANAL. Whilst cycling alongside the Glamorgan Canal. at Crrdiff on Thursday, John McGillivia, an engineering apprentice,side-slipped into the water and was drowned.</p>
        <p>"It looks as if the race were becoming better tempered" was the jocular comment of the Mayor at Warrington's third annual baby show on Saturday. His Worship was alluding to the fact that the precious mites of humanity whose mothers had proudly brought them to Parr Hall appeared much less fretful than babies are wont to be when on view amid unaccus- tomed surroundings. At previous shows the ears of visitors to the hall had been assailed with a lusty chorus of infantile wailing, but this time I the rising hopes of Warrington homes were, for the most part, complacently restful—for which judges and visitors were thankful. It was gratifying to see the healthy, well-cared-for appearance of the babies—the most eloquent testimony that one could desire to the worth and use'ulness of the show. Numerically there has been a falling-off each year, the entries on this occasion totalling 162. In one respect the promoters have not yet fully achieved their object—and that is in the desire to enlist the interest of the very poorest parents, for a great amount of apathy towards the project still remains to be dispelled in those quarters of the town where drab, sordid surroundings breed hopelessness and indifference, even on such a vital question as the efficient up-bringing of the young. However, gradual headway is being made, even in the most unpromising neighbourhoods, and the show is destined to have untold benefit in staying that "massacre of the innocents " which goes to swell the infantile mortality returns. The Medical Officer of Health (Dr J Coote Elibbert), and his staff had, as usual, devoted an infinitude of patient and willitT work to the preliminary arrangements and all the babies had been weighed in advance. At two o'clock on Saturday afternoon the Parr Ball was besieged by mothers and babies, and the corridors of the building were soon congested with mail carts and perambulators. The body of the hall was comfort- ably arranged for the convenience of the competitors who were soon placed in their respective classes, according to the ages of the babies. Ten local medical men undertook the judging and no one envied them them their task of discriminating where</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>WARRINGTON'S BABY</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S BABY</p>
        <p>ManekeBter COUI</p>
