      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>STRETTON.</head>
        <p>STRETTON.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF MRS HUGH DARBYSHIRE.—A well- known and highly respected resident of Stretton, has passed away in the 'person of Mrs Hugh Derbyshire of Sheperoft. The interment took place in Stretton Churchyard on Monday afternoon, the Rev the Hon C F Cross officiating in the presence of a large number of mourners and friends. A large number of beautiful wreaths were forwarded. The funeral arrangements were carried out by Messrs J and W Dutton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>STOCKTON HEMPH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEMPH.</p>
        <p>SALE OF WORK AT HILL CLIFFE.—The proceeds of this effort amounted to £l4O. In a concert arranged by Mrs Jas Kenworthy (choir leader) the following artistes took part : Mrs Jas Kenworthy (contralto), Mrs Longbottom (elocutionist), Mr Warburton (baritone), Mr Humphreys (violin), Mr Derbyshire (recitation). Miss Parkinson accompanied, and the church choir rendered two glees. A souvenir of the sale (arranged by Mr Jas Kenworthy) who was the author of the "History of Hill Cliffs Church," was highly appreciated. Ou the first page of this booklet it is recorded that a place of worship has existed on the site of the present buildings for at least 400 years (the first minister dying in 1594).</p>
        <p>HILL CLIFFE AND STOCKTON HEATH 'UNITED BAND OF 1101'144—Success attended the first entertainment of the session, which was held in the Wesleyan school- room on Monday evening. Mr Walter Twiss presided, and was supported by the Rev W A Beckett, superin- tendent Wesleyan minister, who delivered an address. A good programme was given, including recitations by Sarah Gamble, Hilda Adamson, Emily Williams, Lizzie Brooks. Agnes Phillips; songs by Gladys Pemberton. Doris Speakman, and by Miss Sophie Cross, Miss E Evans and Miss Whitmore. Emily Rustage contributed a harp solo with good effect. A dialogue was given by Gertie CHITA and Gladys Pemberton. Miss Amy Littlewood, "The Band of Hope Queen" contributed two recitation. The humour ot the evening was supplied by Mr Gordon Starkey, whose songs and jests caused much amusement. The various musical items were accompanied on the piano by Miss Turner, Miss Noden, Miss Starkey and Master Cyril Pemberton.</p>
        <p>Beautifully made frame fitted with lenses to suit any sight 17/6, as sold in Liverpool and Manchester for 35/. •</p>
        <p>PLEASE NOTE.—MR. WATSON attends on Monday and Tuesday from 11 to 4-30; Wednesday 11 to 5-50 Thursday 11 to 1; Friday and Saturday 11 to 5-30. Hours of Business: 9-30 to 7-30; Saturday: 9-30 to 9-30.</p>
        <p>•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>GROUP OF PRIZE WINNERS.</head>
        <p>A GROUP OF PRIZE WINNERS.</p>
        <p>the standard of merit was AO uniformly high. "They are a splendid lot of babies," was the verdict of a judge who chatted with our representative. "Although the numbers are not so high as before, the quality is really fine." The mothers were provided with tea by a large and representative ladies' committee, under the presidency of Mrs H Roberts. The prizes were well chosen and took the form of useful household articles, each winner also receiving a certificate as a permanent Teminder of the success. The champion babies for this year are Lydia Fowler, of 30. Grafton-street, aged five months, and weighing 191 b 3oz ; and Frank Wilson, 33, Orford-street, aged eleven-and-a-half months, and weighing 291 b loz. These infants were awarded the special prizes given for the best-developed and most carefully-tended babies in the show.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR, in performing the opening ceremony, mentioned the fact that the show owed its inception to Councillor Wilson,whose baby it was—(laughter)—and it had been adopted by the Health Committee.(Hear,hear.) One of the results of the show was to present prizes to the winner; but the real objects that the promoters had in view were to encourage mothers to look after their babies;to see to the general home life; to improve the condition of their children ; and to promote the comfort and health of the family as a whole. He likened the far-reaching effects of the show to the cast- ing of a pebble into the placid stream, producing a succession of circles. His worship pointed out that the medical men had a very difficult task to perform, but they did it conscientiously. Those who did cot get prizes must not be disappointed, because the work was not entirely lost, as they had the satisfaction of knowing that their children benefited by the efforts they had made in competing. He was glad to see that there were entries from some of the poorer districts in the borough, and emphasised the fact that the promoters desired to encourage to the utmost of their Power the very poor to bring their children. At the close of the judging Councillor Wilson dis- tributed the prizes. Subsequently the Mayor moved a vote of thanks to the judges and the Ladies' Com- mittee. He also read a letter from Alderman James Evans (the chairman of the Health Committee), who wrote : "My sympathies are with the objects of the promoters, viz., to lessen the infantile mortality of the town. I hope you may have a very successful gather- ing and that much good may result from the effort."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>WIN WICK.</head>
        <p>WIN WICK.</p>
        <p>SMOKING CONCERT.-A smoking concert was held, by members of the Asylum Workers Unions and a few friends, in the Swan Hotel, on Saturday night. The occasion was the departure of Mr 0 Gibson who has been a prominent and hard working member of the 11600 for a considerable time, and was the recipient of a presentation for his services some time ago. He has now gone to undertake the duties of assistant secre- tary to the union, along with the present secretary the Rev H M S Bankhart at Manchester. A very pleasant evening was spent, those present being entertained with musical items by Messrs It Chandler, 0 Washington, Woods, G Richards, D C Hughes and by the Orpheus Glee Society of which Mr Gibson was a member. Messrs J W Taylor and T Greenhalgh were accom- panists. Mr B Hambleton occupied the chair, and in the course of a few remarks said that he was pleased to see such a good attendance. He was sorry that one of their best members was leaving them but still bethought that it was to the benefit of the society that they should have the services of such a member at their command. In returning thanks Mr Gibson said that in taking up his new position he would not be leaving them alto- gether, he would still be working for them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN CO-OPERATIVES AND INSURANCE.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Monday, Viscount Wolmer asked the chancellor of the Exchequer whether the Insurance Commissioners had now received an application from the Earlestown Industrial Co-opera- tive Society, under sub-section (7) of section 47 of the National Insurance Act, to enable them and their employees, to whom they had guaranteed six weeks' full wages during sickness, to avail themselves of the reduced contributions provided for under the Act, and whether be could see his way to grant the application, since it was demanded by both parties. Mr. Mastermon : An application was received from this society under sub-section 7of section 47 of the National Insurance Act on the 17th inst. The Commis- sioners communicated with the society on the 18th inst to ascertain the views of the employers as required by the sub-section, and when the replies have been received they will at once consider the propriety of issuing a special order.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>MAYORAL TREAT FOR ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Sir Joseph Beecham, before ending his third year of office as Mayor of St. Helens on November 9, will give the townspeople yet another musical treat. On November 7, at the Town Hall, the Beecham Orchestra will give an up-to-date programme of music, and the Major will invite as many townspeople as can be com- pressed into the hall. HOUSEWIFE'S SAD DEATH.</p>
        <p>Mrs Jane Hughes, wife of an engine-driver, of 22, Carnegie-street, Sutton, with a family of seven children died in St. Helens Hospital on Saturday under said circumstances While sweeping the floor last Wednes- day week she ran a splinter from the brush handle into One of her fingers. She complained to her husband that it was very painful, and he advised her to poultice it. She did this till last Monday, when the hand Wass° bad that she went to Dr. Tough's surgery, and it was found that blood-poisoning had been set up. This became worse, and an operation was necessary. Mrs Hughes , went to hospital, and Drs Tough and Dow carried out the operation, but about ten minutes later the unfortu- nate woman collapsed and died. ST. HELENS GLEEMEN'S CONCERT.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens Town Hall on Monday evening the ' local gleemen and the Volkslied Quartet gave a most enjoyable and successful concert. A new fantasia on English folk-songs, by Dr. R. T. White, was sung for the first time by the Volkslied Quartet, and delighted the audience immensely. Miss Holding and Dr. S. B Siddall sang the" Four Cautionary Tales and a Moral,'. by Hilaire Belloc and Liza Lehmann, the humour and charm of which were acknowledged enthusiastically by the listeners. Miss Maud Harrison sang in beautiful style "Songs my mother taught me" and "She wandered down the mountain side." Dr Siddall sang the " Sword Song" from Elgar's " Caractus," and the gleemen gave " Deep Jordan's Banks," as well as old English part-songs. The O.M. Male Choir were highly successful in two choruses by Eiger and Bautock. Mr R Lloyd Moore sang in place of Mr J Hooker, his and Lohr's " Eyes that used by Osborne Edmundson, d to gaze" and "Time was litermovsebdeng " A Serenade.iMr Thomas Rimmer, of the Halle and Richter concerts, received a most flattering ovation at the close of his viola solos.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>LANCASHIRE STORIES.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE STORIES.</p>
        <p>- --- - So great has been the demand for "Lancashire Stories" that the publishers have had to go to press VI ith a second impression of Part 1., which is therefore still to be had. Part 11. has been issued thi3 Week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>PRIZE-WINNERS.</head>
        <p>THE PRIZE-WINNERS.</p>
        <p>Not exceeding three calendar months.-Girls: 1, Hilda Csrtwright, 112, Winwick-road ; 2. Marjorie Lilian Ditchfield, .50, Baxter.street ; 3, Dorothy Lytbgoe, 73, Thelwall-lane • he Ethel Wright, 36, Wellington-street. Bove : . Stanley Roach, 37, Leonard-street; 2, Thomas Gerrard McDonald, 39, Greenall-street ; 3, Ronaid Woods, 41, Green-street ; he, James Hazlehurst, 7, Clare-street. Over three, but not exceeding six months.-Girls : 1. Lydia Fowler, 30, Grafton-street ; 2, Bessie Richardson 468, Knutsford-road; 3, Hilda Rowley, 56, Hume-street; he, Lilian Boardman, 15, B.trry-street. Boys : 1, Stanley Clifford Cripps, 7, Longford-street; 2. Thomas Lesch, 21, Gorey-lane ; S. Harold Redvers Peacock, 9, Gari- baldi-street ; hc, Peter Peers, 86, Sharp-street. Over six and not exceeding Dine months.-Girls : 1, Florence Mary Gibbons, 30, Glazebrook-street • 2, Ethel Dennett, 121, Mersey-street; 3, Kathleen McHugh, 8, Pierpoint-street ; h c, Bertha Jeavons, 9, Elizabeth-street. Boys : 1, Patrick Lyons, 38, New- road; 2, Wilfrid Morris, 85, Hoyle-street ; 3, Edward Walser, 3, Brook-place ; h c, Eric Acton, 51, Bostock- street.</p>
        <p>Over nine and not exceeding twelve months.—Girls: 1, Bertha Scott, 36, Elaine-street ; 2, Inez Gorst, 300, Knutsford-road ; 3, Mary Corbett, 69, Plumpton-street; hc, Alice Aston, 21, Guardian-street. Boys: 1, Frank Wilson, 33, Orford-street ; 2, Harry Domville, 117, Manchester-road; 3, Ronald Philip Owen, Oaklands, Orford; h c, Edward Ellison, 19, Gladstone-street. Over twelve and not exceeding fifteen months.— Girls : 1, Evelyn Bacsler, 82, Evelyn-street ; 2, Sarah Ann Lavinia Roberts, 32, Dial-street ; 3, Marie Houghton, 14, Brookland-street hc, Marion Higginson 32, Wellington-street. Boys: 1, George tiostock, 4, Poachers-lane ; 2, Arthur Brindle, 53, Hume-street ; 3, Clifford Edmondson, 383, Wellfleld-street ; hc, George William Parry, 42, Baxter-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians was held at Dutton on Tuesday, Mr J Spruce presiding.</p>
        <p>COST OF LUNATICS.</p>
        <p>The authorities of the County Asylum, Chester, wrote stating that the rate of maintenance of pauper lunatics in this asylum for the quarter ending December 31st next fixed at 9s Oid per head per week. The Clerk pointeu out that the amount was an inc of 33d on the last quarter. The East Ham Union asked the Board to sup- port the following resolution :—That in the opinion of this Board the time has arrived when the Local Govern- ment Board either by prpmoting legislation or other- wise should deal with the whole question of vagrancy ; and further that any alteration in the existing law should provide for the supervision, control, and main- tenance of vagrants being handed over to the police authorities. Air Woodward asked if that meant that they were to stamp every vagrant on the road as a criminal. If it did he was absolutely opposed to it. It was admitted that there was a large number of undeserving vagrants, but on the other band there were a number of deserving cases and it would not be fair to have those stamped as criminals.</p>
        <p>was</p>
        <p>:mese</p>
        <p>The Chairman said they all knew how they were pestered with vagrants .every day in a very unpleasant manner. He thought they ought to support the resolu- tion in a safeguarding way in order that those who were deserving might have a certificate or something by which they would know that they were deserving. Mr Antwiss : It would not do some of them much harm to be looked after by the police. Mr Dean said the cure would perhaps be worse than the disease as far as the cost was concerned, and they ought to be careful that they were not going to incur an extra cost by handing the vagrants over to the police. It would be more costly in some cases than giving the vagrants a night's lodgings. Mr Morris said the matter was brought forward at the Poor Law conference and there was a concenans of opinion that the vagrants should not be handed over. It would increase the expenditure and the Guardians would have to pay it either directly or indirectly through the County rate. He moved that the letter be allowed to lie on the table. Mr Davies seconded and this course was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>MR RELIEF OF WIDOWS AND CHILD-REN.</head>
        <p>MR RELIEF OF WIDOWS AND CHILD-REN.</p>
        <p>Mr Hallows, having given notice at the previous meeting, was called upon to move a resolution in respect to the relief of widows and children. In doing so he said that the question was a big one and one about which much could be said. He asked for some consideration for the children sod widows of honest working men. They should give those children a proper chance of proving their citizenship. They were going to be the men and women of the future. Mr Davies : Move your resolution. Mr Bellows: I move that a small Committee be appointed to consi.ler what can be done in this case. They only received Is. 6d. per week now and he would probably have moved a resolution increasing that amount, but he found out that they bad 62 widows and 181 children in receipt of such money in their district.</p>
        <p>three-</p>
        <p>After stating the painful case of a widow with children and the miserable existence they had, Mr Hallows said that probably the Guardians would say they did not complain. They did not, because they dare not, because they were afraid of their house being taken from them. They ought to do something for those children in order to ensure the efficiency of the forthcoming generation. Mr Dean was quite in sympathy with the tone of Mr. Hallows proposition and he was delighted that such a resolution had been proposed, because, since he had been a guardian—and that was not long—he had been struck with the very meagre allowance given to those poor unfortunate widows. He did not under- stand how the family managed to get a bite each. If it was true,that there were three children the widow had 21 meals to provide each week for each child and all that had to be done out of Is. Gd. per head per week. It was a very serious matter. People said that children were a national asset. He, in seconding the resolution, would say: Let them support and bring up the children to be strong men and women so that the nation of the future should be as strong as possible. Mr Davies moved an amendment to the effect that the matter be left in the hands of the Relief Supervision Committee. Under the Local Government Board regulations, the Guardians were not simply tied down to the is 6d per week but they were allowed a free hand in the matter. Every case ought to be received and dealt with on its merits. (Hear, hear.) If it co 11 be proved that there was a case where the amount allowed was insufficient, he felt sure that the Guardians would deal with it in a manner which would be quite adequate for the needs. (Hear, hear). kir Linaker had every sympathy with Mr Hallows. The amount allowed was very meagre at the present day. On the other hand he also agreed with Mr Davies that the matter should be left with the Relief Super- vision Committee. He would also add that that com- mittee should report to the Board on all the cases. Mr Hallows said at the previous meeting that he thought of moving an extra sixpence per week. Mr Halloas : No I did not. I did not mention any amount.</p>
        <p>Mr tanaker : Supposing you did that, it would mean an addition on the rates of £231 per year. That, how- was nothing compared with the fact that such amount would be utilized by those widows to put the children well and in a clean, healthy condition. The time bad now arrived when it was incumbent on them to take the matter under consideration. There should, however, be descretion used. If the relieving officer recommended an increase they should give it. (Hear, hear.) He did not for one moment think they would give it to slatternly women who did not attend to their children in a proper manner. He seconded Mr. Davies' motion. The Chairman had every confidence in their officers and he did not think they would suffer anyone to want if the case came to their knowledge. They would report from their several districts and the cases would come before the Relief Supervision Committee and that com- mittee would submit its report to the Board for its con- firmation. No one would suffer any harm if the matter I was dealt with on the lines proposed by Mr Davies. ' Mr Hallows did not mind how it was done if the children were looked after. He did not care who did it so long as the matter was looked into. The Clerk pointed out that if the matter had been fully gone into he had a number of observations showing where widows were getting more than Is Gd per week. He would also have read Local Government Board regulations on the matter. It was unanimously decided to leave the matter to the relief supervision committee and also the question of appointing a lady visitor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>SHOW.</head>
        <p>SHOW.</p>
        <p>Councillor WILSON seconded the motion, which was heartily carried. in moving a vote of thanks to the Mayor, Dr. McKEE said the show had been a great success. They were deeply thankful to the Mayor for the interest he had taken in it. His Worship had the children's cause at heart. By the show they were educating people to bring up a healthier lot of children. The vote was carried with acclamation and in response the MAYOR railed for cheers, which were heartily given, for Councillor Wilson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>NO DEPUTATION'S.</head>
        <p>NO DEPUTATION'S.</p>
        <p>The Secretary of the inter-County Committee on vagrancy wrote stating that the Boards of Guardians in the area which originally propounded the" way ticket" system, invited the Board to appoint representatives to attend a meeting for the purpose of considering the question of dealing with vagrancy.—The Clerk pointed out that the place of meeting was not stated. He supposed they wanted to know how many delegates would attend. It seemec4to him to be an attampt to revive what was going on some years ago. The Chairman : We were represented at one time but I dont think we got very much out of it. Mr Morris moved that they be not represented. Mr Woodward said it was their duty to be represented. Mr Dean : You can always buy the reports and the happenings at such conferences. Mr Woodward: If every Board of Guardians thought that, there would be no conference. He had known of conferences that bad been productive of matter which had been very useful to them. Mr Morris had seen very little results from conferences. The motion was carried. • OVERSEERS' BONDS. Mr Morris moved "that all assistant overseers within the area of the Board !be required to obtain bonds from guarantee societies." He thought that the Guardians would agree with him that that was the beat course to adopt. Mr Linaker seconded the motion and said the position of assistant overseer ought to be demolished. The overseer could be wholly responsible. • The resolution was then carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>WIDOWS AND CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>WIDOWS AND CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>Mr T Cooke (Runcorn) reported that he had widows and 109 children chargeable in his district. Mr James Pearson (Frodsham) stated that in his area. there were 10 widows and 32 children. Mr Wll de Prez (Budworth) replied that in hit district there were 14 widows and 40 children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council was held at Dutton Tuesday, Mr C R Linaker presiding. PETROLEUM DUTIES. The Hai!sham Rural Council provided a copy of the following resolution. "That this council is of the opinion that the revenue derived from the duty on petroleum and motor licenses should be distributed to all the road maintenance authorities in the area of collection and the distribution should be proportioned according to the amount expended on materials by such authorities." They requested that the resolutAon should be laid before the Council asking them to take similar action by approaching the council of the County ia which their respective districts are situated. On the motion of Mr Woodward the matter was allowed to lie on the table.</p>
        <p>OVERHANGING TREES. The Clerk to the Frodsham Parish Council wrote calling the attention to the overgrowing of trees in Howey-lane, Prodsham Lordship so as to interfere with. driving and carting along the road. It was also com- plained that the gas lamp in Bradley-lane was obscured by trees. The Clerk said that a copy of the letter had been sent to Mr. Swinton (surveyor) who was proceeding in the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>MANLEY WATER SCHEME.</head>
        <p>MANLEY WATER SCHEME.</p>
        <p>_ Mr Parry, water engineer, wrote stating that the length of pipe line required between the Wallasey main and the Manley engine house was 450 yards and the cost of laying the necessary pipes ec.,would be approximately £2OO. The most satisfactory arrangement and the ono- most easily carried into effect would be for the supply to be taken by the Runcorn District Council from the existing Norley pipes. It was not possible, at the moment,to give even an approximate estimate of the cost of this. A Committee consisting of the Chairman, Messrs Harrison, Wright and Woodward were appointed to I go into the matter fully and report thereon.</p>
        <p>£BO FOR FIRST EDITION OF " WAVERLEY." At Sotheby's on Thursday, a fine copy of the first edition of " Waverley," in three volumes, printed in Edinburgh for A Constable and Co., 1814, was sold for LSO. For the first edition of another of Scott's novels, " Guy Mannering," 1815, £47 was paid.</p>
