      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>ISFIELD AND INSCRIPTION.</head>
        <p>ISFIELD AND INSCRIPTION.</p>
        <p>LORD ROBERTS' " DOCT' FORCE."</p>
        <p>PiiINE OF</p>
        <p>Sir,—So far as Parliamentary representa- tion is concerned, we in Warrington are committed not merely to a policy of Food Taxation, but actually to Conscription into the bargain! And we are sufficiently familiar with the propaganda by which the National Service League supports this policy in the Constituency. Those of your readers who have followed, or partially followed that propaganda; Those who heard, a couple of years since, Lord Haldane's conclusive answer; Those who agree with what Lord Haldane argued from a military standpoint, and who on other grounds as well are opposed to Conscription; And again those who have read Lord Roberts's speech delivered last Tuesday ;n ' Manchester will, I believe, be struck by force and truth of the measured criticism of' that speech in the article I enclose. To be quite accurate, it is a lengthy extract from the leading article in Wednesday's "West- minster Gazette,"—a journal which, as your readers will be aware, represents the most influential part of Liberal opinion in this quarter of the kingdom.—Yours etc., A. H. CROSFIELD. 39, Hyde Park Gate, S.W. October 24th, 1912.</p>
        <p>"With these grim events going for- ward under our eyes, discourses 3n the ethics of war, such a*Lord Roberts delivered at a meeting of the National Service League at Manchester last night, gain a special significance. We can only express our profound regret that Lord Roberts should have chosen this moment to advance the crudest doctrine of force as the arbitrament in human affairs and to have applied it without qualification to !Germany, with whom our relations are, we hope, gradually but steadily improving. He tells us that to "strike. when the hour has struck" is the "time-honoured policy" which Germany. has relentlessly pursued from the days of Bismarck and Moltke till now; and he calls it an "excellent policy " and contrasts it, before a Manchester audience, with the discredited maxims of Bright and Cobden. The mere moralist might ask "Strike for what?" before he endorses this verdict a " excellent," but striking for striking's sake appears to suffice for this militarist philosophy. When General yon Bernhardi published a book in Germany last year exalting the blind struggle of force as the proper policy for a civilised country, all that was best and soberest in Germany rose up to repudi- ate him, and we can do nothing less when Lord Roberts adopts this theme. The Germans pointed out that for forty years and more they had not drawn the sword, though many times an hour had struck which would have enabled them to destroy their neighbours or rivals, if that had been their intention. It is at least due to the Germans to say that they, more than any other nation, have taught us that war is not an object in itself, but the means of enforcing a policy which, if not rational or moral in itself, can never be made rational or moral by war. We all of us acknowledge that it is the duty of a nation to defend, itself effectively and it is as much an aim of those who are opposed to Lord Roberts as of those who support him to see that the defences of this country are effectively organised. • And if we do not endorse his compulsory military service idea, it is primarily because we think that the defensive strength of this country, con- sidering its insular position, can be better developed in other ways, But after reading his Manchester speech, we will frankly avow that we have also another objection, which is that the idea behind this militarist movement is not a defensive idea at all, but an aggressive and violent idea which, in our view, is essentially retrograde., It creates an appetite which can never be satisfied. We have by far the most powerful fleet in the world, but Lord Roberts sees our commerce conducted on sufferance unless in every sea at every moment we have the biggest fleet visibly present. He thinks of all the operations of commerce as carried on in the state of war. There could be no more disastrous idea for an Englishman to inculcate, since if tempor- arily in some seas we only trade "Jay the sufferance of other Powers," then in most seas at all times other Powers are only trading by our sufferance. On these lines it becomes the positive duty of every country to endeavour to build a stronger fleet than we possess. There is, indeed, no end to the absurdities of militarism pursued to these lengths."</p>
