      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>WOMAN.</head>
        <p>WOMAN.</p>
        <p>The thousands of books that have been written about woman have brought an understanding of her no nearer. So long as woman is precise, careful, and dainty in regard to the small things—the things that really do not much matter—and wan- tonly reckless and prodigal in those things which are of the very first importance to herself, she never will be miderstocd. How many women take any measures to combat those frightful pains which come to rack them from time to time? They are the outcome of ailments to which all women are subject, but which are unknown to the other sex. They are recognisable by pains, often of an excru- ciating nature, in the back, sides and head. These, and the frightful sense of depression and bearing down which they often cause, will leave the victim prostrated, time after time. They may wreck her constitution. They have' been • known to effect the mental powers. In spite of all this many women make no effort to free themselves from, them. After reading the following letter from a Derbyshire lady, no woman can plead ignorance of how to accomplilh this. Elie writes : "Having suffered for many years from certain female ailments and getting no relief from anything, I at last took my husband's advice and purchased a bottle of Lydia E. Pinkham's Vegetable Compound. Afitzir two doses I was surprised at the relief I had got, and now, after taking a few bottles, I feel greatly benefited, and can I say that the pain I used to experience has entirely left me. I am perfectly convinced that I owe my 1 relief to-your wonderful Vegetable Compound." Let every _woman who is., tc-day---:•or will be to-morrow or next week—troubled as this lady Was, get for herself thw same speedy Remedy._ Any phemist will T liiibityltd-gt 2s. 9d. it Isottle; °FA(4s.tekkfor.a pc* largeMpttl nlystadY Manager, c,r(i he Lydia El Pinkñi M Wtn,pstelid bifead,lAindon, N.W., will supply it-ark&amp; Uiheife prides. She will also send a free Private Book (for women only) to those who will apply.</p>
