        <p>The promise of an all-round fight, chiefly on party political lines, is to be fulfilled. The Dominations teceived on Thursday disclose the fact that there will be a contest in each of the nine wards, and, although in some cases the candidates have not come forward until almost the eleventh hour, the elections are being entered into with considerable spirit and some very close results are predicted. Friday next is the poll- ing day and progressives are urged to take this epportunity of striking a blow at Tore domination and helping forward the cause of good government. , The past week has witnessed important develop- iments. As announced in our last issue Mr William saw consented ta stand as the Liberal candidate in the Town Hall ' Ward in opposition to Mr W H J Jenkins, the retiring member. Mr Peter Delaney came forward as an independent, at the request of the local life insurance workers and, in order to avoid a three-coreered fight, Mr Isaac has now stood aside. Mr Delaney seeks the suffrages of the electors irrespective of all party polities and points to his 30 years experience of insurance in all its branches as an evidenceof his thorough commercial training. ,In Whitecross Ward, the Liberals have a strong candidate in Mr Peter Peacock, a keen businessman, a thoroughly democratic politician and a, leedin.g friendly' society' worker. ' The ' withdrawal Of Mr W H'Belhan, the retiring representative, loft the Con- servatives in the lurch and they have apparently experienced great difficulty in inducing anyone to attempt the hopeless teak of challenging Mr Peacock's , return. At the last moment, however, Mr Samuel Oswald Allen, foreman joiner, 8, Tanners-lane, has stepped into the breach. In Fairfield Ward the Liberal Committee have been fortunate in including Mr James Jarman, the esteemed secretary of the Warrington Co-opera- tive Society, to do battle in the progressive Mr. Edmund Osborne's Candidature. interest. His business experience, his sound prog