        <p>Mn. JANES SHAW.. (5-UeStiOni4 1, t! Hnd 3. Yes. CouNciLLoic STEEL.—Questicn 1. It would gteatly depend upon who the person was. I believe there at e honest and good men connected with the liquor trade just as there are in other callings. Question 2. Certain'y not. A man is elected alderman as a reward for the services be has rendered the town, not because he does or does not belong to a particular trade Question 3. Does not apply to me, as I should decline to serve on the Watch C ommittee for personal reaaone.</p>
        <p>( t</p>
        <p>ile was net aSicing the Council to do something that ta impossible, because the gas works was a profitable. °cam and last year £13,797 us 10d was devoted from That snoriie to the relief of rates. ft might be very tithe to reduCp the rates to the tune of about Is in the gfrOut the gas undertaking, but •why should the people used Was' help to relieve those who used electricity? The users erajot-meters,who paid cash in advance,should his treated better than they were at the present time. Ile contended that the consumers of gas were being made to pay through the nose for an article .which the Corporation undertook to sell at as reasonable a price as possible. He maicitained that they were entitled to a ' little consideration. (Applause.) Finally Mr.Osborjae expressed his intention, to support, a living wage for all Corporation employees and. to do his utmost to secure &amp;Ater recreative facilitieslar the vrorkingclass districts. CA pplause ) I "Qtre'stidos were then invited; and probably the roost 'itSpOrtant was one relating to trade union conditions in regard to the Police Station. The first question was `; Are you in.favonr of the low paid employees of the Corporation having an eight hours day bill ? " Mr Osborne replied that he believed eight hours per day was long enough for any man to work. He was not going to equivocate in the slightest and he would vote in favour of eight hours per day. "Are you aware" began another questioner, "that in this ward some of the voters were asked to vote for your opponent because he is a Churchman. " "That is contemptible," Mr Osborne replied. "If you can tell me that a Churchman will make a better town councillor than a Nonconformist then I will with- draw that statement. " An old tramcar driver raised the question of the hours of exposure a driver had to submit to, also to the inadequate and awkward meal arrangement. • Mr Osborne said that when they were making £2,455 18s 2d profit per year on the tramways, they ought to be able to afford to pay a relief man whilst a driver had his meals. (Hear, hear.) Eight hours per day for seven days a week for a car driver was a crying shame. No man ought to work longer than six days per week and he advocated eight hours per day. Another question was " Are you in favour of the municipalities of this district joining together and taking up a municipal coal supply ? " Mr Osborne said be believed in municipalisation but with the protection that he would not injure a private dealer in any case. He would deal with the muni- cipalisation of the milk supply first but he would see that the retailers were recompensed. , A QUESTIONER referred to a system which he said had only been introduced at the Police Station since the preheat Chief Constable came into office. There was one constable who had been engaged at the police station for a number of years and,he,had never yet been on duty as a constable. Ile was -working there as a tailor, doing repairs to the uniforths, eco. The Trades Council had had a complaint from the Tailors' Union that that kind of prooeduce was unfair and the tailors were paying rates for the upkeep of the police station and it, was not good enough for the police to be knob- sticking " at their trade. There was also a joiner's shop at the Police Station and there were policemen working as joiners. The joiners had complained,as also had the plunibers who said that there were constables doing plumbing work. The Trades Council were accorded a hearing by the Watch Committee but they got no aatisfaction. He (the questioner) did not know whether anything had been done but he wanted to ask Mr. Osborne if he would be prepared to take the matter up and see that policemen engaged to do policemen's work did that and no. oth,r.</p>
        <p>_ Mr OSBORNE said that if a man was enraged to do policeman's work he ought to do that and tailors work should be left to tailors. If, however,a man was engaged in the police buildings to be a tailor, although called a policeman, they bad a difficult problem. He would look at those things from a Trade Union point of view and if those policemen were knob-sticking he would take the first opportunity of calling attention to it.(Applause. Mr H 0 Roberts proposed a resolution to the effect that Mr Osborne was a fit and proper person to repre- sent us in the Town Council." He was quite sure Mr Osborne's views would meet with their approval. The candidate believed in equal opportunities for everyone. The resolution was seconded by Mr Alfred Tilling and carried unaimously. Mr OSBORNE returned thanks.</p>
