      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>MAGISTRATES AT LAW.</head>
        <p>MAGISTRATES AT LAW.</p>
        <p>COUNTY COURT SEQUEL TO MOTOR</p>
        <p>, COLLISION.</p>
        <p>The sequel to a motor car collision near the Dog and Dart Inn. Knutsford-road, Grappenhall. was heard at the Warrington County Court, on Thursday. before his Honour Judge Stanger, KG., when Mr. Abraham Fielding. J.P.. Trentham, Stoke-on-Trent, brought an action against Mr. W. Peter Ryland.% ii'., Dingle Bank, 'Lymm, to recover 1:44 13s. 6d. for damage alleged to have been done to his motor car through the negligent driving of his chauffeur. There waS a counter-claim by Mr. Rylands for £27 on similar grounds. Mr. Frank Rowland, of Man- chester, appeared for the plaintiff, and Mr. Gordon Hewart. K.C., and Mr. Duckworth Stuart (instructed by Messrs. Henry Greenall &amp; Co.) represented Mr. Rylands.</p>
        <p>, . The accident occurred at the four cross roads at Grappenhall on March 29th. According to Mr. Row. land's opening statement, Mr. Fielding was driving his own car from Knutsford In the direction of Latchford and a landaulette—belonging to the defendant and dtiven by his chauffeur, was travelling afonk, the Chester-road, In the direction of Lymni. It wis alleged that Mr. Ltylands' car was going at a tremendous rate. The gabriel horn of the plaintiff's car was continuously blown for a con- siderable distance, but no warning sound was ,given by the defendant's chauffeur. who was alone in the car. Evidently the brakes were suddenly jammed on, as the defendant's car began to 'swerve. It went first to the right, then appeared to do a "dry skid." and finally came crashing round to the Left. Seeing that the car was coming into him, the plaintiff drew to the near side, as far as' he could. In front of the Dog and Dart, the defendant's car crashed into the rear portion of the plaintiff's car. striking the back wheel and pushing the car violently from the road, over the cobble stones, and into the back of a kirry which was standing out- side the Inn.</p>
        <p>A very strange thing occurred, continued, Mr. Row. land. Mr. Rylands had the advantage of being a County justice of the peace. The plaintiff wrote him on March 30th, and was subsequently. prosecuted at the Police Court for driving his car to the danger of the public. He would be able to prove that Mr. Rylands sat on the bench during the hearing of the case, although he said he was not adjudicating on it. His Honour: Did he give evidence? Mr. Rowland: No, he did not. Having pointed out that the prosecution wAs dismissed Mr. Rowland said .that if the defendant's chauffeur had kept on his original course, even going as fast as he did, the accident could not have happened. The negligence alleged was the driver's disregard for the safety of other vehicular traftic, his omission to exercise the necessary skill and care to avoid an accident., his failure to give warning of his approaching the cross roads or to keep a sufficient idol:- out. There was a danger sign on the Knutsford-road and, after passing it, the plaintiff slackened speed: If he had not done so. he would have passed the Cross roads before the defendant's car could have hit him. Unfortunately for him, he was driving ia a very careful manner.</p>
        <p>Plaintiff gave evidence bearing out this statement. He said he was motoring to the Grand National at Aintree. He was travelling at the rate of 12 to 15 miles an hour on nearing the scene of the accident. 'He had slackened speed at the danger signal, for he, knew the place well. fie had not time to accelerate before the defendant's car crashed into him. Referring to the police prosecution, Mr. Rowland asked: Was the defendant on the Bench?</p>
        <p>Plaintiff: Yes. Mr. Rowland: He did not take any part in the Pro. ccedings?—Yes, he did. He passed notes to the superin- tendent who-was conducting the prosecution. Mr.. Rylands was asked to desist front it. Mr. Gordon Hewart (cross-examining): When you came to the danger signal, you did not reduce your speed ?-- I cannot rezollect. I might have done so, or I might not. I put it' to, you that you were travelling at a speed far greater than 12 or 13 miles an hour as you came to the cross-roads ?—I must be a very bad judge of speed then. Plaintiff added that, judging by the dust it was making, defendant's car , was travelling at from 30 to 40 miles an hoer. Why didn't you stop at the corner ?-1 should have been 'worse off than I was. • , Mr. Rylands is a County justice ?—Yes. understand you are also ?—Yes. Do you really suggest to his Honour that Mr. Rylands instigated the prosecution of yourself ?—He was with the superintendent of police on the scene of the accident before the summons was issued. You suggest that ?—Well, l•was informed so. Do you suggest that Mr. Rylands instigated the prose- cution or had anything to do with it ?—l. would not suggest anything at all of the sort. It looked very awkward to me, and very uncomfortable, unpleasant, and un-English—very unsportsmanlike too. Mr. Hewart: Much obliged. You say he was present in Court during the hearing ?—He was. Do you know that he was invited by :his brother justices to sit where he did?—No. Do you know that he took no part in the adjudi- cation ?—!'saw him P•notes" myself to the superinten- dent of the police who was conducting the prosecution. I suggest there is not a word of truth in that ?-1 can believe my own eyes.</p>
        <p>_ You say some person requested him not to pass notes. That person was your solicitor ?--Yes. Mr. Rowland: On that occasion it was to his brothers on the bench that I objected to hirp passing notes, and I do now.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hevvart: Do you really stiggest that Mr. Rylands first of II was instrumental in procuring your prosecu- tion and then made a wrong use of his position as a justice either to Instigate his brother magistrates or to instruct the superintendent while the case was proceeding? —I have got my own mind on it. I am nbt going to say auything about it. I will leave you to form your own opinion as to what you would have done, under my own conditions. I wrote to the superintendent and told him that if he summoned one, he ought to summon the two. George Thomas Smith, Dolphin Inn, Colbridge, Staffs., bore out the plaintiff'sistAt4,4oir,4o4.t. - Atihatrltkitlel trite d t ff"CsatAil,c, top d;r4eal&gt;, ptk, hould ~avelbfttiq a )x -told meat, 'a the,'llot, of us." Eviderfee was also given by Ettwarsi .lAker tke</p>
        <p>,d,F,lmseciop A}ad</p>
