      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-10-26_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>Furnish for Cash or or. oil r Convenient Paument Sustem</head>
        <p>Furnish for Cash or or. oil r Convenient Paument Sustem</p>
        <p>GLOBE</p>
        <p>Furnishing Co. I</p>
        <p>U. R. GRANT, Prop-rico:tr.) PENIIROKE PLAOE, Liverpool</p>
        <p>IUSINESS HOURS 9 to 8 DAILY.</p>
        <p>employ of Messrs. J. Fairclough &amp; Sons, Warrington, who said plaintiff sounded his horn, and William Perry Hatchfield, manager of the repairs department of Messrs. Crossley, Opensbaw, who described the damage to the plaintiff's car. I)4' 06</p>
        <p>For the defence, Christopher Ciranger, chauffeur to the defendant, was called. He stated that he was well ac- quaintel with the corner as a dangerous spot, and on the date in question he sounded his horn six yards before reaching it. , He was travelling very gently—at about eight miles an hour. fie first caught sight of the Plain- tiff's car when he was nearly entering the Warringten road. It was going at a very dangerous speed. Witnes9 turned slightly to the left to get on his own side. He saw the plaintiff's car was coming in front of him and he stopped. The back wheel of the plaintiff's car caught the dumb irons of the defendant's car, twisting thenc and damaging the radiator. That was the only damage to the defendant's car. Witness was confident that when the impact took place the defendant's car had stopped. The force of the collision was not sufficient to prevent' witness retaining his seat. After striking the defendant's car, plaintiff's car dashed another four yards and collided with the lurry. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Rol%land: Can you produce a single human being. with the exception of yourself, who heard your horn sounded? Witness: No.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Peter Rylands next gave evidence. He said the corner in question was the most dangerous he had encountered in all his motoring experience. Witness was not riding in the car when the accident occurred, but he was afterwards telephoned for and on examining the car fouud that the damage was as described by his chauffeur. Subsequently Superintendent Ennion wrote Granger asking him to meet him at the Dog and Dart' corner on the Sunday after the accident. Witness was naturally interested to know what the superintendent wanted to see Granger about, so he accompanied hint; to hear what he had to say on the spot. Five accidents, of the same character, had happened at the, corner since the smash in question.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hewart: I want to ask you about the suggestion which seemed to be made and seemed not to be made• Had you anything whatever to do with the prosecutioa ;of Mr. Fielding? Witness: Absolutely nothing. I would not have dreamed of wishing any motorist to be summoned. I was very much surprised wheh I heard that Superintendent ErtlliOlT was summoning Mr. Fielding, because I did not knoW what his evidence was, or anything about it. • It is said that you were present in the Court while the' summons was being heard and that you passed notes to somebody.—Exactly what happened was this: When I, heard that this case was going on ,it was of considerable. ' interest'to me to know whether Granger or Fielding to blame—what the evidence was as to the accident, of which I personally knew absolutely nothing. I went to the Daresbury Court to hear the case, and my 'hrother magistrates, somewhat naturally perhaps, asked me to. sit at the end of the bench. Did you take, any part in the hearing?—Absolutely part at all: And the result was the case was dismissed 7—Yes; What Mr. Rowland was referring to is this: I naturallY was very interested, knowing nothing about the case" at all. I heard some witness give evidence and I rentem'. ber putting a note to a brother magistrate—" It would- be very interesting to know Mr. Fielding's explanation of the small damage done to my car." It had rn't very much bearing upon the case, but it would have been an interesting bit of information for me. If had wanted to be malicious and' assist the police. certainly should not have taken the position I hold 01' the bench. Edmund Grant, 'manager to Messrs. Bellian 8: Davies, gave evidence as to the damage to the defendant's car:. Mr., Gordon fiewart, addressing his Honour, pointen out that the plaintiff had plenty of space in which t•cr. manceuvre his car, and there were various directions' In which he could have moved or brought himself to a standstill, with perfect safety. Plaintiff's story as to the speed at which defendant's car was travelling was. Incredible. If it were true, neither the plaintiff nor tb: defendant' S chauffeur would have survived to tell the tale 1 . His Honour said he had come to the conclusion that, the pace at which the plaintiff . approached the ccirrier waci too great. Having regard also to the fact that if he,ha,, looked across the country he might have seen the lanua,"; lette -sooner than he did, the result of any neffhgen;-. there might have been on the part of the defendant chauffeur might have, been avoided by the exercise too emphatically reasonable care by the plaintiff. It could not be W.". emphatically insisted upon that, in approaching dangerou;‘ corners, whether in the country or near a town, drivers r motor cars ought to use the greatest possible cannaa., He did not think that was exercised in the Present case. On On the other hand he was satistied that the P tiff sounded his horn and that the defendant's chauffeen.'_ did not keep a proper look out. ln view of the evid !,11;: of the plaintiff and Mr. Smith he could not accept To,, statement of the defendant's chauffeur—no doubt nurri''`e, honestly made—that he pulled up dead. There was want of care on his part which rendered the defendan‘ counter-claim untenable. lands) Judgment was given for the defendant (Mr. Rtheir out the claim and for the plaintiff (Mr. Fielding) ° 11 counter-claim.</p>
        <p>HOUSEWIFE'S SAD DEATH. Mrs Jane Hughes, wife of an engine-driver, of 22' Carnegie-street, Sutton, with a family of seven childrirl died in St. Helens Hospital on Saturday under circumstances While sweeping the floor last Wedno day week she ran a splinter from the brush handle ene of her fingers. She complained to her husband Cr! It was very painful, and he advised her to poultice it,: She did this till last Monday, when the bend Wass° b5:l that she went to Dr. Tough's surgery, and it was foils!, that blood-poisoning had been set up. This bent:, worse, and an operation was necessary. Mrs lltig et went to hospital, and Dr a Tough and Dow carried o the operation, but about ten minutes later the tud"til ' nate woman collapsed and</p>
