        <p>CO UNTY BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON. MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS, NOVEMBHR sr. 1912. TO THE BURGESSES OF FAIRFIELD WARD. LADIES AD GENTLEMEN,-,- I have been asked to allow myself to be nominated for the vacancy on the Town Council caused by the retirement of 4.1,1 r Jackson Holmes and have consented to do so. In asking for your vote and influence, I wish to do so entirely on Non-Party lines as I do not consider these should enter into the question of our Municipal Elec- tions but that the sole question should be whether the candidate is suitable in ability and personal character for the position of representative. I have no personal ends to serve in seeking the posi- tion but should consider it an honour to be on your Town Council and would do my utmost to further the interests of the town generally, if elected. I think I may claim general business ability and a knowledge of financial matters which should be of value to the town and would endeavour 'to see that economy and efficiency were exercised in any depart- ments that came under my notice and'that the rate- paws had the fullest possible value always for the expenditure incurred. At any time I should be pleased 'to listen to the opinions of the electors whom I hope to represent and to further their wishes on the Council as fat op 1 cart possibly do so. At the moment, and in the future alb, the ñnôi n tives in the administration of Insuradoe ACt: a' good deal through its rermipts_l yedeonsidsble Interest in this PO 649141,4 bw4oaivilmebytaeukctelevseetri: to see that the interests of the workers 1 as insured persons were adequately represented. I remain, Ladies and Gentlemen, your sincerely, ; 1 78, Manchester Road, . JAMES JARMAN, Warrington, October 22nc1,1912. •</p>
