        <p>MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS, NOVEMBER 182., 1912. rflo- THE ELECTORS OF T. JOHN'S WARD. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN, In response to the solicitations of a very large number of residents in our Ward, I ' have for the third time consented to plans my services at your disposal.' I feel this Wsrd with which I have been intimately connectfid for over 90 years should above all others be represented by a member of the Progressive Party, particnitirly as the housing question, which is one of the foremost problems, is before the various Municipal Authorities. For over 30 year. I have been officially connected With Friendly Societfr work, and as the Town ' Council will have a lot to do with the benefits under the Insurance Act, I feel I am well qualified to study the workers' needs. St. John's Ward having no reasonable facilities in the tram route, should participate in the Motor 'bus service, which is being considered in War- rington. I should immediately, press forward the operation of halfpenny tram fares. In my opinion lower paid Corporation employees should be considered before the higher paid officials. I trust that I may receive a renewal a your confidence on Friday, November Ist. Your obedient servant, 83, Lythgoes Lane. JAMES SHAW.</p>
